window.CP_CONTENT = {"publication":{"schema_version":1,"edition":"1.0","repository":"Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract","commit":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","catalog":"publication-catalog.json"},"records":[{"canonical":true,"id":"semantic-objects-and-context","kind":"paper","type":"paper","path":"papers/semantic-objects-and-context/PAPER.md","route":"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/","title":"Semantic Objects, Context, and Conditions of Applicability","titleRu":"Semantic Objects, Context, and Conditions of Applicability","summary":"A formal account of source interpretations, context, mandatory dependencies and conditions of applicability.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>A knowledge system must distinguish what a source contains, how that content is interpreted, and whether an interpretation supports a particular use. This paper specifies a model that keeps those questions separate. Its basic unit is a versioned semantic object with an attributable interpretation, explicit context, and inspectable grounds. Applicability is a decision relative to declared requirements, rather than a permanent property of a passage. We establish five elementary conditional results: source fidelity does not establish truth; a finite dependency graph has a least required closure; mandatory strengthening narrows certified applicability; additional possible readings narrow common consequences; and complete backward slicing preserves selected deterministic outputs. Counterexamples show why omitted dependencies, ambiguous scope, and unexamined modal distinctions defeat stronger conclusions. These are properties of the stated model, not a proof that an interpreter extracts all relevant meaning. The contribution is an integrated, falsifiable account of the obligations between extraction and use. Reproducible transcription and dependency studies are published separately from the scientific argument.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> semantic object; provenance; context; applicability; interpretation; dependency closure.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-research-question-and-contribution\">1. Research question and contribution</h2>\n<p>An accurate quotation can be false. A true statement can be irrelevant to a task. Two identical sentences can concern different objects, dates, or obligations. A system that stores only text and a confidence score cannot express these differences without assigning several incompatible meanings to the same field.</p>\n<p>Our question is: <strong>what must remain explicit when source material becomes knowledge offered for a particular task?</strong> We propose a separation between source representation, interpretation, grounding, applicability, and delivery. The contribution is the organization of these obligations and the identification of their failure conditions. The mathematical results below are elementary consequences of the definitions; we do not claim a new general theory of meaning.</p>\n<p>The model complements established work. W3C PROV-DM describes provenance through entities, activities, and agents [1]. SKOS provides concepts, labels, and relations for knowledge organization [2]. Neither a provenance link nor a concept label is, by itself, evidence that a proposition is true or applicable. We use those distinctions while adding a task-relative admission question: what has to hold before this interpretation may support this use?</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-objects-interpretations-and-grounds\">2. Objects, interpretations, and grounds</h2>\n<p>A <strong>source representation</strong> is a particular version of material: text, image, table, audio, or a combination. A locator identifies a region within that representation. A locator can make inspection repeatable; it does not certify the correctness of the region’s interpretation.</p>\n<p>A <strong>semantic object</strong> is a record</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\no=(i,v,m,A,C,G),\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i</annotation></semantics></math> is identity, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math> is revision, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the represented content, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math> is attribution, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math> is context, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of grounds and dependency relations. Content may represent a proposition, question, instruction, constraint, example, or another declared role. A role is not an epistemic verdict: recording an instruction does not establish its legitimacy, and recording an example does not establish a universal rule.</p>\n<p>An <strong>interpretation</strong> maps a source region to such a record under declared reading assumptions. Interpretation can be uncertain or plural. If a diagram permits two readings, the record can retain both, with the observations needed to resolve them. Choosing one silently changes the knowledge claim.</p>\n<p><strong>Grounding</strong> identifies why a particular part of the interpretation is supported. A source may support that an author asserted <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math>, while an independent measurement supports <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math> itself. These are different grounds. We therefore distinguish</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>Says</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">and</mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mi>P</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\operatorname{Says}(a,P) \\quad\\text{and}\\quad P.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>There is no inference from the first to the second without additional premises about the source and the relevant domain. Co-occurrence is likewise not an implication: placing two statements in one paragraph does not establish a causal or logical relation between them.</p>\n<p>Extraction must preserve logical structure when it matters. For a domain with two objects, one satisfying <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math> and one not, “not every object satisfies <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math>” is true while “every object fails <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math>” is false. Similarly, absence of an obligation to act is different from an obligation not to act. A semantic representation that merges these distinctions changes the claim even if it preserves most words.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-context-and-applicability\">3. Context and applicability</h2>\n<p>We distinguish six context roles: source context, including terminology, referents, and local legends; extraction context, including the inspected representation and unresolved details; request context, including intent, completeness requirements, and budget; recipient context, including usable forms and modalities; situation context, including the target object, time, and reference frame; and intended-action context, specifying what is to be explained, compared, computed, or executed. These are functional roles, not a requirement to duplicate a value in six places. A single linked value can serve several roles, while source conditions and target conditions remain distinguishable.</p>\n<p>Let a use request be <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math>, a relevant state be <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math>, and the finite mandatory conditions be <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(q)</annotation></semantics></math>. Each condition has an assessment</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mi>𝖺</mi><mi>𝗌</mi><mi>𝗌</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝖿</mi><mi>𝖺</mi><mi>𝗂</mi><mi>𝗅</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝗎</mi><mi>𝗇</mi><mi>𝗄</mi><mi>𝗇</mi><mi>𝗈</mi><mi>𝗐</mi><mi>𝗇</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\ne_r(o,q,s)\\in\\{\\mathsf{pass},\\mathsf{fail},\\mathsf{unknown}\\}.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Certified applicability is defined by</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>Applicable</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇔</mo><mo>∀</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mi>𝖺</mi><mi>𝗌</mi><mi>𝗌</mi></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\operatorname{Applicable}(o,q,s)\n\\iff \\forall r\\in R(q),\\ e_r(o,q,s)=\\mathsf{pass}.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>This definition deliberately does not treat unknown as pass. It also does not equate failure to certify with proof that the content is false. An unanswered question about a relevant date may block a current recommendation while leaving the underlying statement unresolved.</p>\n<p>Conditions can be inherited only through a declared rule. A section-level exception may apply to its paragraphs, but arbitrary proximity does not establish inheritance. Conflicting conditions require an explicit conflict decision or a restricted answer. A system must not select whichever condition produces a convenient result.</p>\n<p>The request determines the necessary depth. A short identification question may require fewer dependencies than an instruction to execute a consequential action. Increasing depth can create new obligations; it is not merely a demand for a longer response. Applicability must also be checked for the actual recipient and delivered representation. A condition available to the system but absent from the delivered instructions may not protect the recipient.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-conditional-results\">4. Conditional results</h2>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-1-fidelity-cannot-discriminate-indistinguishable-worlds\">Proposition 1. Fidelity cannot discriminate indistinguishable worlds</h3>\n<p>Suppose two possible worlds produce the same observed source representation, while <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math> is true in one and false in the other. Any deterministic procedure using only that representation produces the same decision in both worlds. It therefore cannot correctly decide the truth of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math> in both.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> The procedure receives identical inputs, so its outputs coincide. The correct truth verdicts differ. Hence at least one verdict is incorrect if the procedure returns a definite truth value. A randomized procedure has the same output distribution in both worlds and likewise receives no discriminating evidence. ∎</p>\n<p>This result does not make sources useless. It identifies the extra premise needed to move from faithful attribution to a truth claim: a reliable domain model, independent observation, or another justified discriminating ground.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-2-least-required-closure-exists-on-a-finite-graph\">Proposition 2. Least required closure exists on a finite graph</h3>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite set of pinned object revisions, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math> selected roots, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_q\\subseteq V\\times V</annotation></semantics></math> the active mandatory dependencies for a fixed request. Define</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>:</mo><mo>∃</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nC_0=R,\\qquad\nC_{n+1}=C_n\\cup\\{v:\\exists u\\in C_n,(u,v)\\in D_q\\}.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Then the sequence reaches a fixed point <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^*</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the least <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_q</annotation></semantics></math>-closed set containing <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> The sequence is increasing and contained in finite <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math>, so only finitely many strict additions are possible. Its fixed point is closed by construction. For any closed set <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R</annotation></semantics></math>, induction gives <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_n\\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^*\\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>\n<p>Cycles do not invalidate this set-theoretic result. Missing edges do invalidate an interpretation of the result as semantic completeness: a perfectly computed closure can still omit an unrecorded exception.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-3-mandatory-strengthening-narrows-certified-applicability\">Proposition 3. Mandatory strengthening narrows certified applicability</h3>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\subseteq R&#39;</annotation></semantics></math>, and keep the assessments of shared conditions unchanged. Every object certified under <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> is certified under <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Passing every condition in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> entails passing every condition in its subset <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>\n<p>The fixed-semantics condition matters. If adding a requirement also changes the meaning of an earlier one, the comparison is no longer a simple strengthening.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-4-additional-readings-narrow-common-consequences\">Proposition 4. Additional readings narrow common consequences</h3>\n<p>For a nonempty set of possible readings <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo>Conseq</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K(I)=\\bigcap_{i\\in I}\\operatorname{Conseq}(i)</annotation></semantics></math>, with a fixed consequence relation. If <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I\\subseteq I&#39;</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K(I&#39;)\\subseteq K(I)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Membership in the intersection over <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> requires membership in every set indexed by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as any additional sets. ∎</p>\n<p>This supports conservative reporting under ambiguity. It does not establish that the recorded readings include the correct one, or that the consequence relation is sound for the domain.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-5-a-complete-backward-slice-preserves-selected-deterministic-outputs\">Proposition 5. A complete backward slice preserves selected deterministic outputs</h3>\n<p>Let a finite acyclic computation graph have fixed deterministic node functions and fixed input values. Retain the selected output nodes and every node on which they depend. Evaluating that retained graph gives the same selected outputs as evaluating the full graph.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Order the retained graph topologically. Retained input nodes have unchanged values. Every later retained node has all its predecessors retained and, by induction, receives their unchanged values. Its fixed function therefore returns the same value. This includes the selected outputs. ∎</p>\n<p>The result concerns complete computational dependencies. Hidden inputs, changing functions, omitted edges, or an inadequate model of the original task defeat its premises. It is not a proof that an arbitrary textual summary preserves meaning.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-granularity-identity-and-delivery\">5. Granularity, identity, and delivery</h2>\n<p>There is no task-independent ideal size for a semantic object. A large unit may hide an exception; a tiny unit may detach a predicate from its scope. A useful decomposition exposes independent revision and inspection while retaining explicit mandatory connections. Granularity is therefore evaluated against reconstruction and use obligations, not by a preferred number of words.</p>\n<p>Identity, equivalent content, and interchangeable use must also remain separate. Two records can contain equivalent claims but have different provenance. Two revisions of one record can differ on a critical condition. Two objects interchangeable for one question can be distinguishable for another. Deduplication should consolidate a verified content class without discarding the provenance that supports or qualifies its members.</p>\n<p>Delivery creates a further obligation. A preserved image identifier does not guarantee that a recipient receives a readable image; alternative text does not necessarily preserve a visual relation needed by the task. The delivered package must include or expose the relevant material at a usable resolution and with the necessary legend or interpretation. These are inspectable engineering conditions, not consequences of naming a multimodal field.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-falsification-and-scope-of-evidence\">6. Falsification and scope of evidence</h2>\n<p>The propositions establish properties of declared representations. The harder empirical question is whether an extraction process supplies the right representations and dependencies. That question requires independently prepared cases and adjudication rules. A useful evaluation distinguishes transcription error, attribution error, logical-scope error, omitted context, and unsupported inference. A single aggregate accuracy score hides these different failure mechanisms.</p>\n<p>Counterexamples should include a changed referent with unchanged wording, an exception outside the extracted region, an ambiguous visual mark, and a correct quotation of a false assertion. An independent reference annotation should specify what counts as a permissible alternative reading. Agreement between two outputs is insufficient when both inherit the same omitted condition.</p>\n<p>This article establishes the stated conditional properties and a falsifiable specification of extraction obligations. It reports no measured superiority of a particular model, annotation process, or user interface. Executable examples can test compliance on finite inputs; they cannot prove complete interpretation of unrestricted language or images.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work\">7. Connections to the published engineering work</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/\">source transcription and uncertainty study</a> addresses source regions, alternative readings, and inspection. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">dependency closure and package delivery study</a> separates graph closure from delivery checks. These studies provide the appropriate location for domain cases and executable controls.</p>\n<p>Definitions shared across the project are indexed in the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>. The next scientific steps are <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">constrained transformations</a> and <a href=\"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/\">retrieval and context composition</a>, which use the applicability and dependency obligations defined here.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>W3C. <em>PROV-DM: The PROV Data Model</em>. W3C Recommendation, 2013. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/\" class=\"uri\">https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/</a></li>\n<li>W3C. <em>SKOS Simple Knowledge Organization System Reference</em>. W3C Recommendation, 2009. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/skos-reference/\" class=\"uri\">https://www.w3.org/TR/skos-reference/</a></li>\n</ol>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"1-research-question-and-contribution","title":"1. Research question and contribution"},{"level":2,"id":"2-objects-interpretations-and-grounds","title":"2. Objects, interpretations, and grounds"},{"level":2,"id":"3-context-and-applicability","title":"3. Context and applicability"},{"level":2,"id":"4-conditional-results","title":"4. Conditional results"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-1-fidelity-cannot-discriminate-indistinguishable-worlds","title":"Proposition 1. Fidelity cannot discriminate indistinguishable worlds"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-2-least-required-closure-exists-on-a-finite-graph","title":"Proposition 2. Least required closure exists on a finite graph"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-3-mandatory-strengthening-narrows-certified-applicability","title":"Proposition 3. Mandatory strengthening narrows certified applicability"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-4-additional-readings-narrow-common-consequences","title":"Proposition 4. Additional readings narrow common consequences"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-5-a-complete-backward-slice-preserves-selected-deterministic-outputs","title":"Proposition 5. A complete backward slice preserves selected deterministic outputs"},{"level":2,"id":"5-granularity-identity-and-delivery","title":"5. Granularity, identity, and delivery"},{"level":2,"id":"6-falsification-and-scope-of-evidence","title":"6. Falsification and scope of evidence"},{"level":2,"id":"7-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work","title":"7. Connections to the published engineering work"},{"level":2,"id":"references","title":"References"}],"title_id":"semantic-objects-context-and-conditions-of-applicability","math_count":44,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/papers/semantic-objects-and-context/PAPER.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/papers/semantic-objects-and-context/PAPER.md","source_sha256":"a52ed66427fb840cfa6b2e06ad8f856c94bbfdbff01e9be19b70d7a2862b251f","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"foundations"},{"canonical":true,"id":"constrained-transformations","kind":"paper","type":"paper","path":"papers/constrained-transformations/PAPER.md","route":"/en/science/constrained-transformations/","title":"Constrained Transformations and the Limits of Knowledge Transfer","titleRu":"Constrained Transformations and the Limits of Knowledge Transfer","summary":"Conditional preservation, composition and reconstruction of knowledge under explicit observations and losses.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>Knowledge is repeatedly transformed: interpreted from a source, normalized, summarized, projected into a task, or instantiated for a new situation. A successful transformation need not preserve every property, but it must preserve the properties on which its permitted use depends. This paper models transformations as partial relations with explicit preconditions, protected observations, postconditions, and loss records. We prove conditional composition and preservation results, distinguish observation equivalence from identity, and show when a projection can or cannot be reversed. A separate result explains the limits of transferring a rule between domains. The claims concern declared transition models and successful executions; they do not imply that a transformation terminates, that its semantic annotations are complete, or that a language model follows the specified relation. The resulting obligations provide a precise basis for executable projections and for testing transformations with counterexamples.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> transformation contract; partial relation; semantic preservation; projection; transfer; reconstruction.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-research-question-and-relation-to-prior-work\">1. Research question and relation to prior work</h2>\n<p>An answer may retain the main words of a source while losing a condition that makes the answer usable. Conversely, a change of notation can preserve everything required for a particular task. The question is therefore not whether a transformation changes its input, but <strong>which observations must remain valid, under which assumptions, for which use</strong>.</p>\n<p>We develop a task-relative account of transformation. It applies to changes of representation and to restricted inference, while keeping them distinct. Converting a value to another unit, summarizing an argument, and deriving a consequence require different premises. Naming all three “processing” does not supply a common correctness guarantee.</p>\n<p>Hoare’s account of program correctness separates preconditions, program execution, and postconditions [1]. Our formulation uses the same discipline at the boundary of knowledge operations. It does not claim that arbitrary natural-language transformations already have a mechanically verified semantics. The contribution is to expose the proof obligations and the cases in which a claim of preservation is too strong.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-a-transformation-model\">2. A transformation model</h2>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be declared spaces of input and output states. A transformation is a relation</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>Y</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nT\\subseteq X\\times Y.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>A relation, rather than a total function, permits multiple valid outputs and inputs on which the operation has no admitted result. A transformation contract specifies an input predicate <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math>, output predicate <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, protected observations, and an account of allowed losses. Its partial-correctness condition is</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>:</mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\forall x,y:\\ P(x)\\land T(x,y)\\Rightarrow Q(y).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>This implication concerns successful transitions in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math>. It neither promises an output for every admitted input nor bounds computation time. A practical implementation must separately distinguish successful output, refusal, ambiguity, insufficient budget, and execution failure.</p>\n<p>A <strong>protected observation</strong> consists of interpretation functions <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_X:X\\to O</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_Y:Y\\to O</annotation></semantics></math> into a common observation space. Preservation means</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nP(x)\\land T(x,y)\\Rightarrow p_X(x)=p_Y(y).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>The observations are selected for the task. They may include a numerical value, a referent, a condition, an attribution, or an unresolved uncertainty. Equality of two unknown observations preserves the recorded uncertainty; it does not establish the unknown proposition.</p>\n<p>Not all transformations require equality. A projection may retain only a declared subset of observations; an inference may establish an output proposition from input premises under a rule. The contract must state which relation is required. Calling every relation “equivalence” would erase the distinction between preserving information and deriving a consequence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-composition-and-preservation\">3. Composition and preservation</h2>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-1-compatible-partial-correctness-contracts-compose\">Proposition 1. Compatible partial-correctness contracts compose</h3>\n<p>Suppose <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_1\\subseteq X\\times Y</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{P\\}T_1\\{Q\\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_2\\subseteq Y\\times Z</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{R\\}T_2\\{S\\}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(y)\\Rightarrow R(y)</annotation></semantics></math> for every intermediate state, then their relational composition satisfies <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∘</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{P\\}(T_2\\circ T_1)\\{S\\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> For an admitted composed transition <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x,z)</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_1(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_2(y,z)</annotation></semantics></math>. From <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the first contract yields <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(y)</annotation></semantics></math>. The compatibility assumption yields <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(y)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the second contract yields <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(z)</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>\n<p>This proposition does not prove total correctness. A second operation may have no successful transition for an intermediate state even when its partial-correctness implication holds. Existence of a result, termination, and resource sufficiency require additional assumptions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-2-a-shared-protected-observation-survives-a-finite-chain\">Proposition 2. A shared protected observation survives a finite chain</h3>\n<p>Let a finite chain of admitted transitions use observation functions into the same space <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O</annotation></semantics></math>. If every transition preserves the observation across its own interface, then the initial and final observations coincide.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Adjacent observations are equal by assumption. Equality is transitive, so repeated substitution gives equality between the first and last observations. ∎</p>\n<p>The common-observation requirement is substantive. If one operation preserves a numerical value but drops its interpretation, and another preserves only the remaining text, the chain has not preserved a quantity with units. Local declarations about different properties do not combine into a stronger end-to-end claim.</p>\n<p>A second counterexample concerns recipients. An operation may omit a condition because its current recipient already knows it. Passing the result to a different recipient changes that precondition. A technically intact record of the earlier check cannot restore a condition that the new recipient lacks.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-equivalence-projection-and-residual-information\">4. Equivalence, projection, and residual information</h2>\n<p>For a fixed observation function <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>⇔</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nx\\sim_p x&#39;\\iff p(x)=p(x&#39;).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>This is an equivalence relation because equality is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive. It is an equivalence of declared observations, not of object identity or all meaning. Enlarging the observation set can split an equivalence class that was adequate for a narrower task.</p>\n<p>A transformation descends to equivalence classes only if it respects the chosen relation. For a deterministic transformation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math>, this requires that <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\sim_p x&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> imply <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow></msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)\\sim_{p&#39;}f(x&#39;)</annotation></semantics></math>. For a partial transformation, its domain must also contain every equivalent representative or none of them. Otherwise admission or output depends on which representative was selected, and replacing one representative by another is not justified.</p>\n<p>A <strong>projection</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_J</annotation></semantics></math> retains observations indexed by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J</annotation></semantics></math>. For coordinate projections, repeated application is idempotent and projection to a smaller set loses no further information about those retained coordinates. These properties follow from the defined operation; they must not be assumed for an arbitrary summarizer merely called a projection.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-3-a-noninjective-transformation-cannot-have-a-left-inverse\">Proposition 3. A noninjective transformation cannot have a left inverse</h3>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:X\\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> map two distinct inputs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1\\ne x_2</annotation></semantics></math> to the same output. There is no function <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g:Y\\to X</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(f(x))=x</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Such a function would require the same value <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(f(x_1))=g(f(x_2))</annotation></semantics></math> to equal both distinct inputs. ∎</p>\n<p>Reconstruction becomes possible if a retained residual <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho(x)</annotation></semantics></math> makes the pair <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(f(x),\\rho(x))</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently informative and a reconstruction function is defined on that pair. For example, a projection of a two-component record can be reversed when the omitted component is stored as residual information. That restoration uses additional retained information. It is not evidence that compression creates information or that every summary is reversible.</p>\n<p>Loss records must therefore distinguish intentionally omitted content, unresolved interpretation, and information retained elsewhere. A link to a residual helps only when the recipient can retrieve and interpret it within the permitted scope and resource budget.</p>\n<p>A finite Boolean model gives an exact special case of shared content and residual information. Write <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(F)</annotation></semantics></math> for the models of formula <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math>. Entailment is inclusion of model sets. The formula <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=F\\lor G</annotation></semantics></math> is a strongest common consequence of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math>: both entail <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and any consequence of both is true throughout <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(F)\\cup M(G)=M(C)</annotation></semantics></math>. Define <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>¬</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_F=\\neg C\\lor F</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo>¬</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>≡</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nC\\land R_F=C\\land(\\neg C\\lor F)\\equiv C\\land F\\equiv F,\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>because <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math> entails <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves reconstruction with that residual in the declared logic. It does not identify a useful common theme between arbitrary documents. The residual convention is also not unique outside <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math>. An inconsistent premise set has no models and classically entails every formula; operational admission must therefore separately require an appropriate consistent, nonempty interpretation rather than turn vacuous entailment into a recommendation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-transfer-between-tasks-and-domains\">5. Transfer between tasks and domains</h2>\n<p>Transfer is a directed operation. A rule applicable under conditions <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math> can be used for a target only after those conditions have been mapped and checked. Similar vocabulary is a candidate-generation signal, not a proof that the required relation is preserved.</p>\n<p>Consider a source rule</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nC(x)\\Rightarrow P(x).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>To infer <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>′</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P&#39;(y)</annotation></semantics></math> in a target representation, one needs a mapping between the relevant source and target objects, justification of the corresponding condition, and a bridge showing that the source conclusion supports <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P&#39;</annotation></semantics></math>. Without such a bridge, the source rule remains a statement about its original interpretation.</p>\n<p>An abstract counterexample is sufficient. Two systems can share a scalar variable called “size,” while one has output proportional to size and the other has output proportional to its square. Matching the variable name and one observed point does not justify transferring the linear rule. A usable transfer contract identifies the governing relation and its domain, rather than relying on surface similarity.</p>\n<p>Units provide a limited positive case. If two units describe the same dimension and their relation is an exact rational factor, exact conversion preserves the represented quantity. The conclusion depends on dimension compatibility, the factor, and exact arithmetic. It does not validate an incorrectly extracted input value, an uncertain measurement, or an unrelated domain equation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-records-implementation-and-tests\">6. Records, implementation, and tests</h2>\n<p>A transformation record should identify the input and output revisions, declared operation, active preconditions, protected observations, verification result, and any loss or residual. Provenance is useful here because it makes dependencies inspectable [2]. Provenance alone does not establish that the operation satisfied its contract.</p>\n<p>An implementation claiming compliance must connect its actual outputs to the relation used in the argument. A contract written next to a model does not constrain the model’s behavior unless execution is checked or the implementation is independently justified. A finite checker can establish only properties in its supported language and bounded domain. Unsupported rules remain unsupported; they do not become valid because the system produces a plausible explanation.</p>\n<p>Tests should include incompatible intermediate types, a dropped exception, changed recipients, a noninjective projection offered as reversible, an omitted residual, and a transfer with matching labels but incompatible relations. Positive tests should vary irrelevant features while keeping protected observations fixed. Negative tests should change one material assumption at a time and require refusal, qualified output, or a failed check.</p>\n<p>The propositions in this paper are complete within their stated mathematical assumptions. No empirical claim is made that a particular extractor or generator reliably supplies those assumptions. The corresponding measurement question is the frequency and consequence of violations on independently constructed transformations, with separate reporting for interpretation, execution, and verification errors.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work\">7. Connections to the published engineering work</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">executable projections and revision study</a> is the location for finite transition implementations and their controls. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">dependency closure and package delivery study</a> examines whether protected conditions survive actual delivery. Domain-specific conversions and transfers belong in those studies.</p>\n<p>The shared <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a> defines project terminology. <a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">Semantic objects and context</a> supplies the interpretation boundary; <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">controlled knowledge reuse</a> applies the preservation argument to previously prepared artifacts.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Hoare, C. A. R. “An Axiomatic Basis for Computer Programming.” <em>Communications of the ACM</em> 12(10), 1969, 576–580, 583. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1145/363235.363259\" class=\"uri\">https://doi.org/10.1145/363235.363259</a></li>\n<li>W3C. <em>PROV-DM: The PROV Data Model</em>. W3C Recommendation, 2013. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/\" class=\"uri\">https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/</a></li>\n</ol>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"1-research-question-and-relation-to-prior-work","title":"1. Research question and relation to prior work"},{"level":2,"id":"2-a-transformation-model","title":"2. A transformation model"},{"level":2,"id":"3-composition-and-preservation","title":"3. Composition and preservation"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-1-compatible-partial-correctness-contracts-compose","title":"Proposition 1. Compatible partial-correctness contracts compose"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-2-a-shared-protected-observation-survives-a-finite-chain","title":"Proposition 2. A shared protected observation survives a finite chain"},{"level":2,"id":"4-equivalence-projection-and-residual-information","title":"4. Equivalence, projection, and residual information"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-3-a-noninjective-transformation-cannot-have-a-left-inverse","title":"Proposition 3. A noninjective transformation cannot have a left inverse"},{"level":2,"id":"5-transfer-between-tasks-and-domains","title":"5. Transfer between tasks and domains"},{"level":2,"id":"6-records-implementation-and-tests","title":"6. Records, implementation, and tests"},{"level":2,"id":"7-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work","title":"7. Connections to the published engineering work"},{"level":2,"id":"references","title":"References"}],"title_id":"constrained-transformations-and-the-limits-of-knowledge-transfer","math_count":56,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/papers/constrained-transformations/PAPER.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/papers/constrained-transformations/PAPER.md","source_sha256":"9cd74f05b1612e136060f2190a13099a35e6e4dfafe6c0a1fc1b543719ea0db0","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"foundations"},{"canonical":true,"id":"retrieval-and-context-composition","kind":"paper","type":"paper","path":"papers/retrieval-and-context-composition/PAPER.md","route":"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/","title":"Retrieval, Coverage, and Context Composition under Resource Limits","titleRu":"Retrieval, Coverage, and Context Composition under Resource Limits","summary":"Search coverage, finite context selection, dependency closure and the limits of greedy composition.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>Retrieving relevant fragments and assembling a sufficient context are different problems. A retrieved fragment can be pertinent while omitting a mandatory exception; a package can cover declared requirements without demonstrating that the search was exhaustive. This paper gives a finite model of requirements, alternative supporting groups, mandatory dependencies, representations, and resource limits. We distinguish search assurance, requirement coverage, composition feasibility, and actual output verification. Conditional results characterize exact finite selection, a set-cover special case, and the limited applicability of a classical submodular-greedy guarantee. Counterexamples show why jointly necessary grounds and shared dependencies defeat common additive assumptions. The contribution is a precise contract for what a retrieval-and-composition result establishes, including meaningful stopping outcomes. Domain evaluations and executable selectors are separate engineering objects; the present argument establishes no general empirical advantage for a retrieval method or language model.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> retrieval; context composition; coverage; dependency closure; resource budget; submodularity.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-the-question-between-retrieval-and-answering\">1. The question between retrieval and answering</h2>\n<p>A retrieval system proposes material that may support an answer. The original retrieval-augmented generation formulation combines external retrieval with a generative model [1]. That architecture does not itself certify that every necessary condition reached the generator or survived its answer.</p>\n<p>Our question is: <strong>what can be certified about a bounded context assembled from a declared search result?</strong> We separate four judgments: whether the search procedure examined the required scope; whether the available grounds support the requirements; whether the delivered representation fits the resource constraints; and whether the final answer preserves the required content.</p>\n<p>These judgments are related but not interchangeable. Returning ten plausible passages does not prove that an eleventh exception does not exist. Fitting a token limit does not prove that the recipient notices a warning. A completed search does not make contradictory sources consistent.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-query-scope-and-coverage\">2. Query, scope, and coverage</h2>\n<p>A query record fixes an intent, referents, time interpretation, requirements, permitted source scope, and answer form. Its requirements are obligations to establish or communicate, rather than merely words to match. A request for one supporting example has different obligations from a request for all examples, a count, or a statement that none exists.</p>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math> be the declared source snapshot and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math> the returned candidate set. Search assurance concerns the procedure that produced <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math>. An exhaustive finite scan under a specified predicate can support completeness within <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math>; a top-ranked sample generally cannot. Changes to access visibility, scope membership, predicates, or ranking rules can change the answer even if previously returned records are unchanged.</p>\n<p>Coverage is assessed relative to a pinned requirement set <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R</annotation></semantics></math>. For each requirement we distinguish supported, partial, unsupported, conflicted, and unknown. These are reporting categories with explicit criteria, not a universal many-valued logic. “Unsupported in this package” does not mean false, absent from the whole corpus, or impossible to establish.</p>\n<p>Coverage should be recorded at three boundaries: the registered source base, the retrieved candidates, and the delivered package. A fourth check concerns the actual answer or action proposal. Keeping these reports distinct locates loss: missing from the base, missed by retrieval, omitted during composition, or ignored during generation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-composition-with-joint-grounds-and-dependencies\">3. Composition with joint grounds and dependencies</h2>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite set of candidate object revisions. For a selected subset <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>cl</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{cl}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> denote its mandatory dependency closure under a fixed, declared graph. A requirement <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math> can have alternative supporting groups <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒢</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal G_r</annotation></semantics></math>. A group contains jointly necessary grounds, not merely individually relevant fragments.</p>\n<p>Under fixed assessments, define support by</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>Supports</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇔</mo><mo>∃</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝒢</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mi>G</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mo>cl</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mo>Valid</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\operatorname{Supports}(r,A)\n\\iff \\exists G\\in\\mathcal G_r:\\ G\\subseteq\\operatorname{cl}(A)\n\\ \\land\\ \\operatorname{Valid}(G,r).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>The validity predicate includes the relevant interpretation and applicability checks. Listing a group does not prove that its members support the requirement. In empirical use, that judgment requires independently assessed evidence or a sound supported rule.</p>\n<p>A composition decision <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math> includes selected content, permitted transformations, ordering, modality, and delivery form. Its actual representation is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>Render</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Render}(z)</annotation></semantics></math>, with declared cost <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c</annotation></semantics></math>. Feasibility requires mandatory support, all other admission conditions, and</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo>Render</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nc(\\operatorname{Render}(z))\\leq B.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>The budget may be a vector: tokens, bytes, recipient time, or another measured resource. Different units should not be silently added. A scalar objective requires a declared conversion or priority rule.</p>\n<p>Costs need not be additive over fragments. Two selected objects can share a dependency, so summing their separately closed sizes double-counts it. Conversely, a delivery format can introduce headings, repeated legends, or fixed overhead. The delivered representation, rather than the unformatted fragments, is the relevant accounting object.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-exactness-and-special-case-guarantees\">4. Exactness and special-case guarantees</h2>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-1-complete-finite-selection-gives-a-scoped-optimum\">Proposition 1. Complete finite selection gives a scoped optimum</h3>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite, explicitly declared decision space. Suppose feasibility and objective value are evaluated correctly for every member. If enumeration finishes and returns a feasible member with maximal objective, that member is optimal in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. If enumeration finishes with no feasible member, no feasible member exists in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Every possible decision in the declared space has been checked. A better feasible decision or any feasible decision in the second case would therefore have appeared in the enumeration, contradicting the stated result. ∎</p>\n<p>This establishes neither completeness of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z</annotation></semantics></math> relative to all possible summaries nor correctness of semantic annotations. If execution stops early, the best verified candidate remains a feasible incumbent; optimality and infeasibility are no longer established.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-2-a-restricted-composition-problem-contains-set-cover\">Proposition 2. A restricted composition problem contains set cover</h3>\n<p>Consider unit-cost, independent packages, no transformations or dependencies, and the requirement to cover every obligation using at most <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math> packages. Given a set-cover instance with universe <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math> and subsets <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_1,\\ldots,F_n</annotation></semantics></math>, create one requirement per element of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math> and one package per <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_i</annotation></semantics></math>, supporting exactly those requirements. The construction is polynomial, and selected packages cover all requirements exactly when their corresponding subsets cover <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<p>This reduction transfers the known combinatorial difficulty of set cover [2] to this special case. It does not imply that every practical query is difficult, that extraction itself is formally solved, or that a heuristic has a particular approximation ratio.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-3-the-cardinality-greedy-bound-requires-submodularity\">Proposition 3. The cardinality-greedy bound requires submodularity</h3>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math> be a nonnegative, monotone, submodular set function with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⌀</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(\\varnothing)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. With at most <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\geq1</annotation></semantics></math> selected elements and no other constraints, greedy selection by greatest marginal gain achieves at least</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>OPT</mo><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>OPT</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\left(1-\\left(1-\\frac1k\\right)^k\\right)\\operatorname{OPT}\n\\geq (1-1/e)\\operatorname{OPT}.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><strong>Argument.</strong> At step <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i</annotation></semantics></math>, submodularity bounds the remaining gain of an optimal set by the sum of at most <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math> current marginal gains. The best next gain is therefore at least <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo>OPT</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\operatorname{OPT}-f(A_i))/k</annotation></semantics></math>. Each step reduces the remaining gap by a factor no greater than <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-1/k</annotation></semantics></math>. Iterating from the empty set gives the bound. This is the classical result of Nemhauser, Wolsey, and Fisher [3], not a new theorem of the present model. ∎</p>\n<p>The result does not cover arbitrary size-based greedy selection, nonadditive costs, or arbitrary mandatory closure. Those settings need their own justified algorithms and assumptions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-counterexamples-to-convenient-assumptions\">5. Counterexamples to convenient assumptions</h2>\n<p>Joint grounds need not be submodular. Suppose a requirement needs both a rule <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math> and a condition <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⌀</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(\\varnothing)=f(\\{a\\})=f(\\{b\\})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, but <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(\\{a,b\\})=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Adding <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math> to the empty set yields no gain; adding it after <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math> yields one. This violates diminishing returns. Individual ranking can therefore discard both members of the only useful pair.</p>\n<p>Mandatory dependency closure can also break downward closure of feasible selections. A package containing a conclusion and its required condition may be valid; removing the condition makes the remaining subset invalid. A method justified for systems where every subset of a feasible set remains feasible cannot be imported unchanged.</p>\n<p>Budget pressure does not remove these obligations. If two mandatory grounds and an exception cost seven units in total and the budget is five, deleting the exception produces a smaller but inadmissible package. A feasible reformulation might exist, but it must be constructed and checked. Failure of one heuristic to find it is not proof of impossibility.</p>\n<p>Finally, declared coverage can be invalidated by new information. Support is monotone only under explicitly fixed grounds, applicability judgments, and conflict rules. An added contradictory source or a changed requirement can require reassessment even when old passages remain available.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-stopping-and-measurement\">6. Stopping and measurement</h2>\n<p>A useful result reports its scope and stopping reason. Distinct outcomes include a verified complete package, an explicitly partial package, a need for clarification, insufficient resources, an exhausted search limit, and stale or conflicting evidence. Stopping after a limit does not establish absence. A useful partial answer is possible when the answer form permits it and its uncovered obligations remain visible.</p>\n<p>Evaluation should compare methods under the same requirement snapshot, source scope, delivery constraints, and outcome rubric. Relevant baselines include ranked selection with dependency repair, selection of complete supporting groups, and exact enumeration on small instances. Removing dependencies or truncating delivery supplies ablations, not necessarily competitive baselines.</p>\n<p>Measures should distinguish retrieval misses, invalid support groups, omitted dependencies, budget violations, and final-answer failures. Report denominators and uncertainty, including the number of cases that could not be adjudicated. Counting selected fragments or citing a larger source list does not measure sufficiency.</p>\n<p>The mathematical results here hold for the declared finite model. They establish neither universal end-to-end answer correctness nor measured superiority on people, models, or production workloads. Recipient understanding and attention require separate observation, even when representation cost is measured exactly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work\">7. Connections to the published engineering work</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">dependency closure and package delivery study</a> contains the appropriate finite controls for closure, selection, rendering, and delivery. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/\">contract profile conformance study</a> concerns structural validation, which must remain distinct from semantic sufficiency.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a> gives common terminology. <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">Evidence and falsification</a> specifies evaluation obligations; <a href=\"/en/science/knowledge-utility/\">knowledge utility</a> explains why a composition objective must state its baseline, horizon, and resource accounting.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Lewis, P., et al. “Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks.” <em>Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems</em> 33, 2020. <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401\" class=\"uri\">https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401</a></li>\n<li>Karp, R. M. “Reducibility Among Combinatorial Problems.” In <em>Complexity of Computer Computations</em>, 1972, 85–103. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4684-2001-2_9\" class=\"uri\">https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4684-2001-2_9</a></li>\n<li>Nemhauser, G. L., Wolsey, L. A., and Fisher, M. L. “An Analysis of Approximations for Maximizing Submodular Set Functions—I.” <em>Mathematical Programming</em> 14, 1978, 265–294. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01588971\" class=\"uri\">https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01588971</a></li>\n</ol>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"1-the-question-between-retrieval-and-answering","title":"1. The question between retrieval and answering"},{"level":2,"id":"2-query-scope-and-coverage","title":"2. Query, scope, and coverage"},{"level":2,"id":"3-composition-with-joint-grounds-and-dependencies","title":"3. Composition with joint grounds and dependencies"},{"level":2,"id":"4-exactness-and-special-case-guarantees","title":"4. Exactness and special-case guarantees"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-1-complete-finite-selection-gives-a-scoped-optimum","title":"Proposition 1. Complete finite selection gives a scoped optimum"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-2-a-restricted-composition-problem-contains-set-cover","title":"Proposition 2. A restricted composition problem contains set cover"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-3-the-cardinality-greedy-bound-requires-submodularity","title":"Proposition 3. The cardinality-greedy bound requires submodularity"},{"level":2,"id":"5-counterexamples-to-convenient-assumptions","title":"5. Counterexamples to convenient assumptions"},{"level":2,"id":"6-stopping-and-measurement","title":"6. Stopping and measurement"},{"level":2,"id":"7-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work","title":"7. Connections to the published engineering work"},{"level":2,"id":"references","title":"References"}],"title_id":"retrieval-coverage-and-context-composition-under-resource-limits","math_count":39,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/papers/retrieval-and-context-composition/PAPER.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/papers/retrieval-and-context-composition/PAPER.md","source_sha256":"618d3af8d7f41823246627efe3c497069a374250fdf318d1c4089d890c1543cc","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"systems"},{"canonical":true,"id":"interaction-and-memory","kind":"paper","type":"paper","path":"papers/interaction-and-memory/PAPER.md","route":"/en/science/interaction-and-memory/","title":"Interaction, Documents, and Memory with Explicit Revision Boundaries","titleRu":"Interaction, Documents, and Memory with Explicit Revision Boundaries","summary":"Versioned requirements, dialogue branches, memory and evidence of learning in agent interaction.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>An assistant’s conversation, work state, documents, and durable memory evolve at different rates and under different rules. Treating them as one expanding transcript makes corrections, delayed results, and changes of purpose difficult to interpret. This paper defines a state model that separates messages from communicative acts, Work Items from execution attempts, and current task context from admitted memory. Documents are versioned outputs whose correctness must be checked after rendering or revision. We establish conditional properties for generation boundaries, idempotent event handling, dependency-triggered reassessment, and narrow update scope. Counterexamples identify where correct state handling still permits semantic error. The model also distinguishes observed task success from learning and user acceptance from evidence. Its contribution is a coherent set of transition obligations for dialogue and memory, with falsifiable implementation consequences. It does not establish that any current interpreter reliably recognizes every intention, referent, or learning outcome.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> dialogue; Work Item; document revision; memory; requirements; learning assessment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-research-question\">1. Research question</h2>\n<p>A conversation can contain instructions, quotations, corrections, tentative ideas, and reports of previous actions. It can also receive a delayed result after the user has changed the goal. If every new message simply overwrites one mutable state, the system cannot reliably explain which requirements a result satisfies or why a memory applies.</p>\n<p>We ask: <strong>how should interaction state change so that the scope of a statement, correction, or result remains inspectable?</strong> The proposed answer separates interpretation from authorized transition, and separates immediate dialogue context from persistent knowledge.</p>\n<p>The model builds on ordinary distinctions between activities, entities, and agents represented in PROV-DM [1]. It adds explicit obligations for dialogue generations, document outputs, and admission into memory. These obligations are a design model. Their practical adequacy must be assessed against independently interpreted conversations, not inferred from the existence of a structured event log.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-messages-acts-and-work\">2. Messages, acts, and work</h2>\n<p>A <strong>message</strong> is a received or generated representation with sender attribution, time, and content. A <strong>communicative act</strong> is an interpreted function of some content: a request, correction, question, proposal, acknowledgment, or report. One message can contain several acts. An act record should identify its target, scope, and uncertainty.</p>\n<p>Quoted content is not automatically an instruction from the present speaker. Likewise, a statement that an action occurred is evidence of that report, not proof of execution. Interpretation assigns a candidate role; authorization determines whether the resulting transition is permitted.</p>\n<p>A <strong>Work Item</strong> is a bounded objective with requirements, constraints, expected outputs, and acceptance conditions. <code>WorkItem</code> is a serialization spelling of that same entity, not a second concept. An <strong>attempt</strong> is one execution toward the objective. A document or other <strong>output</strong> is a result of an attempt. A message can create or modify a Work Item without itself constituting an execution attempt.</p>\n<p>Let the active work state be</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>O</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nW=(i,g,R,C,O,A),\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i</annotation></semantics></math> is identity, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math> is a requirement generation, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R</annotation></semantics></math> requirements, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math> constraints, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O</annotation></semantics></math> output expectations, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math> acceptance conditions. A material change creates a new generation. This does not require abandoning all previous work: an earlier output can be re-evaluated for the new generation, with its original provenance preserved.</p>\n<p>Understanding, agreement, evidence, and authority remain separate. A user can understand an answer and reject its conclusion. An authorized owner can accept a document despite an acknowledged evidential limitation. Neither act changes the truth of its factual propositions by definition.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-context-and-memory-layers\">3. Context and memory layers</h2>\n<p>We distinguish immediate conversation context, the current Work Item state, durable user or project memory, and externally sourced knowledge. The distinction is operational: each layer has different admission, revision, scope, and retrieval rules. Storing everything in one database does not eliminate these differences.</p>\n<p>A memory record contains content, provenance, applicability scope, assessment state, and a revision or revocation relation. A statement intended for one task does not become a permanent preference merely because it is repeated. Widening its scope requires a justified transition, such as an explicit instruction or a separately supported generalization.</p>\n<p>Memory admission is therefore a decision with conditions. Relevant conditions can include attribution, intended persistence, consistency with current instructions, permission to retain, and a defined use scope. Unknown mandatory conditions do not count as satisfied. Retrieval later must check whether the record remains applicable to the present task and recipient.</p>\n<p>Memory has dependencies. A derived preference, summary, or plan may rely on earlier records whose revision changes its basis. Revocation does not always imply physical deletion: a historical record may remain necessary for an audit while being inadmissible for current guidance. Current use, archival availability, and storage retention must be modeled separately.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-documents-as-evaluated-outputs\">4. Documents as evaluated outputs</h2>\n<p>A document is a versioned representation, not merely a string emitted during conversation. Its assessment has several axes: source fidelity, factual support, satisfaction of requirements, internal consistency, readability, accessibility, and acceptance for a specific use. A fluent document can fail a mandatory requirement; a faithful transcription can be poorly organized.</p>\n<p>Generation and editing therefore have a final-output obligation. The actual document must be inspected after assembly, including headings, references, tables, images, and any constraints that can be lost during rendering. Checking only the retrieved sources or an intermediate outline leaves the delivered output unevaluated.</p>\n<p>A local edit also has both local and global effects. Replacing one section can change cross-references, definitions, totals, or the force of a conclusion elsewhere. The edit record should state its intended scope, while dependency information identifies the additional checks needed. A claim that “only one paragraph changed” is not evidence that every unaffected-looking paragraph remains valid.</p>\n<p>Document summaries become a separate memory object when reused. A summary may omit details acceptable for its original audience but necessary for a later task. Reuse should apply the same applicability and dependency discipline as any other prepared knowledge.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-conditional-transition-properties\">5. Conditional transition properties</h2>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-1-generation-admission-prevents-a-specified-stale-update\">Proposition 1. Generation admission prevents a specified stale update</h3>\n<p>Suppose a result carries the Work Item identity and generation for which it was produced. Suppose the state transition accepts it as current only when those identifiers match the active state, or after a separate successful re-evaluation against the active requirements. Then a result from an earlier generation cannot be accepted as current solely through its original completion event.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> A mismatching event fails the direct-admission condition. The only remaining path requires a new evaluation, so original completion alone is insufficient. ∎</p>\n<p>This property depends on correct identifiers and enforcement at the transition boundary. It does not guarantee that the interpreter recognized a material requirement change or that the re-evaluation is semantically correct.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-2-recorded-duplicate-events-need-not-duplicate-effects\">Proposition 2. Recorded duplicate events need not duplicate effects</h3>\n<p>Let an event handler record stable event identifiers and their payload fingerprints in the same atomic state change as the internal effect. Repeating an already committed event with the same payload returns the recorded result without applying the effect again. An identifier with a different payload is rejected as a conflict.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> After the first commit, the event identifier is present. The duplicate branch performs no new effect. Atomicity excludes a committed effect without the corresponding recorded identifier. ∎</p>\n<p>This establishes idempotent handling within the stated state boundary. It does not establish exactly-once execution of an external action whose system is outside that atomic boundary.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-3-complete-dependency-tracking-exposes-affected-outputs\">Proposition 3. Complete dependency tracking exposes affected outputs</h3>\n<p>For a fixed finite dependency graph, if every material dependency of an output is represented, reverse reachability from a changed record identifies every output whose declared basis depends on that record.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Each such dependency is a finite path from the output to the changed record. Reversing the path makes the output reachable from the changed record. Complete traversal therefore includes it. ∎</p>\n<p>Reachability marks a need for reassessment; it does not prove that the output has become false. An omitted dependency defeats the completeness premise, even if traversal is implemented perfectly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-4-a-narrow-update-preserves-independent-state-components\">Proposition 4. A narrow update preserves independent state components</h3>\n<p>Let a transition write only a declared component set <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J</annotation></semantics></math> of a product state and leave all other components unchanged. Then every observation depending solely on components outside <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J</annotation></semantics></math> is preserved.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Such an observation receives identical component values before and after the transition. ∎</p>\n<p>This syntactic preservation result requires correct scope. If two components are semantically coupled but that coupling is omitted, unchanged bytes do not guarantee an unchanged valid interpretation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-learning-correction-and-counterexamples\">6. Learning, correction, and counterexamples</h2>\n<p>A useful tutoring conversation distinguishes the learner’s own attempt, supplied assistance, observed performance, and the assessment being made. Repeating an explanation or accepting an answer does not establish learning. A learning claim requires a declared skill, assessment conditions, a time horizon, and evidence that discriminates retained ability from immediate assistance or memorized responses.</p>\n<p>This is a measurement obligation rather than a theory of human learning. Different studies may examine transfer, delayed retention, error correction, or independent task completion. Their outcomes should not be collapsed into one unqualified “learned” flag.</p>\n<p>Counterexamples test the boundaries of dialogue interpretation. A correction to one branch must not silently rewrite another branch. An ambiguous “use that version” should not become a verified referent without resolution. A delayed answer must not reinstate a withdrawn requirement. A quoted instruction must not acquire the authority of its surrounding message merely because it uses imperative wording.</p>\n<p>Further counterexamples concern memory and documents. A task-specific choice should not become an unrestricted preference; a revoked premise should not remain an unnoticed basis for a recommendation; and a successful local edit should not waive whole-document consistency checks. These cases can be specified without assuming any particular domain or model.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-evidence-and-limits\">7. Evidence and limits</h2>\n<p>The model supports finite transition tests and independently annotated dialogue cases. A useful evaluation reports interpretation mistakes separately from transition-handling mistakes. Otherwise a correct event processor can hide a poor interpreter, or an accurate interpreter can be blamed for an unrelated state-management defect.</p>\n<p>This paper establishes only its conditional state properties. It reports no measured improvement in learning, long-term recall, or agent reliability. Structural validation cannot authenticate a speaker by itself, and a requirements map cannot prove that the final document satisfies its intended meaning. Those dependencies require their own evidence and must remain visible in a claim of end-to-end performance.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">executable projections and revision study</a> covers bounded revisions and transition controls. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">editorial skill pilot</a> is the place for comparing produced documents with actual editorial requirements. The shared <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>, <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">revision and governance</a>, and <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">evidence and falsification</a> supply related definitions and assessment boundaries.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>W3C. <em>PROV-DM: The PROV Data Model</em>. W3C Recommendation, 2013. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/\" class=\"uri\">https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/</a></li>\n</ol>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"1-research-question","title":"1. Research question"},{"level":2,"id":"2-messages-acts-and-work","title":"2. Messages, acts, and work"},{"level":2,"id":"3-context-and-memory-layers","title":"3. Context and memory layers"},{"level":2,"id":"4-documents-as-evaluated-outputs","title":"4. Documents as evaluated outputs"},{"level":2,"id":"5-conditional-transition-properties","title":"5. Conditional transition properties"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-1-generation-admission-prevents-a-specified-stale-update","title":"Proposition 1. Generation admission prevents a specified stale update"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-2-recorded-duplicate-events-need-not-duplicate-effects","title":"Proposition 2. Recorded duplicate events need not duplicate effects"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-3-complete-dependency-tracking-exposes-affected-outputs","title":"Proposition 3. Complete dependency tracking exposes affected outputs"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-4-a-narrow-update-preserves-independent-state-components","title":"Proposition 4. A narrow update preserves independent state components"},{"level":2,"id":"6-learning-correction-and-counterexamples","title":"6. Learning, correction, and counterexamples"},{"level":2,"id":"7-evidence-and-limits","title":"7. Evidence and limits"},{"level":2,"id":"references","title":"References"}],"title_id":"interaction-documents-and-memory-with-explicit-revision-boundaries","math_count":9,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/papers/interaction-and-memory/PAPER.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/papers/interaction-and-memory/PAPER.md","source_sha256":"1460a5f983dd83ee08e01023b37597f50bfbd7e37f371d0b3530f55c530f583a","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"systems"},{"canonical":true,"id":"evidence-and-falsification","kind":"paper","type":"paper","path":"papers/evidence-and-falsification/PAPER.md","route":"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/","title":"Evidence, Reasoning, and Falsification in Knowledge Systems","titleRu":"Evidence, Reasoning, and Falsification in Knowledge Systems","summary":"How claims, proofs, finite tests, counterexamples and comparative evaluations support different conclusions.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>A knowledge system can pass structural checks while offering an unsupported conclusion. It can also possess a sound formal argument whose assumptions are not satisfied by an implementation. This paper specifies how to connect claims, grounds, inference rules, checks, and decisions without confusing their evidential roles. We define a claim record and a finite justification graph, prove a conditional soundness property for supported acyclic derivations, and characterize the scope of finite testing. We then give a falsification protocol that separates counterexamples to a model, implementation defects, annotation failures, and limits of empirical transfer. Evaluation must use pinned requirements, explicit comparators, independent reference judgments where needed, and a predeclared analysis and stopping policy. The contribution is a method for determining exactly what a result supports, including negative findings and unresolved claims. No number of structural checks is treated as proof of unrestricted semantic correctness or practical superiority.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> evidence; inference; falsification; claim scope; evaluation; counterexample.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-research-question-and-evidential-distinctions\">1. Research question and evidential distinctions</h2>\n<p>Knowledge systems produce many objects that resemble evidence: citations, validation reports, explanations, benchmark scores, approvals, and execution logs. Their resemblance does not make them interchangeable. A citation can establish provenance without establishing truth. Approval can establish permission without establishing correctness. A passing test can establish one observed behavior without establishing all possible behaviors.</p>\n<p>We ask: <strong>how can a scientific and engineering claim remain accountable to the kind, scope, and limitations of its actual grounds?</strong> Our answer is a claim-centered method connecting each assertion to its premises, evaluators, counterexamples, and revision conditions.</p>\n<p>Program-logic reasoning already makes correctness conditional on stated preconditions and semantics [1]. PROV-DM provides a vocabulary for the origins and production of entities [2]. We use those distinctions while addressing a further operational problem: evidence about a source, model, checker, implementation, and deployment must not silently migrate between those subjects.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-claims-and-independent-assessment-axes\">2. Claims and independent assessment axes</h2>\n<p>A <strong>claim</strong> is a proposition or quantified assertion with an identified subject, scope, and interpretation. A useful record specifies its content, revision, claim kind, assumptions, supporting grounds, potential refuters, assessment method, and unresolved dependencies. The scope includes relevant versions, task distribution, population, time interval, and operating conditions when those matter.</p>\n<p>Claim kinds include definitional, deductive, empirical, comparative, causal, and normative. These are not mutually exclusive labels for an entire document: a paper can define a model, prove a property of it, and propose an empirical hypothesis. Each assertion needs the appropriate grounds.</p>\n<p>We distinguish at least five questions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Is the source or record authentic and correctly attributed?</li>\n<li>Is its represented content faithful to that source?</li>\n<li>Does the evidence support the proposition under the stated interpretation?</li>\n<li>Is the proposition applicable to the current use?</li>\n<li>Is the proposed use authorized?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These assessments need not agree. An accurately attributed false statement can be useful as an object of refutation. A true proposition can be inapplicable to a different date. An authorized decision can be poorly supported. A single confidence score cannot preserve these distinctions unless its meaning and aggregation rule explicitly encode them.</p>\n<p>Unknown is not false, and absence of a recorded refutation is not confirmation. An assessment can remain unresolved while a restricted informational output is permitted. A mandatory action precondition, however, is not satisfied merely because the system failed to find contrary evidence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-justification-graphs-and-conditional-soundness\">3. Justification graphs and conditional soundness</h2>\n<p>A <strong>justification graph</strong> contains claim nodes, premise nodes, and explicit inference applications. An inference application identifies its rule, input claims, output claim, and relevant side conditions. It may also carry a computation or verification witness. The graph is an explanation of the offered justification, not a complete record of every internal mental or computational event.</p>\n<p>For the following result, take a finite directed acyclic derivation graph. Each leaf is a premise, and every other claim is obtained by a supported inference rule whose inputs appear earlier in a topological order. Interpret truth in one fixed domain model.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-1-sound-rules-preserve-true-premises-in-a-finite-derivation\">Proposition 1. Sound rules preserve true premises in a finite derivation</h3>\n<p>If every premise is true in the model and every used rule is sound for that model, every derived claim is true in that model.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Order the graph topologically. The initial premise nodes are true by assumption. At each subsequent node, all input claims are true by the induction hypothesis. Soundness of the applied rule makes the output true. Induction covers every node. ∎</p>\n<p>The result is conditional on both premises and rule semantics. It does not show that the premises were correctly extracted, that the domain model is adequate, or that the implementation actually followed the rule. A computation with correctly applied arithmetic and an incorrectly read input remains an incorrect domain result.</p>\n<p>Circular support is outside this proof. A cycle that repeats a conclusion as its own premise supplies no independent foundation. Some systems admit explicitly defined fixed-point reasoning, but they require a separate semantics and correctness argument. Finite serialization of a cycle is not that argument.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-2-a-countermodel-refutes-a-universal-claim-within-its-scope\">Proposition 2. A countermodel refutes a universal claim within its scope</h3>\n<p>For a claim <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall x\\in D, P(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, an admissible <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0\\in D</annotation></semantics></math> with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>¬</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg P(x_0)</annotation></semantics></math> refutes the claim.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Universal quantification requires <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math> for every element of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math>, including <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>\n<p>The domain condition matters. A failure outside the claimed domain can motivate a broader study but does not refute the narrower theorem. Conversely, narrowing the domain after seeing a failure produces a revised claim; it does not retroactively turn the original claim into a successful one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-what-different-checks-establish\">4. What different checks establish</h2>\n<p>Structural validation establishes compliance with a declared representation rule when the validator is correct. It may verify mandatory fields, references, enumerations, or finite relational conditions. It does not establish that a field’s natural-language content is correctly interpreted.</p>\n<p>A formal proof establishes a consequence under its assumptions and formal semantics. Executable tests establish observed behavior for the tested cases, subject to their oracle and execution conditions. An exhaustive finite test can support a universal statement about its enumerated finite model; it does not automatically cover unbounded inputs or a deployed environment with different behavior.</p>\n<p>Human review can assess interpretation, relevance, and argument quality within a defined rubric. Its strength depends on expertise, access to sources, independence, and adjudication. Production observations can reveal actual failures and costs, but selected observations alone do not identify a causal benefit.</p>\n<p>These evidence types are complementary, not a single ladder on which more items always mean stronger support. Ten tests sharing the same incorrect oracle can repeat one mistake. Two reviewers with access to each other’s answer may not provide the intended independent judgment. Independence should be described as a concrete procedure, not inferred from different agent names.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-a-falsification-protocol\">5. A falsification protocol</h2>\n<p>An evaluation begins by freezing the claim and requirements before examining the decisive cases. The record should specify the subject versions, admissible inputs, expected behavior, failure criteria, scope of generalization, and how uncertain cases will be handled. Changes create a new evaluation record rather than overwriting the earlier one.</p>\n<p>Counterexamples should target assumptions. For extraction, change a referent, logical scope, or source revision. For composition, omit a jointly required ground or add a conflicting condition. For reuse, change a material dependency or current permission. For resource accounting, repeat a telemetry event or expose a shared cost counted twice. The purpose is to distinguish a system that enforces the condition from one that merely passes familiar positive examples.</p>\n<p>An independent impact oracle is especially important for dependency completeness. Traversing an annotated graph can verify traversal; it cannot reveal an omitted dependency if the reference answer is generated from that same graph. A useful test mutates a relevant source feature and independently specifies whether the output should change.</p>\n<p>The protocol should separate at least four failure classes: the model’s claim is false; the implementation violates the model; the input interpretation or annotation is wrong; or the evaluation lacks enough information to decide. Combining them into a single failure total can obscure the repair required.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-comparators-analysis-and-stopping\">6. Comparators, analysis, and stopping</h2>\n<p>A comparative claim needs a relevant baseline with matched tasks, resource accounting, and acceptance conditions. A method that produces more complete outputs at a higher cost must not be described as unconditionally cheaper. A method that skips required checks must not gain an apparent speed advantage without reporting the lost assurance.</p>\n<p>The analysis plan should state the primary outcome, denominators, treatment of missing and unresolved cases, uncertainty estimates, and stopping conditions. If evaluation stops after a budget limit, the report should identify the covered and uncovered cases. Selecting only successful attempts or changing the success rule after inspection creates a different evidential object from the planned study.</p>\n<p>Data separation also matters. Cases used to design a rule, tune a threshold, or repair an implementation are development evidence. A later evaluation can reuse them for regression testing, but should not label that reuse an unseen assessment. Shared sources, repeated near-duplicates, and leaked reference annotations can make nominally separate cases dependent.</p>\n<p>For causal or practical-effect claims, the required design is stronger than a catalog of passing controls. The <a href=\"/en/science/knowledge-utility/\">knowledge utility paper</a> states the intervention and attribution conditions. Observed use, user approval, or a plausible demonstration alone does not establish that the system improved outcomes relative to a meaningful alternative.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-the-evidential-status-of-this-research-program\">7. The evidential status of this research program</h2>\n<p>The scientific results presented here are definitions, explicit conditional arguments, and countermodels. They specify what a compliant knowledge process would have to preserve. Published engineering studies provide the separate place for reproducible executions, actual observations, and their limits. A link to a study is not a declaration that every proposed experiment has been performed; the study’s own methods and evidence record determine its scope.</p>\n<p>This distinction allows a formal or methodological paper to have a complete argument while practical hypotheses remain open. Completion means that the paper states and supports its actual contribution, not that every imaginable deployment question has been settled. Conversely, removing a provisional label cannot substitute for missing evidence.</p>\n<p>The central falsifiable engineering prediction is conditional: if an implementation enforces a declared mandatory condition, cases that violate that condition should not receive the corresponding successful admission. Semantic and practical predictions require independently interpreted or measured outcomes in addition to structural controls.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/\">contract profile conformance study</a>, <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/\">source transcription and uncertainty study</a>, and <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/\">reuse admission and cost controls study</a> address distinct subjects. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">editorial skill pilot</a> addresses document production. Their results should remain separate until a justified analysis explains how they combine.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-scope-and-shared-definitions\">8. Scope and shared definitions</h2>\n<p>The model does not automate the discovery of every relevant premise, guarantee the trustworthiness of a reviewer, or establish universal usefulness of structured knowledge. It makes these dependencies inspectable and supplies conditions under which a particular claim can be rejected, retained, or narrowed.</p>\n<p>The shared <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a> defines evidence, verification, authority, and related terms. <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">Revision and governance</a> specifies how new evidence changes assessments without erasing their history.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Hoare, C. A. R. “An Axiomatic Basis for Computer Programming.” <em>Communications of the ACM</em> 12(10), 1969, 576–580, 583. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1145/363235.363259\" class=\"uri\">https://doi.org/10.1145/363235.363259</a></li>\n<li>W3C. <em>PROV-DM: The PROV Data Model</em>. W3C Recommendation, 2013. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/\" class=\"uri\">https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/</a></li>\n</ol>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"1-research-question-and-evidential-distinctions","title":"1. Research question and evidential distinctions"},{"level":2,"id":"2-claims-and-independent-assessment-axes","title":"2. Claims and independent assessment axes"},{"level":2,"id":"3-justification-graphs-and-conditional-soundness","title":"3. Justification graphs and conditional soundness"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-1-sound-rules-preserve-true-premises-in-a-finite-derivation","title":"Proposition 1. Sound rules preserve true premises in a finite derivation"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-2-a-countermodel-refutes-a-universal-claim-within-its-scope","title":"Proposition 2. A countermodel refutes a universal claim within its scope"},{"level":2,"id":"4-what-different-checks-establish","title":"4. What different checks establish"},{"level":2,"id":"5-a-falsification-protocol","title":"5. A falsification protocol"},{"level":2,"id":"6-comparators-analysis-and-stopping","title":"6. Comparators, analysis, and stopping"},{"level":2,"id":"7-the-evidential-status-of-this-research-program","title":"7. The evidential status of this research program"},{"level":2,"id":"8-scope-and-shared-definitions","title":"8. Scope and shared definitions"},{"level":2,"id":"references","title":"References"}],"title_id":"evidence-reasoning-and-falsification-in-knowledge-systems","math_count":6,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/papers/evidence-and-falsification/PAPER.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/papers/evidence-and-falsification/PAPER.md","source_sha256":"60bd9216daddf0dc69396acfe2da2e29cc046bdb807717847f13926e8ba398b4","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"evaluation"},{"canonical":true,"id":"revision-and-governance","kind":"paper","type":"paper","path":"papers/revision-and-governance/PAPER.md","route":"/en/science/revision-and-governance/","title":"Knowledge Revision, Version Identity, and the Adoption of Norms","titleRu":"Knowledge Revision, Version Identity, and the Adoption of Norms","summary":"Separate identities for content, assessment and normative adoption, with explicit revision and publication decisions.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>Knowledge can change because its content is corrected, its evidence is reassessed, its use conditions change, or a community adopts a different rule. These changes should not be represented by one undifferentiated status. This paper separates object identity, content revision, representation, assessment, and normative adoption. It defines revision and admission operations, establishes conditional properties of pinned references and dependency-based reassessment, and explains why the adoption of a norm does not establish the truth of the claims it contains. A central distinction is between reproducing a historical state and authorizing a present use. The contribution is an explicit account of what changes, what remains attributable, and which previous checks can legitimately transfer. The account is suitable for finite implementation and counterexample testing, but does not establish complete dependency discovery, authenticated authority, or a universally correct governance policy.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> knowledge revision; provenance; versioning; governance; normative adoption; historical reproducibility.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-research-question\">1. Research question</h2>\n<p>A knowledge repository often uses “current,” “approved,” or “valid” as if each described one property. In practice, a record can be the latest revision while being disputed, an approved policy can contain a factual error, and a historical version can remain necessary for reproducing an old result.</p>\n<p>Our question is: <strong>how can a system revise knowledge and adopt norms without confusing content, evidence, and authority?</strong> We propose distinct objects and transition rules for each. The purpose is not to freeze knowledge, but to preserve a clear explanation of why a particular revision or assessment was used at a particular time.</p>\n<p>PROV-DM offers the general distinction between entities, activities, and responsible agents [1]. We use that structure to connect revisions and decisions. The additional contribution is a set of obligations for transferring checks, following current versions, retaining historical grounds, and admitting normative decisions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-five-different-objects-of-change\">2. Five different objects of change</h2>\n<p>An <strong>identity</strong> groups revisions treated as versions of one object under a declared identity policy. The policy matters: a correction, a translation, a derived summary, and a competing interpretation need not all be revisions of the same object.</p>\n<p>A <strong>content revision</strong> is a pinned content state. Material content changes create a new revision with a relation to its predecessor. Immutability here is a property of the repository’s version discipline, not a guarantee supplied by writing a revision label.</p>\n<p>A <strong>representation</strong> is a delivery or serialization of content: a document rendering, machine format, or other declared form. Two representations may preserve selected content while differing in accessibility or layout. Equivalence requires an appropriate preservation check; a shared title is insufficient.</p>\n<p>An <strong>assessment</strong> concerns a proposition about a revision: whether it is supported, applicable, conflicted, current for a purpose, or compliant with a rule. New evidence can change an assessment without changing the assessed text. Overwriting the text to reflect every assessment change would confuse the object with judgments about it.</p>\n<p>A <strong>normative adoption</strong> is a decision to use a rule within a named community, process, or jurisdiction. It identifies the decision maker, authority basis, scope, effective period, adopted revision, and review or withdrawal conditions. Adoption makes the rule operative under that policy; it does not make every factual premise of the rule true.</p>\n<p>These distinctions permit a corrected revision and a withdrawn assessment to coexist with an earlier historical record. They also prevent unrelated properties—such as popularity, technical availability, or a successful build—from acting as substitutes for adoption or evidential support.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-revision-operations-and-their-grounds\">3. Revision operations and their grounds</h2>\n<p>A revision operation records the affected object, motivating evidence or request, proposed change, relevant dependencies, decision, and resulting revisions or assessments. A useful operation can correct a transcription, replace a false premise, narrow an applicability condition, add a competing interpretation, or change a normative decision.</p>\n<p>Not every conflict should be resolved by replacing one statement. The conflict may concern different dates, referents, scopes, or interpretations. Preserving those distinctions can dissolve an apparent contradiction without declaring either source universally correct. If the conflict remains unresolved, the system should retain that assessment and avoid presenting one branch as established merely because it is newer.</p>\n<p>Checks attach to a revision and an assessment scope. A successful check of an old text is not automatically a successful check of a materially changed text. Transfer requires a reason: the checked property is unaffected by the change, the transformation preserves it, or the check has been repeated. The reason should be inspectable rather than inferred from a similar filename.</p>\n<p>Duplicate detection is likewise scoped. Equivalent content can be consolidated while retaining multiple provenance relations. Merging identities without a justified identity policy can destroy distinctions between independent sources, conflicting readings, or separate normative authorities.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-conditional-properties\">4. Conditional properties</h2>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-1-pinned-references-preserve-historical-selection\">Proposition 1. Pinned references preserve historical selection</h3>\n<p>Suppose a reference names an immutable revision, and retrieval either returns that exact revision or reports failure. Changing the repository’s current-revision pointer does not change the content selected by that reference.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Selection depends on the named revision, not on the current pointer. The pointer change therefore cannot alter the selected identifier or its immutable content. ∎</p>\n<p>This property requires storage integrity and exact reference resolution. A readable label or hash field alone does not ensure either. It also guarantees selection, not perpetual availability, current applicability, or permission to disclose the historical content.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-2-checks-transfer-through-preserved-complete-inputs\">Proposition 2. Checks transfer through preserved complete inputs</h3>\n<p>Let a deterministic check <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math> depend on a complete input tuple <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d</annotation></semantics></math>. If two revisions and their evaluation contexts induce the same tuple, then the check returns the same result for both.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Determinism gives equal outputs for equal complete inputs. ∎</p>\n<p>The word “complete” is decisive. A check that depends on a policy version, current authorization, or a source interpretation cannot be transferred using only equality of visible text. If the evaluator itself changes, the equality-of-function assumption must also be reconsidered.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-3-material-changes-require-reassessment-along-complete-dependencies\">Proposition 3. Material changes require reassessment along complete dependencies</h3>\n<p>If every dependency relevant to an assessment is represented in a finite graph, complete reverse reachability from a changed dependency identifies all assessments potentially affected by that change.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> A relevant dependency gives a finite path from the assessment to the changed object. Reversal makes the assessment reachable from the change. Complete traversal includes every such path. ∎</p>\n<p>This result identifies candidates for reassessment, not automatic failures. An assessment may remain valid because an alternative ground still suffices. Conversely, a missing edge can hide an affected assessment despite a correct traversal algorithm.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-4-adoption-is-not-a-truth-preserving-inference-rule\">Proposition 4. Adoption is not a truth-preserving inference rule</h3>\n<p>Without an additional premise connecting an adoption procedure to truth, the fact that a rule is adopted does not logically entail the truth of its factual content.</p>\n<p><strong>Argument.</strong> Consider two interpretations with the same recorded adoption event, one in which the factual content is true and another in which it is false. Both satisfy the adoption record. The content therefore is not a consequence of that record alone. ∎</p>\n<p>This does not deny the practical force of adopted rules. It locates that force in the declared governance policy rather than in an unsupported epistemic inference.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-authority-and-normative-admission\">5. Authority and normative admission</h2>\n<p>A governance policy defines who may propose, review, adopt, amend, or withdraw a norm, under which conditions. Those are different permissions. A role description can explain responsibility without granting every operation associated with the role.</p>\n<p>Admission should evaluate current authority, the applicable procedure, the exact proposed revision, required reviews, unresolved conflicts, and any effective-date conditions. When a condition is mandatory, unknown is not pass. A record that says a reviewer approved something must be connected to the correct subject and a trustworthy attribution mechanism; a field named “reviewer” cannot authenticate itself.</p>\n<p>The model does not prescribe one decision rule for every community. A maintainer decision, a quorum, or an external mandate can each be represented, provided the rule and its scope are explicit. The research obligation is to state what the procedure establishes and what it leaves unproven.</p>\n<p>An appeal or correction creates a new event rather than erasing the earlier decision. This permits analysis of why an earlier output was produced and how the current basis differs. Accountability requires that history remain inspectable under the applicable retention and access rules; it does not require unrestricted public disclosure of every underlying record.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-historical-reproduction-and-current-use\">6. Historical reproduction and current use</h2>\n<p>Historical reproduction asks what followed from a pinned state and policy. Current use asks whether the result is admissible now. These can have different answers. A historical computation may remain reproducible even when its source has been superseded. A current recommendation may require reassessment despite exact reproduction of the earlier computation.</p>\n<p>Time therefore has several meanings: when an event occurred, when it was recorded, when a rule became effective, and when a use is being admitted. Collapsing them into one timestamp makes delayed information and retrospective correction difficult to represent correctly.</p>\n<p>Likewise, storage retention, publication status, and use eligibility are distinct. An artifact can remain archived while current use is withdrawn. Removing it from search results does not erase all copies. Retaining an original for reproducibility does not grant permission to use it in a new context. Each transition should identify its intended effect.</p>\n<p>Migration between representations introduces another preservation question. A migration report should state what is mapped, what is transformed with checked preservation, what is intentionally omitted, and what cannot be represented. A successful import is not evidence of semantic equivalence if critical distinctions were collapsed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-falsification-and-evidence-boundary\">7. Falsification and evidence boundary</h2>\n<p>The model can be tested with cases that change one material feature: a source revision, authority grant, effective date, interpretation rule, or mandatory dependency. It should also include unchanged content with a changed assessment, and changed layout with preserved content but impaired delivery.</p>\n<p>Counterexamples are particularly useful where identifiers conceal assumptions. Reusing a prior receipt after a policy change tests whether the receipt is wrongly treated as permanent permission. Updating a current pointer while resolving a pinned reference tests historical selection. Merging two textually equal records with different provenance tests whether deduplication destroys relevant history.</p>\n<p>These controls can establish behavior on finite represented cases. They do not prove complete dependency discovery, correct interpretation of all governance rules, or trustworthy authentication in an unexamined deployment. The formal propositions remain conditional on those boundaries.</p>\n<p>The contribution is a complete account of the distinctions needed to reason about revision and adoption, together with checkable consequences and counterexamples. It makes no empirical claim that one governance procedure is universally superior or that the model resolves substantive disagreement automatically.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work\">8. Connections to the published engineering work</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">executable projections and revision study</a> concerns pinned revisions and bounded update behavior. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/\">reuse admission and cost controls study</a> examines current eligibility of previously prepared artifacts.</p>\n<p>The shared <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>, <a href=\"/en/science/interaction-and-memory/\">interaction and memory</a>, and <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">controlled knowledge reuse</a> define the connected work-state and admission obligations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>W3C. <em>PROV-DM: The PROV Data Model</em>. W3C Recommendation, 2013. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/\" class=\"uri\">https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/</a></li>\n</ol>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"1-research-question","title":"1. Research question"},{"level":2,"id":"2-five-different-objects-of-change","title":"2. Five different objects of change"},{"level":2,"id":"3-revision-operations-and-their-grounds","title":"3. Revision operations and their grounds"},{"level":2,"id":"4-conditional-properties","title":"4. Conditional properties"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-1-pinned-references-preserve-historical-selection","title":"Proposition 1. Pinned references preserve historical selection"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-2-checks-transfer-through-preserved-complete-inputs","title":"Proposition 2. Checks transfer through preserved complete inputs"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-3-material-changes-require-reassessment-along-complete-dependencies","title":"Proposition 3. Material changes require reassessment along complete dependencies"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-4-adoption-is-not-a-truth-preserving-inference-rule","title":"Proposition 4. Adoption is not a truth-preserving inference rule"},{"level":2,"id":"5-authority-and-normative-admission","title":"5. Authority and normative admission"},{"level":2,"id":"6-historical-reproduction-and-current-use","title":"6. Historical reproduction and current use"},{"level":2,"id":"7-falsification-and-evidence-boundary","title":"7. Falsification and evidence boundary"},{"level":2,"id":"8-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work","title":"8. Connections to the published engineering work"},{"level":2,"id":"references","title":"References"}],"title_id":"knowledge-revision-version-identity-and-the-adoption-of-norms","math_count":2,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/papers/revision-and-governance/PAPER.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/papers/revision-and-governance/PAPER.md","source_sha256":"0e348ea0c656509cc24de82d444175fa7366e9edacb70e2a4a706672b5e61154","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"systems"},{"canonical":true,"id":"controlled-knowledge-reuse","kind":"paper","type":"paper","path":"papers/controlled-knowledge-reuse/PAPER.md","route":"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/","title":"Knowledge Contracts and the Admission of Prepared Knowledge","titleRu":"Knowledge Contracts and the Admission of Prepared Knowledge","summary":"The conditions under which saved knowledge may support a new task, recipient or execution.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>Preparing knowledge in advance can reduce repeated work, but a previously acceptable artifact is not automatically acceptable for a new task. This paper defines a knowledge contract as an explicit account of requirements, constraints, grounds, permitted transformations, and verification obligations. It distinguishes that contract from its professional specializations and from a prepared artifact produced under it. Reuse is modeled as a directed admission relation, evaluated for a particular request and state. We prove a conditional preservation result requiring complete material dependencies, equivalent conditions, and a property-preserving transformation. Counterexamples cover changed permissions, incomplete dependencies, negative search results, and races between admission and delivery. The contribution is a precise boundary between reusable preparation and current authorization, including cases where rebuilding or rejection is required. The argument does not establish automatic discovery of every semantic dependency, industrial coordination guarantees, or a universal economic advantage from caching.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> knowledge contract; compilation; reuse admission; constraints; dependencies; current authorization.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-research-question-and-relation-to-caching\">1. Research question and relation to caching</h2>\n<p>A system may prepare a summary, context package, workflow, or professional instruction once and use it repeatedly. Repetition saves work only if the prepared result remains suitable. Identical request text can hide a changed recipient, policy, source revision, or time interpretation.</p>\n<p>We ask: <strong>when does an earlier verification justify a new use of a prepared artifact?</strong> The problem resembles caching, but the cached object carries semantic and normative obligations as well as bytes. HTTP caching provides explicit rules for freshness and reuse [1], and extensions describe limited stale-serving behavior [2]. Those protocols are relevant comparisons; their guarantees do not automatically apply to knowledge interpretation or authorization.</p>\n<p>The contribution is a contract model that makes the extra admission obligations explicit. It treats reuse as a decision under conditions rather than a consequence of similarity, popularity, or storage location.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-contracts-and-their-professional-specialization\">2. Contracts and their professional specialization</h2>\n<p>A <strong>knowledge contract</strong> specifies a bounded use: its objective, requirements, mandatory constraints, admissible grounds, permitted transformations, expected result, and verification or stopping conditions. Its content can be represented in different formats. The scientific concept does not depend on one schema version or transport envelope.</p>\n<p>Constraints are first-class obligations. They can limit scope, resources, time, recipient, authority, interpretation, or allowed operation. A preference can guide selection among admissible options. A mandatory constraint cannot be compensated by a high utility score. If a mandatory condition is unknown, the corresponding admission has not passed.</p>\n<p>The contract does not itself establish truth, grant authority, or supply a missing capability. It states what must be supported and checked. A structurally valid contract can still contain a false premise or an unimplementable demand.</p>\n<p>In a professional profile, a <strong>role</strong> specifies expected responsibilities; a <strong>competency</strong> specifies an ability under stated conditions, while a competency assessment binds supporting evidence to a particular executor; a <strong>skill</strong> is a reusable procedure with conditions, steps, and checks; and a <strong>blueprint</strong> organizes roles, procedures, and decision points for a class of work. These are the project’s operational definitions, not a claim of one universal external taxonomy.</p>\n<p>A <strong>Work Item</strong> instantiates an objective with actual requirements and outputs. A <strong>capability</strong> concerns an available operation. <strong>Authorization</strong> concerns permission to perform it in the current context. A role can be responsible for an outcome without possessing every capability or permission needed to produce it. A skill description does not establish that an executor meets a competency requirement, and a successful attempt does not establish that ability across every task in the described class.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-preparation-and-the-reuse-relation\">3. Preparation and the reuse relation</h2>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math> include the task, requirements, referents, recipient, isolation scope, resource budget, and time interpretation. Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a pinned source state and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math> a preparation recipe. A preparation attempt produces, when successful,</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>Compile</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\operatorname{Compile}(b,q,S)\\to(a,D,E,L),\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math> is an artifact revision, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math> its declared dependencies, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math> its verification evidence, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math> its coverage, losses, and unresolved conditions. Preparation can fail or produce an explicitly partial result. A content hash checks bytes under a declared serialization; it does not establish truth or deterministic regeneration.</p>\n<p>For a new request <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q&#39;</annotation></semantics></math>, policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math>, and current state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math>, write</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mo>⇝</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\na\\rightsquigarrow_{p,s}q&#39;\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>when the artifact is admitted for that use. This relation is directed. A complete artifact may support an authorized narrower projection; a partial artifact does not support a broader task merely because the same topic is mentioned.</p>\n<p>Possible decisions include reuse unchanged, instantiate with checking, revalidate, rebuild, bypass reuse, and reject the requested use. Revalidation is an outstanding obligation, not permission to deliver before the check. An instantiated result has its own content identity and actual-output checks.</p>\n<p>Build dependencies and admission dependencies differ. A generator version can matter for provenance or a known defect; current permission can matter at every delivery. Neither invalidating everything after every tool update nor ignoring all interpretation changes is a justified general policy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-conditional-preservation-theorem\">4. Conditional preservation theorem</h2>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_1,\\ldots,P_m</annotation></semantics></math> be the properties previously verified for artifact <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math> in state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_0</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math> contains every state input material to these properties and their interpretation.</li>\n<li>The new state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_1</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_0</annotation></semantics></math> on the complete observations of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math> relevant to each property.</li>\n<li>A permitted transformation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math>, including the identity transformation, preserves each <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_j</annotation></semantics></math> in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_1</annotation></semantics></math>.</li>\n<li>Independent current admission conditions, including authorization and delivery requirements, pass for the new use.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Then the transformed artifact retains the verified properties for the declared new use, and the stated admission conditions hold.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> For each <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math>, completeness of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math> and equality of its relevant observations imply that changing from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_1</annotation></semantics></math> does not change <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_j(a,\\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_j(a,s_1)</annotation></semantics></math> holds. Property preservation gives <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_j(T(a),s_1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The remaining admission conditions hold by the fourth assumption. This establishes the finite selected set, not additional unverified properties. ∎</p>\n<p>The theorem is deliberately conditional. A manifest labeled “complete” does not prove its completeness. Natural-language interpretation, hidden dependencies, and current authority need their own justification. The theorem also does not make a false premise true: preservation can maintain a faithful record of a mistaken source.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-dependencies-and-scope-counterexamples\">5. Dependencies and scope counterexamples</h2>\n<p>Dependencies can concern content fields, relations, alternative grounds, interpretation rules, normative bases, representations, and search scope. Fine granularity can avoid unnecessary rebuilding only when it preserves completeness for the selected properties.</p>\n<p>Negative, exhaustive, counted, and ranked answers expose a common failure. Suppose an artifact states that no record satisfies a predicate. Recording only previously returned record identifiers is insufficient: a new matching record changes the answer without modifying any earlier result. The relevant dependency includes the searched scope, visibility, predicate, and any completeness assumptions. Exact top-ranked results additionally depend on ranking and tie rules. Approximate search does not become exhaustive because its output is cached.</p>\n<p>Multimodal dependencies can concern an image revision, region, legend, or transformation. Correcting a symbol can invalidate an unchanged textual interpretation. Moving identical bytes may require a locator or delivery check without changing semantic content. These cases require different responses.</p>\n<p>Alternative grounds also matter. Losing one source need not invalidate a conclusion if an independently sufficient permitted ground remains. A dependency traversal can identify affected artifacts, but deciding whether they remain supported requires the declared support rule and current assessments.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-state-time-and-delivery\">6. State, time, and delivery</h2>\n<p>Lifecycle, residency, freshness, and reuse eligibility are separate dimensions. An artifact can be ready and resident but unauthorized for a particular recipient. Eviction can change latency without changing its content. A recent cache hit is evidence of use, not of renewed validation.</p>\n<p>Time policies must be explicit. <strong>Strict current use</strong> requires the relevant current conditions at a declared admission point. <strong>Historical pinned use</strong> preserves a past state for the requested purpose while still respecting present access and revocation conditions. <strong>Bounded stale use</strong> permits specified age or divergence only where the contract allows it. Known revocation, corruption, or incompatibility is not repaired by an unexpired time-to-live.</p>\n<p>Checking immediately before delivery is insufficient for a strong current-state guarantee if a material condition can change between the check and the protected operation. For example, a permission can pass, then be withdrawn, then the artifact can be sent. Every individual step can be implemented as described while the desired current-admission claim fails.</p>\n<p>A stronger guarantee requires a declared admission point and a coordination mechanism that binds the relevant evaluation to that point: a coherent snapshot, suitable lock, valid lease, or equivalent protocol under stated assumptions. The guarantee concerns that point and scope; it does not promise that the world will remain unchanged afterward.</p>\n<p>Publication has a related race. A slow preparation attempt must not replace a newer artifact merely because it finishes later. Generation checks and protected publication can prevent that overwrite under their storage assumptions. Repeated or reordered invalidation events can be handled idempotently, but lost events require reconciliation or independent validation. Event deduplication alone does not recover missing information.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-economic-and-practical-limits\">7. Economic and practical limits</h2>\n<p>Admission first determines which routes are permitted. Cost or predicted utility can then rank those routes. A cheap inadmissible result is not a valid optimization candidate. Reuse may reduce repeated preparation while adding storage, validation, invalidation, reconstruction, and coordination costs.</p>\n<p>Comparisons should include a valid cold path and account for the same required quality and authority conditions. Provider-level caching, retrieval caching, prepared context, and professional artifacts are different units of reuse. Savings attributed to the same avoided operation must not be counted repeatedly across layers.</p>\n<p>The present result is the conditional admission argument and its counterexamples. It establishes no universal speedup, cost reduction, or production concurrency guarantee. Those are workload- and implementation-specific claims that require their own measurements and failure models. Repeated successful reuse does not by itself raise the truth or completeness status of the underlying knowledge.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work\">8. Connections to the published engineering work</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/\">reuse admission and cost controls study</a> examines finite admission decisions and accounting controls. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/\">contract profile conformance study</a> addresses supported representations. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">dependency closure and package delivery study</a> addresses closure and actual delivery.</p>\n<p>Machine interfaces and their compatibility versions belong in the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/\">engineering core specification</a>. The shared <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>, <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">constrained transformations</a>, and <a href=\"/en/science/knowledge-utility/\">knowledge utility</a> complete the conceptual links.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Fielding, R., Nottingham, M., and Reschke, J. <em>HTTP Caching</em>. RFC 9111, 2022. <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9111\" class=\"uri\">https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9111</a></li>\n<li>Nottingham, M. <em>HTTP Cache-Control Extensions for Stale Content</em>. RFC 5861, 2010. <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5861\" class=\"uri\">https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5861</a></li>\n</ol>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"1-research-question-and-relation-to-caching","title":"1. Research question and relation to caching"},{"level":2,"id":"2-contracts-and-their-professional-specialization","title":"2. Contracts and their professional specialization"},{"level":2,"id":"3-preparation-and-the-reuse-relation","title":"3. Preparation and the reuse relation"},{"level":2,"id":"4-conditional-preservation-theorem","title":"4. Conditional preservation theorem"},{"level":2,"id":"5-dependencies-and-scope-counterexamples","title":"5. Dependencies and scope counterexamples"},{"level":2,"id":"6-state-time-and-delivery","title":"6. State, time, and delivery"},{"level":2,"id":"7-economic-and-practical-limits","title":"7. Economic and practical limits"},{"level":2,"id":"8-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work","title":"8. Connections to the published engineering work"},{"level":2,"id":"references","title":"References"}],"title_id":"knowledge-contracts-and-the-admission-of-prepared-knowledge","math_count":29,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/papers/controlled-knowledge-reuse/PAPER.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/papers/controlled-knowledge-reuse/PAPER.md","source_sha256":"9c799b25dbc7c7336e173f31a4877034fd05713151da8c380519f5884fa1370c","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"evaluation"},{"canonical":true,"id":"knowledge-utility","kind":"paper","type":"paper","path":"papers/knowledge-utility/PAPER.md","route":"/en/science/knowledge-utility/","title":"Knowledge Utility, Resource Accounting, and Retention Decisions","titleRu":"Knowledge Utility, Resource Accounting, and Retention Decisions","summary":"Task-relative benefit, complete resource accounting, comparison baselines and limits of causal inference.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>Knowledge is useful relative to a task, recipient, policy, time horizon, and alternative. Truth, evidence quality, popularity, and utility therefore cannot be represented as one interchangeable score. This paper defines conditional utility and separates measured outcomes, predicted benefit, resource costs, and causal attribution. It gives conservation conditions for shared-cost allocation, shows why complementary contributions are generally nonadditive, and derives a restricted break-even condition for prepared knowledge. Retention decisions are framed in terms of future marginal value subject to mandatory constraints, rather than historical acquisition cost or raw use frequency. The contribution is an accounting and evaluation model that prevents common forms of double counting and unsupported attribution. It does not supply universal weights, prices, retention thresholds, or empirical claims that structured knowledge is always beneficial. Those quantities require explicit measurement under a stated intervention and comparison policy.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> knowledge utility; causal attribution; resource accounting; marginal value; retention; reuse economics.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-research-question\">1. Research question</h2>\n<p>A fact can be true and irrelevant to a task. A false claim can be useful as an object of investigation without becoming true. An expensive artifact can be worth retaining, while a frequently retrieved one can add little to outcomes. These cases show why “knowledge value” is not a permanent intrinsic score.</p>\n<p>We ask: <strong>how should a system assess the contribution and cost of knowledge without confusing truth, exposure, attribution, and usefulness?</strong> The model below treats value as conditional and preserves the units and assumptions of each assessment.</p>\n<p>The causal distinction is central. An observed association between access to an artifact and a good outcome does not alone identify the artifact’s effect. Causal inference requires assumptions connecting observed data to the intervention being assessed [1]. Our contribution is to carry that discipline into knowledge selection, prepared-artifact reuse, and retention accounting.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-conditional-utility-and-outcome-vectors\">2. Conditional utility and outcome vectors</h2>\n<p>For knowledge artifact <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math>, write</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nV(k\\mid q,c,\\pi,h,b,r),\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math> is the task or task distribution, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c</annotation></semantics></math> the consumer and context, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math> the use and evaluation policy, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math> the observation horizon, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math> the baseline, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math> the pinned artifact and policy revisions. These conditions are part of the claim. Changing them can change the value being estimated.</p>\n<p>An outcome can be a vector: task quality, consumer effort, latency, uncertainty reduction, prevented harm, introduced harm, and future usefulness. Each component needs a definition, unit, observation method, and treatment of missing data. Predicted benefit, measured outcome, and unresolved assessment must remain distinct. Unknown is not zero.</p>\n<p>A scalar score is legitimate only with an explicit mapping from the vector. That mapping includes weights, trade-offs, and any monetary conversions. Different stakeholders can legitimately choose different mappings. Where no justified common scale exists, a partial comparison such as Pareto dominance is more informative than an unexplained total ranking.</p>\n<p>Costs must be included once. One can define a net utility that already contains resource penalties, or subtract a separate cost measure from a gross benefit measure. Subtracting the same cost again produces an accounting error. A new weight or tariff policy creates a new assessment, rather than silently rewriting the meaning of an earlier score.</p>\n<p>Truth, evidential support, source quality, applicability, and utility remain separate axes. Utility does not authorize a false factual claim, and low utility does not refute a proposition. These distinctions permit honest evaluation of investigation, correction, and rarely needed knowledge.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-resource-accounting\">3. Resource accounting</h2>\n<p>A resource ledger records the operations that actually consume resources. Depending on the system, these can include input and output tokens, processor time, elapsed time, human effort, storage over time, network transfer, index updates, checks, retries, and reconstruction after eviction. The measurement unit and scope must be explicit.</p>\n<p>Elapsed latency is not generally the sum of parallel operation durations. Logical artifacts are not physical replicas. A node count is not a monetary cost without a model connecting it to resources. Monetary amounts require a tariff revision, currency, date, and allocation policy. Billed, estimated, and predicted costs should not be merged into one unlabeled total.</p>\n<p>A retry can incur real additional cost; a duplicated telemetry event does not. Stable event identity with payload-conflict handling separates these cases. Tokens, their priced estimate, and the corresponding invoice line are different views of a cost, not three independent charges.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-1-conservative-shared-cost-allocation-prevents-creation-of-cost\">Proposition 1. Conservative shared-cost allocation prevents creation of cost</h3>\n<p>For one measured operation with cost <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c</annotation></semantics></math>, let allocations <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_1,\\ldots,a_n</annotation></semantics></math> and an unallocated residual <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i+u=c.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>If every underlying operation is included exactly once and all allocated and residual parts are included exactly once, summing the parts reproduces the total measured cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Summing the stated equality over operations gives equality between the total allocations plus residuals and the total operation costs. ∎</p>\n<p>The result is an accounting identity, not a uniquely fair allocation rule. It requires a closed accounting scope. Omitting shared infrastructure or counting the same event in two ledgers defeats the premises.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-contribution-and-causal-attribution\">4. Contribution and causal attribution</h2>\n<p>Events such as found, selected, delivered, consumed, cited, and accepted describe different stages. A citation does not show that the cited artifact caused the outcome. Acceptance of a Work Item does not establish the separate usefulness of every attached source.</p>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_w(\\pi+k)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the outcome for task <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math> under a policy that offers artifact <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_w(\\pi_b)</annotation></semantics></math> the outcome under a declared baseline. A contribution target can be written</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\Delta V(k)=\\mathbb E_{w\\sim D}\n\\left[U(Y_w(\\pi+k))-U(Y_w(\\pi_b))\\right].\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>To attribute this difference specifically to access to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the policies must differ only in that intervention and its predeclared consequences. If the new condition also changes the model, review budget, interface, or task selection, the contrast concerns the whole policy change unless additional identification is justified.</p>\n<p>The expression defines a target; it does not identify it from arbitrary logs. Randomized assignment, paired task designs, or observational adjustment each require their own assumptions about assignment, interference, support, measurement, and the relevant population. Unrecorded selection effects can make a frequently used artifact appear beneficial because it is offered on easier tasks.</p>\n<p>Observation, association, prediction, and causal estimate must therefore be labeled separately. A model’s predicted gain can guide exploration but cannot be reported as an observed effect. The observation horizon also matters: immediate completion, delayed retention, and later reuse are different outcomes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-complementarity-duplication-and-exposure\">5. Complementarity, duplication, and exposure</h2>\n<p>Utility of a set <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math> need not equal the sum of its members’ utility. Consider a rule and its necessary condition. Neither alone is useful for the permitted task, but the pair is. If</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⌀</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nV(\\varnothing)=V(\\{a\\})=V(\\{b\\})=0,\n\\qquad V(\\{a,b\\})=1,\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>then each leave-one-out contribution within the pair is one. Summing those contributions yields two, although the total gain is one. Marginal contribution therefore depends on the reference set, and summing several margins can double-count complementary value.</p>\n<p>Redundant copies create a different problem. Splitting one informational artifact into multiple identifiers should not automatically create multiple independent contributions. Attribution requires a declared identity and coalition policy. An allocation rule can distribute a defined total; it cannot discover unobserved outcomes or establish a uniquely fair reward by itself.</p>\n<p>Use frequency needs exposure and opportunity denominators. Low use can reflect narrow applicability, limited demand, poor retrieval, restricted access, or an immature observation period. If a ranking system suppresses artifacts with few uses, new artifacts may receive no opportunity to demonstrate value. This is a possible feedback mechanism, not proof that every ranking system exhibits it.</p>\n<p>Evaluation can address the problem through a declared exploration budget, independent task samples, and separate reporting for new and mature artifacts. Such choices are policy decisions with costs and assumptions, not universal constants.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-reuse-economics-and-retention\">6. Reuse economics and retention</h2>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-2-a-simple-reuse-policy-has-a-conditional-break-even-point\">Proposition 2. A simple reuse policy has a conditional break-even point</h3>\n<p>Suppose a valid cold execution costs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m</annotation></semantics></math> per request. Preparing an equally acceptable reusable artifact costs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math> once, and each admitted reuse costs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math>, including its required checks. With <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math> requests, no intervening invalidation, and no other costs, reuse is cheaper exactly when</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">equivalently</mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nB+nh&lt;nm,\n\\quad\\text{equivalently}\\quad\nn(m-h)&gt;B.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Compare the total costs and rearrange the inequality. If <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\leq h</annotation></semantics></math>, a positive preparation cost cannot be recovered under these assumptions. ∎</p>\n<p>This result is deliberately narrow. Storage, invalidation, rebuilding, coordination, failed attempts, and changing demand can alter the calculation. The two routes must satisfy the same relevant acceptance conditions; omitting required checks is not an economic improvement under that contract.</p>\n<p>Retention concerns future choices. Past acquisition cost belongs in the historical ledger, but the next decision should compare future marginal benefits, future costs, switching costs, and mandatory constraints. A large historical expenditure does not by itself justify continued expensive residency.</p>\n<p>Retention is also not one binary action. Archiving, removing from search, stopping background preparation, withdrawing current eligibility, and physically deleting content have different consequences. A rarely used artifact may be necessary for reproducibility or a critical deadline. A negative utility estimate does not alone authorize destroying the only retained original.</p>\n<p>Residency policies can use measured demand and rehydration cost, with hysteresis to avoid repeated switching. Thresholds must be calibrated to the workload and constraints. Popularity cannot override revocation, known corruption, or a mandatory scope restriction. The <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">controlled reuse paper</a> defines admission before economic ranking.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-evaluation-and-falsification\">7. Evaluation and falsification</h2>\n<p>A utility study should predeclare the task distribution, baseline, intervention, outcome rubric, horizon, resource scope, and treatment of unknown outcomes. It should record opportunity to use the knowledge, actual exposure, and downstream consumption separately. An ablation must remove the intended contribution without silently changing unrelated parts of the system.</p>\n<p>Useful negative controls include repeated telemetry with one real operation, complementary artifacts whose summed margins overstate total gain, identical knowledge under multiple identifiers, and a policy change bundled with artifact access. Each reveals a specific attribution or accounting error.</p>\n<p>For retention, evaluation should include rare mandatory tasks, bursts of demand, changes of source state, and reconstruction after eviction. Results should state whether costs are observed or modeled and whether the policy was tuned on the same workload. A successful finite control does not establish optimal retention for every future demand pattern.</p>\n<p>The results of this paper are a conditional evaluation model, conservation and break-even properties, and counterexamples to additive or popularity-based shortcuts. No universal utility score or measured economic superiority is asserted. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/\">reuse admission and cost controls study</a> is the place for executable accounting and measured workloads; the <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">editorial skill pilot</a> supplies a separate document-production setting.</p>\n<p>The shared <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a> and <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">evidence and falsification paper</a> define the corresponding claim and measurement boundaries.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Pearl, J. “Causal Inference in Statistics: An Overview.” <em>Statistics Surveys</em> 3, 2009, 96–146. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1214/09-SS057\" class=\"uri\">https://doi.org/10.1214/09-SS057</a></li>\n</ol>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"1-research-question","title":"1. Research question"},{"level":2,"id":"2-conditional-utility-and-outcome-vectors","title":"2. Conditional utility and outcome vectors"},{"level":2,"id":"3-resource-accounting","title":"3. Resource accounting"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-1-conservative-shared-cost-allocation-prevents-creation-of-cost","title":"Proposition 1. Conservative shared-cost allocation prevents creation of cost"},{"level":2,"id":"4-contribution-and-causal-attribution","title":"4. Contribution and causal attribution"},{"level":2,"id":"5-complementarity-duplication-and-exposure","title":"5. Complementarity, duplication, and exposure"},{"level":2,"id":"6-reuse-economics-and-retention","title":"6. Reuse economics and retention"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-2-a-simple-reuse-policy-has-a-conditional-break-even-point","title":"Proposition 2. A simple reuse policy has a conditional break-even point"},{"level":2,"id":"7-evaluation-and-falsification","title":"7. Evaluation and falsification"},{"level":2,"id":"references","title":"References"}],"title_id":"knowledge-utility-resource-accounting-and-retention-decisions","math_count":26,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/papers/knowledge-utility/PAPER.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/papers/knowledge-utility/PAPER.md","source_sha256":"01f037b2266a8bbd85d0f69ed8e16a00fd4ebdd7e7f663b35949e006343ea383","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"evaluation"},{"canonical":true,"id":"knowledge-contract-core","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/","title":"Knowledge Contract: Core Model and Admission Requirements","titleRu":"Knowledge Contract: Core Model and Admission Requirements","summary":"The nine core groups, first-class constraints, profiles, admission and execution requirements.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>A Knowledge Contract binds a body of knowledge to its intended use, grounds, dependencies, constraints, and assessment. It lets a recipient inspect what is offered, which conditions matter, and what has actually been checked. It does not turn a source assertion into a fact or an instruction into permission to act.</p>\n<p>This specification defines the architecture of a conforming implementation. Capitalized requirement words follow <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>. Conformance MUST identify the implemented profile, its version, the reader, and the assessed scope. A claim of full conformance cannot rest on a schema check alone. Publication edition 1.0 is the edition of this document; serialized profiles retain their own versions. The <a href=\"/en/appendices/source-map/#protocol-compatibility\">compatibility record</a> identifies the existing bounded implementation without renaming it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-core-model\">2. Core model</h2>\n<p>The contract has nine logical groups. A serializer MAY organize them differently if its mapping preserves their obligations.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Group</th>\n<th>Required meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Identity</td>\n<td>Stable object identity, immutable revision, contract definition and representation profile versions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Purpose</td>\n<td>Intended use, task and requirements revision, recipient, required profiles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Content</td>\n<td>Addressable units, their semantic roles, source references and protected conditions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Scope</td>\n<td>Conditions of applicability and explicit unknown, inapplicable or withheld values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Grounds</td>\n<td>The source revision, locator, derivation and evidence supporting each relevant claim</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Dependencies</td>\n<td>Required relationships distinguished from optional reading links</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Obligations</td>\n<td>Preconditions, invariants, preservation duties, coverage requirements and acceptance obligations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Projection</td>\n<td>Delivered representation, included and omitted units, declared losses and transformations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Observations</td>\n<td>Assessments bound to a subject revision, method, observer, time and limitations</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>An empty list MUST mean an established empty set within a declared scope. It MUST NOT stand for an unknown set. A profile MAY represent a genuinely irrelevant group as not applicable with a reason. It MUST NOT silently omit a required obligation because no evidence is available.</p>\n<p>The contract’s immutable content and the current decision to use it are distinct records. Revoking a grant changes admissibility for a use; it does not rewrite what the earlier contract said.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-constraints-are-first-class-obligations\">3. Constraints are first-class obligations</h2>\n<p>A <strong>constraint</strong> restricts an admissible interpretation, transformation, delivery or action. Each constraint MUST have an identity, subject, scope, predicate or human-readable rule, source of authority when relevant, severity, and a checking method or an explicit unresolved status. An implementation MUST retain its applicability conditions and dependencies. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/#4-constraints-and-professional-artifacts\">artifact registry</a> defines the readable <code>CONSTRAINT.md</code> artifact.</p>\n<p>Four distinctions are mandatory:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Example of an abstract rule</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Applicability condition</td>\n<td>Under what circumstances does the knowledge apply?</td>\n<td>The input belongs to the stated domain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Action constraint</td>\n<td>What conduct is forbidden or bounded?</td>\n<td>The operation may write only the named resource</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Capability requirement</td>\n<td>Can this executor perform the required method?</td>\n<td>An assessment covers this skill and environment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Acceptance criterion</td>\n<td>What demonstrates the requested result?</td>\n<td>The delivered output satisfies the pinned criterion</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A constraint can also be an acceptance obligation, but the relationship MUST be explicit. Success at an output criterion does not excuse a prohibited action used to obtain it. A skill description does not establish capability. A role assignment does not by itself supply a grant.</p>\n<p>When profiles compose, all applicable mandatory constraints MUST remain in force unless an identified authority has explicitly resolved a conflict within its authority. Load order, a later paragraph, or a more convenient result MUST NOT implicitly override a restriction. A contradiction blocks the affected use or produces a narrowed, explicitly permitted alternative.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-profiles-and-compatibility\">4. Profiles and compatibility</h2>\n<p>The supported profile families are professional work, dialogue, documents, reasoning, visual representations, and reuse. Each profile declares its version, why it is present, whether it is required, and its payload. The need for a profile follows from the declared purpose and requirements; a parser is not assumed to infer it correctly from arbitrary natural language.</p>\n<p>A reader MUST advertise supported representations and required extensions. An unknown required profile makes the contract unusable for that request. An unknown optional profile MAY be retained as opaque data, with the limitation reported. Preserving its bytes does not verify its semantics. Compatibility is directional: a new reader accepting an old record does not prove that an old reader can safely rewrite the new record.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-admission-and-execution\">5. Admission and execution</h2>\n<p>Admission MUST evaluate the exact contract revision, requirements, recipient, environment, applicable constraints, mandatory dependencies, and current authority. Each mandatory check yields pass, fail, unknown, or justified not applicable. Unknown MUST NOT be promoted to pass.</p>\n<p>An execution additionally binds the Work Item revision, requirements revision, contract revision or digest, attempt, executor, relevant grants and capabilities, output identity, and criterion-specific evidence. Receipt of a plan is not execution. A successful process exit is not acceptance. If a remote effect is uncertain after a timeout, the attempt remains unresolved until reconciliation; a retry requires an explicit repeat policy.</p>\n<p>A conforming execution adapter MUST validate evidence provenance at its declared trust boundary. A boolean supplied by an untrusted caller saying that evidence is trusted is insufficient. The existing finite model assumes a supplied context and does not provide that production trust boundary; this limitation is measured in the linked study.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-migration-and-failure-handling\">6. Migration and failure handling</h2>\n<p>Migration creates a new representation and retains the source revision and transformation record. Missing conditions, grants or capabilities remain unknown. A free-text blueprint is not automatically converted into a process graph. Unmapped fields and semantic losses MUST be reported. Schema validity alone cannot justify <code>semantic_equivalence</code>.</p>\n<p>Refusal reports SHOULD identify the failed obligation and an actionable reason without exposing protected grounds to an unauthorized recipient. An implementation MUST NOT create a success-shaped output when assembly, authorization, or evidence checking fails.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-acceptance-cases-and-evidence\">7. Acceptance cases and evidence</h2>\n<p>A conformance suite MUST include: a supported positive request; a missing required dependency; an unknown required profile; a conflicting constraint; an outdated requirement revision; an unauthorized executor; mismatched output evidence; a migration with unknown values; and a false claim of trusted evidence. Schema, internal consistency, applicability, and execution acceptance MUST be reported separately.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/\">contract-profile study</a> reports 35/35 and 26/26 bounded scenario results, alongside false trusted-input acceptances and a 9/9 versus 9/9 baseline tie. These observations support finite conformance claims and expose the trust-boundary gap. They do not establish general task superiority or production authorization.</p>\n<p>The scientific justification is developed in <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">controlled knowledge reuse</a>, <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">evidence and falsification</a>, and the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">shared glossary</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-core-model","title":"2. Core model"},{"level":2,"id":"3-constraints-are-first-class-obligations","title":"3. Constraints are first-class obligations"},{"level":2,"id":"4-profiles-and-compatibility","title":"4. Profiles and compatibility"},{"level":2,"id":"5-admission-and-execution","title":"5. Admission and execution"},{"level":2,"id":"6-migration-and-failure-handling","title":"6. Migration and failure handling"},{"level":2,"id":"7-acceptance-cases-and-evidence","title":"7. Acceptance cases and evidence"}],"title_id":"knowledge-contract-core-model-and-admission-requirements","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"3c6db41d46b0914441af90fb8984aae8f8da8b2a056e1904daf16f4f230d037b","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"contract-family","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/contract-family/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/contract-family/","title":"Contracts for Knowledge Operations","titleRu":"Contracts for Knowledge Operations","summary":"Common preconditions, invariants, postconditions, effects and evidence for knowledge operations.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>An operation contract states when an operation may run, what it preserves or changes, which outcomes are permitted, and what evidence supports the reported outcome. Its promise is local to a declared input, requirements revision and environment. Completing one operation does not necessarily satisfy the entire Work Item.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>. This document specifies operation semantics; a conforming adapter MUST also identify its representation profile and executable checks. The <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">transformation paper</a> supplies the conditional formal model.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-common-contract-record\">2. Common contract record</h2>\n<p>Every operation definition MUST identify the following:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Field group</th>\n<th>Required content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Definition</td>\n<td>Operation name, immutable definition version and supported input/output profiles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Purpose</td>\n<td>The local goal and its relationship to a Work Item and requirements revision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Inputs</td>\n<td>Exact artifact revisions, cardinalities, relevant environment and missing-input behavior</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Preconditions</td>\n<td>Conditions that authorize and enable the attempt; checking methods and trust boundaries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Invariants</td>\n<td>Properties preserved throughout the operation’s declared observable scope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Postconditions</td>\n<td>Conditions attached to each allowed outcome, including partial and refused outcomes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Effects</td>\n<td>Read/write scope, external effects, repeat policy, reconciliation and compensation behavior</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Resources</td>\n<td>Budget, deadline, maximum attempts and stop rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Evidence</td>\n<td>Subject bindings, observation method and the limits of the evaluator</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Preconditions, invariants and postconditions MUST remain separate. A later successful observation does not establish that an invariant held at every earlier step. Evidence of an output’s shape does not establish its semantic correctness.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-operation-families\">3. Operation families</h2>\n<p>The following functional families organize implementations. They are responsibilities, not a requirement to deploy separate services.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Family</th>\n<th>Preservation obligation</th>\n<th>Principal failure mode</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Acquire and address</td>\n<td>Source identity, version, locator and acquisition provenance</td>\n<td>An unavailable or substituted source is treated as observed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Interpret and extract</td>\n<td>Attribution, negation, quantifiers, exceptions and unresolved readings</td>\n<td>A source claim becomes an unqualified fact</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Search and compare</td>\n<td>Query semantics, scope, candidate provenance and comparison criterion</td>\n<td>Similarity becomes equivalence or completeness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Select and compose</td>\n<td>Mandatory dependency closure, joint identity, coverage and total budget</td>\n<td>Necessary conditions disappear during shortening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Transform and render</td>\n<td>Declared protected observations, representation requirements and losses</td>\n<td>A readable description is substituted for essential media</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Deliver and acknowledge</td>\n<td>Recipient, representation, revision and delivery observation</td>\n<td>A locator is mistaken for delivered content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Plan and execute</td>\n<td>Work bindings, capability, authority, attempt identity and effects</td>\n<td>A plan or timeout is mistaken for a known execution outcome</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Assess and revise</td>\n<td>Exact subject, method, new evidence and preserved history</td>\n<td>A verdict is transferred to another revision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Store and reuse</td>\n<td>Dependency pins, current applicability, authorization and invalidation</td>\n<td>A cache hit bypasses the admission decision</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>An implementation MAY expose narrower operations within these families. Every exposed operation MUST state which of these responsibilities it owns and which it delegates. It MUST NOT hide a necessary responsibility between adapters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-composition\">4. Composition</h2>\n<p>For operations A then B, every allowed successful output of A used by B MUST satisfy B’s preconditions. A shared invariant MUST survive both transitions. Failure to establish that relation leaves composition unresolved even when each operation passes its isolated tests.</p>\n<p>A semantic transformation MUST declare its protected observations: for example the truth conditions of a proposition, the order of procedural steps, or the labels and connections of a diagram. Equality of those observations is a narrower claim than equality of all meanings. Lossy transformations MUST retain a loss account. An inverse can be claimed only within a declared domain for which reconstruction has been justified.</p>\n<p>Operations with effects require a separate composition policy. A local transaction around a record does not make an external service atomic. A timeout after dispatch MUST preserve an unknown-effect state until a domain-specific reconciliation establishes what occurred.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-outcomes-and-evidence\">5. Outcomes and evidence</h2>\n<p>The outcome vocabulary is success, partial, refused, failed and unresolved. Stop reasons explain why control ended; effect observations describe what is known about external changes. These dimensions MUST NOT be collapsed into a single completion flag. A comparison operation can succeed by reporting non-equivalence. A refusal can correctly enforce a constraint without satisfying the user’s original goal.</p>\n<p>Evidence MUST identify the definition version, input and output revisions, attempt, relevant requirements, checker and check result. An inherited observation MUST name the premise that permits inheritance. Checks of supplied claims and checks of independently observed conditions MUST be distinguishable.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-acceptance-cases\">6. Acceptance cases</h2>\n<p>A conforming adapter MUST exercise valid input; invalid input; unknown mandatory preconditions; a protected-property violation; partial output; an exhausted budget; a stale requirement revision; and, for effectful operations, a timeout with uncertain effect. A composition test MUST include a valid A output that violates B’s precondition. The expected result is refusal or unresolved composition, not automatic continuation.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">dependency and delivery study</a> supplies a finite closure replay. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">executable projection study</a> supplies bounded projection and revision evidence. Their finite results do not certify every family in this specification.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-representation-and-evolution\">7. Representation and evolution</h2>\n<p>The architecture does not require a new global artifact type for each operation. A versioned definition can be represented as an addressed record linked from attempts and evidence. Legacy example envelopes remain interpretable under their original profiles; an adapter MUST disclose any loss when mapping them to this family.</p>\n<p>An operation definition change creates a new version. Attempts keep the definition they used. Reassessment under the new definition is a new observation, not a retrospective claim that the earlier attempt followed it. See <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution/\">knowledge evolution</a> and the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/\">status model</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-common-contract-record","title":"2. Common contract record"},{"level":2,"id":"3-operation-families","title":"3. Operation families"},{"level":2,"id":"4-composition","title":"4. Composition"},{"level":2,"id":"5-outcomes-and-evidence","title":"5. Outcomes and evidence"},{"level":2,"id":"6-acceptance-cases","title":"6. Acceptance cases"},{"level":2,"id":"7-representation-and-evolution","title":"7. Representation and evolution"}],"title_id":"contracts-for-knowledge-operations","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/contract-family/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/contract-family/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"3eb380947abb58ab73dcd3f97adc65f2b4805569728806f7802982bae4b8c635","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"semantic-types","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/semantic-types/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/semantic-types/","title":"Semantic Types, Attribution, and Representations","titleRu":"Semantic Types, Attribution, and Representations","summary":"Semantic facets, attribution, scoped operators, ambiguous readings and multimodal representations.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>This specification defines the distinctions an implementation must preserve when interpreting and representing knowledge. Its facets organize content without claiming an exhaustive taxonomy of all human meaning. A procedure, a quoted procedure, and a deliberately incorrect procedural example may use similar words but support different uses.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>. A conforming profile MUST state its vocabulary, cardinalities and representation mapping. The <a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">scientific model</a> defines the separation between a source representation, interpretation, grounds and applicability.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-independent-semantic-dimensions\">2. Independent semantic dimensions</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Information to retain</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Representation</td>\n<td>Text, image, audio, video, structured data or an explicit combination</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Content kind</td>\n<td>Definition, observation, description, model, procedure, structure, constraint, goal, argument, question or another declared kind</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Discourse function</td>\n<td>Explanation, instruction, quotation, illustration, counterexample, question or another contextual role</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Aboutness</td>\n<td>The entities, concepts or domains to which the content refers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Attribution</td>\n<td>Who authored, reported, interpreted or assessed the addressed content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Source position</td>\n<td>Whether a source asserts, hypothesizes, denies, asks or reports another position</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Operators</td>\n<td>Negation, quantification, modality, condition, exception, order and their exact scopes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Assessments</td>\n<td>References to observations about fidelity, support or another stated property</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Governance</td>\n<td>Adoption and authority records, where relevant to the intended use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Applicability</td>\n<td>Domain, time, recipient and other conditions under which the content may be used</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A profile MAY store several dimensions in one structured record, but MUST NOT conflate their meanings. A source position is not a truth value. An author’s approval of a method is not current permission for a recipient to perform it. A generated image description is not the source image.</p>\n<p>For each required facet, the profile MUST distinguish known value, unknown value, justified not applicable, and withheld value. Unknown is not an empty known list. Withheld indicates a disclosure boundary and MUST NOT be represented as evidence that no value exists.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-identity-granularity-and-relations\">3. Identity, granularity and relations</h2>\n<p>One source region can support several semantic objects; one semantic object can require several source regions. Extraction boundaries MUST follow the obligations of the intended interpretation. A condition in a heading, a table unit or a diagram legend can be mandatory even if it falls outside a retrieved chunk.</p>\n<p>Source identity, semantic object identity, representation identity and retrieval chunk identity MUST remain distinguishable. Rechunking an index need not change the interpreted semantic object. Correcting an interpretation does require a new semantic revision. A different rendering can preserve a semantic revision only when the claimed equivalence has a stated basis.</p>\n<p>Relations MUST identify their endpoints and kind. A navigation link suggests further reading; a mandatory dependency says that a declared use requires the linked information. A vague association MUST NOT be silently upgraded to a proof premise, causal link or authority relation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-scope-and-ambiguity\">4. Scope and ambiguity</h2>\n<p>Operators MUST bind to their actual scope. “Not all members satisfy P” cannot be rewritten as “all members do not satisfy P.” An absence of permission is not an explicit prohibition, although either may prevent a particular action under its policy. A reported hypothesis is not an accepted conclusion.</p>\n<p>When several interpretations remain possible, an implementation MUST retain the alternatives or mark the interpretation unresolved. Choosing one is permitted only with a recorded basis and a declaration of remaining uncertainty. A confidence score alone does not explain which interpretation was chosen or which distinction could change the answer.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-multimodal-content-and-delivery\">5. Multimodal content and delivery</h2>\n<p>A media reference MUST identify the representation revision and selector semantics. Required legends, ordering, coordinate conventions, units and joint components MUST be retained. Delivery of a textual substitute is sufficient only when the task’s protected observations can be established from that substitute.</p>\n<p>OCR, a model-generated description, a human transcription and an original visual are separately attributable derivatives. A profile MUST record which was inspected. Claims about transcription fidelity require a comparison method and a reference; merely preserving all generated descriptions does not measure their accuracy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-validation-obligations\">6. Validation obligations</h2>\n<p>The conformance suite MUST include an attributed false assertion, a scoped negation, a quotation inside another quotation, a counterexample with tempting instructional wording, an unresolved alternative reading, and a required media dependency omitted from delivery. The expected behavior is preservation or explicit refusal; fluent reconstruction is not a substitute for those checks.</p>\n<p>Structural validation can check facet presence, vocabulary and references. It does not establish that the extractor found every relevant condition. Semantic fidelity therefore requires content-specific assessment and a stated inspection method.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-evidence-and-interoperability\">7. Evidence and interoperability</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/\">transcription and uncertainty study</a> reports how generated descriptions and uncertainty were retained in readable projections. It explicitly separates that result from unmeasured OCR or interpretation accuracy. Domain examples belong to that engineering study.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#skos\">SKOS</a> provides useful concepts, labels and relations; <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#provenance\">PROV-DM</a> supplies provenance distinctions. This profile does not claim to replace either standard or to make their graphs prove content truth. Vocabulary mappings MUST state whether they are exact, broader, narrower or merely related. See the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a> for the project’s preferred terms.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-independent-semantic-dimensions","title":"2. Independent semantic dimensions"},{"level":2,"id":"3-identity-granularity-and-relations","title":"3. Identity, granularity and relations"},{"level":2,"id":"4-scope-and-ambiguity","title":"4. Scope and ambiguity"},{"level":2,"id":"5-multimodal-content-and-delivery","title":"5. Multimodal content and delivery"},{"level":2,"id":"6-validation-obligations","title":"6. Validation obligations"},{"level":2,"id":"7-evidence-and-interoperability","title":"7. Evidence and interoperability"}],"title_id":"semantic-types-attribution-and-representations","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/semantic-types/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/semantic-types/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"2c1323d7845fe1b18281b91880d0db4791048c9f22d888f0fed26e0188290c50","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"context-package","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/context-package/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/context-package/","title":"Context Packages: Coverage, Dependencies, and Delivery","titleRu":"Context Packages: Coverage, Dependencies, and Delivery","summary":"Requirements for selected context, coverage, mandatory dependencies, budgets and observed delivery.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>A context package is a versioned selection of knowledge, conditions and grounds prepared for one declared use under a resource budget. It allows inspection of what was selected, why it is sufficient or incomplete, which dependencies were included, and what the recipient actually receives.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>. The package supports an answer, plan or execution stage. It is not itself a grant to act or evidence that the intended result occurred. See <a href=\"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/\">retrieval and context composition</a> for the formal distinction between coverage and search completeness.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-required-package-description\">2. Required package description</h2>\n<p>A conforming package MUST contain or reference its identity and revision; task and requirements revision; purpose and recipient; source and corpus snapshots; selected unit revisions; mandatory dependency closure; slot-level coverage; declared search scope and assurance; applicable constraints; representation and delivery requirements; resource account; and the observations supporting its status.</p>\n<p>Each requirement slot MUST identify the subject, quantifier, required conditions and supporting units. Joint requirements MUST be supported for the same subject and revision. Evidence for a property of one object and a different property of another cannot satisfy a conjunction about one object.</p>\n<p>The package MUST identify omitted requirements and unresolved dependencies. If a necessary ground is restricted, an authorized summary or substitute requires its own justification. Silently removing the ground while retaining a claim of complete support is prohibited.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-selection-and-closure\">3. Selection and closure</h2>\n<p>Selection starts from candidate units and computes the closure of the mandatory relations active for this use. Navigation links do not automatically join the closure. Unknown or missing required endpoints prevent a complete-package claim.</p>\n<p>The finite set-theoretic closure exists even with cycles, but a particular executable profile MAY require an acyclic dependency graph. That restriction MUST be stated and checked. Rejection of a cycle by such a profile is not a claim that the abstract closure is undefined.</p>\n<p>Adding units can introduce new dependencies and costs. A composer MUST recalculate closure and budget after a change. It MUST NOT infer that a larger text is always safer or more useful: additional content may conflict, distract, disclose protected information or exceed the recipient’s usable capacity.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-coverage-and-assurance\">4. Coverage and assurance</h2>\n<p>Coverage is assessed separately for candidate set, assembled package and final output. Each report MUST bind to its own stage and subject revision. A supported candidate set does not make a truncated package supported; a supported package does not prove that an answer used it correctly.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Coverage value</th>\n<th>Meaning within the declared requirements and scope</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>supported</td>\n<td>Every mandatory slot has the required established support</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>partial</td>\n<td>A supported part exists, with named missing or unresolved obligations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>unsupported</td>\n<td>The evaluated scope provides no required support under the chosen procedure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>conflicted</td>\n<td>Material incompatible grounds prevent a single admitted conclusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>unknown</td>\n<td>The available observations cannot establish the coverage state</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Search assurance is a separate dimension: candidate-only, exhaustive within a declared finite scope, or incomplete execution of the planned search. A request for two examples may be supported without exhaustive search. A request for all matching objects requires an additional completeness obligation. An empty top-k result MUST NOT be reported as proof of global absence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-budget-and-projection\">5. Budget and projection</h2>\n<p>The budget MUST account for the actual delivery context: system and task instructions, relevant history, selected material, tool descriptions, anticipated tool results, output allowance and margin where applicable. A character count is not an exact token measurement. Estimated and measured quantities MUST be distinguishable and identify the tokenizer or measurement method.</p>\n<p>A projection MUST retain protected units, their relations and task-relevant uncertainty. It MUST identify excluded units and losses. An output that reconstructs the selected bytes exactly can still be semantically incomplete if selection omitted an exception. Byte equality and task sufficiency therefore require different checks.</p>\n<p>When the mandatory closure cannot fit, the composer MUST return a partial result, negotiate an allowed narrower scope, change representation with justified preservation, or refuse. It MUST NOT silently discard a mandatory condition to meet the limit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-delivery-and-receipt\">6. Delivery and receipt</h2>\n<p>The manifest describes what should be delivered; a receipt records what was observed at the recipient boundary. A media URL does not establish byte availability, display, legibility or understanding. The delivery check MUST match the task’s representation requirements and the declared reader capabilities.</p>\n<p>Any output assessment MUST name the exact delivered package and requirements revision. An acknowledgement of receipt or comprehension is not a capability assessment, agreement to execute, or authorization.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-acceptance-and-evidence\">7. Acceptance and evidence</h2>\n<p>Acceptance cases MUST include an unresolved dependency, cross-object conjunction, missing quantifier coverage, a budget overflow caused by closure, omitted mandatory media, an outdated requirement revision, and a final output that drops a supported condition. For each, the check must distinguish package construction from downstream use.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">dependency-closure and delivery study</a> includes an executable finite replay of all 4,096 subsets of the 12 skill roots and 24,576 single-element inclusion relations on a 110-node dependency graph. This verifies the specified graph behavior, not the completeness of dependencies extracted from arbitrary sources. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">projection study</a> examines downstream representations and reports the limits of measured minimality.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-compatibility\">8. Compatibility</h2>\n<p>Legacy coverage fields can be imported only with their subject and semantics. Missing source snapshots, budget measurements or delivery observations remain unknown. Document edition 1.0 does not retrospectively upgrade earlier illustrative examples into executed packages. Terms are defined in the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-required-package-description","title":"2. Required package description"},{"level":2,"id":"3-selection-and-closure","title":"3. Selection and closure"},{"level":2,"id":"4-coverage-and-assurance","title":"4. Coverage and assurance"},{"level":2,"id":"5-budget-and-projection","title":"5. Budget and projection"},{"level":2,"id":"6-delivery-and-receipt","title":"6. Delivery and receipt"},{"level":2,"id":"7-acceptance-and-evidence","title":"7. Acceptance and evidence"},{"level":2,"id":"8-compatibility","title":"8. Compatibility"}],"title_id":"context-packages-coverage-dependencies-and-delivery","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/context-package/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/context-package/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"5f379fdad9b76b384a0b3abcc53ca42aaa8030ff5272ac28bf91bcc2f230aaa5","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"compiled-artifact-contract","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract/","title":"Compiled Knowledge Artifacts and Reproducible Derivation","titleRu":"Compiled Knowledge Artifacts and Reproducible Derivation","summary":"Saved derivatives with exact inputs, recipes, dependency manifests, templates and output evidence.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>A compiled knowledge artifact is a saved derivative produced by a declared extraction, selection, transformation or composition. It connects a result with its inputs, recipe, dependencies, requirements, output representation and evidence. Its availability can avoid repeated work, but availability alone does not justify reuse.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>. A conformance claim MUST identify the artifact kind, representation profile and checks performed. <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">Controlled knowledge reuse</a> provides the model; <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/\">cache identity</a> and <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/reuse-policy/\">reuse policy</a> govern later applications.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-result-kinds-and-minimum-information\">2. Result kinds and minimum information</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Result kind</th>\n<th>Additional minimum obligation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Retrieval result</td>\n<td>Query semantics, searched snapshot, procedure limits and candidate identities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Selected context</td>\n<td>Requirement coverage, mandatory closure, projection and resource account</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Derived explanation or answer</td>\n<td>Supported claims, intended audience, losses and required uncertainty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Dependency closure</td>\n<td>Roots, relation policy, exact graph snapshot and unresolved edges</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Parameterized template</td>\n<td>Allowed substitutions, domain restrictions and instance validation rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Scoped negative result</td>\n<td>The exact negative claim, finite scope, completeness premise and invalidators</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These kinds do not exhaust all possible derivatives. An extension MUST state its additional obligations and compatibility rules.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-artifact-and-build-records\">3. Artifact and build records</h2>\n<p>The artifact MUST identify immutable output bytes or a precisely canonicalized structured value, its hash domain, representation profile, content identity, source and rule-set revisions, recipe identity, and the producing attempt. A content digest establishes equality within its encoding assumptions; it does not authenticate the author or establish source truth.</p>\n<p>The build recipe MUST include exact input references, transformation definition versions, relevant parameters, and environment dependencies that can change the claimed result. A build identity records this declaration. Reproducibility additionally requires actual successful reruns under a stated equivalence criterion. A matching recipe hash does not prove that the recipe was executed or that nondeterministic outputs are equal.</p>\n<p>An attempt record MUST distinguish planned, running and terminated execution; outcome; stop reason; output references if any; and observed effects. A failed attempt MUST NOT create a fictional successful artifact. Partial outputs can be retained as partial with their limitations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-dependency-manifest\">4. Dependency manifest</h2>\n<p>The manifest MUST include each declared dependency’s identity, revision, role, requirement status, relevant validity conditions and change sensitivity. Dependencies can include sources, selectors, profiles, rules, compiler versions, authority epochs, queries and requirements. Omitting a relevant dependency invalidates any claim that the manifest is complete for that use.</p>\n<p>A result based on absence or exhaustive enumeration MUST bind the universe or index snapshot and completeness procedure. Adding a new matching item can invalidate a negative result even if none of its previously read positive inputs changed.</p>\n<p>The manifest MUST distinguish a navigational citation from an operational dependency. It SHOULD explain why each operational dependency is material, so a reviewer can identify both missing dependencies and unjustified over-invalidation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-templates-and-instances\">5. Templates and instances</h2>\n<p>A template defines allowed substitutions, protected structure and the conditions under which an instance may be produced. Every instance receives its own identity, bound parameters, requirements and admission decision. The template’s age or prior acceptance does not automatically transfer to an instance.</p>\n<p>Parameter substitution MUST NOT remove scope, change a protected quantifier or carry authority from one recipient to another without an explicit rule. Unvalidated parameters yield an unresolved or rejected instance. If instantiation changes the meaning outside the declared template domain, the result is a new derivation rather than a valid instance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-evidence-and-publication-boundary\">6. Evidence and publication boundary</h2>\n<p>Evidence MUST bind to the exact artifact and check. Structural validity, reconstruction equality, task sufficiency, current applicability and execution success remain separate observations. An artifact can be immutable and byte-correct while ineligible for a new request.</p>\n<p>Publishing a mutable current pointer requires a guard against stale workers and changed dependencies. A build that finished successfully under an old generation MUST NOT overwrite a newer or revoked head. The final admission decision must be valid at the actual serve boundary, not only when the build began.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-acceptance-cases-and-limits\">7. Acceptance cases and limits</h2>\n<p>A conformance suite MUST cover an altered input with an unchanged lookup key, a recipe change, an unsuccessful build, a template parameter outside its domain, a stale publication generation, a negative result invalidated by corpus growth, and evidence for the wrong output. The expected result must be an explicit new build, refusal, unresolved state or valid guarded reuse under a named policy.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/\">reuse-admission study</a> separates analytical policies, bounded checks and measured outcomes. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">projection study</a> reports finite transformations and revision behavior. Neither establishes a deployed distributed cache or a general semantic equivalence solver.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-compatibility-and-migration\">8. Compatibility and migration</h2>\n<p>A migration MUST retain the old artifact and hash domain, identify the mapping and its losses, and create a new representation record. A new document edition does not change an existing binary or wire protocol. Earlier bounded profiles are named in the <a href=\"/en/appendices/source-map/#protocol-compatibility\">compatibility appendix</a>; implementations claiming additional behavior require corresponding evidence.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-result-kinds-and-minimum-information","title":"2. Result kinds and minimum information"},{"level":2,"id":"3-artifact-and-build-records","title":"3. Artifact and build records"},{"level":2,"id":"4-dependency-manifest","title":"4. Dependency manifest"},{"level":2,"id":"5-templates-and-instances","title":"5. Templates and instances"},{"level":2,"id":"6-evidence-and-publication-boundary","title":"6. Evidence and publication boundary"},{"level":2,"id":"7-acceptance-cases-and-limits","title":"7. Acceptance cases and limits"},{"level":2,"id":"8-compatibility-and-migration","title":"8. Compatibility and migration"}],"title_id":"compiled-knowledge-artifacts-and-reproducible-derivation","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"102676d61dad36cc3cf4961b8ad68b342885272d065ee7506825f3b1a0609bd3","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"artifact-registry","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/","title":"Artifact Registry, Readable Names, and Document Formats","titleRu":"Artifact Registry, Readable Names, and Document Formats","summary":"Readable artifact names, canonical Markdown formats, identity rules and professional artifact distinctions.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>The registry gives humans and agents a predictable way to find, interpret and revise artifacts. It separates the identity of an object from its title, revision, file representation and current assessment. A new technical term does not automatically require a new database table or service.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>. The formats below are project conventions except where an external format is explicitly named. They do not claim to be universal standards for competencies, roles or blueprints.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-names-identity-and-revisions\">2. Names, identity and revisions</h2>\n<p>Every current artifact MUST live in a uniquely named directory within its type collection. Directory names use meaningful lowercase words separated by single hyphens; a reader must be able to infer the topic without looking up an internal number. Names such as <code>r1</code>, <code>r10</code> or <code>p09</code> are not valid titles or current canonical slugs. Historical identifiers MAY remain in provenance and migration maps.</p>\n<p>The main file has a predictable name determined by type. A rename preserves provenance and provides a compatibility map when an old path was published. An immutable revision identifies a particular content state. A mutable “current” pointer is a selection governed by a separate decision, not a new identity for every request.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-publication-formats\">3. Publication formats</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Collection</th>\n<th>Main file</th>\n<th>Required content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>papers/&lt;topic&gt;/</code></td>\n<td><code>PAPER.md</code></td>\n<td>Abstract, question and contribution, definitions, argument or results, counterexamples, limitations, references to engineering evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>engineering/specifications/&lt;topic&gt;/</code></td>\n<td><code>SPECIFICATION.md</code></td>\n<td>Purpose, conformance, model, requirements, failure handling, acceptance cases, compatibility, evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>engineering/studies/&lt;topic&gt;/</code></td>\n<td><code>STUDY.md</code></td>\n<td>Question, materials and rights, method, actual results, adverse cases, reproducibility, limitations, links to claims</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>reference/glossary/</code></td>\n<td><code>GLOSSARY.md</code></td>\n<td>Preferred terms, definitions, distinctions, aliases and language mapping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>reference/bibliography/</code></td>\n<td><code>BIBLIOGRAPHY.md</code></td>\n<td>Identified works, primary URLs or DOIs, and the role of each source</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>appendices/&lt;topic&gt;/</code></td>\n<td><code>APPENDIX.md</code></td>\n<td>A named supporting subject, its scope, references and relationship to the main documents</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Current publications MUST appear in <code>publication-catalog.json</code>. The catalogue records type, slug, path, title, summary, language and edition. It is a reading manifest, not an authorization to index or execute the content.</p>\n<p>Each main Markdown file MUST have YAML front matter with <code>name</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>artifact</code>, <code>edition</code>, <code>author</code>, <code>language</code>, <code>license</code>, and a nonempty <code>description</code>. Sources are registered through the repository source register. It MUST contain one descriptive H1. Version and machine identifiers belong in metadata, compatibility notes or provenance; they MUST NOT dominate the reading title. Relative links connect the current edition. Historical claims link to pinned revisions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-constraints-and-professional-artifacts\">4. Constraints and professional artifacts</h2>\n<p>The professional profile extends the same folder convention. Collections are created when actual artifacts exist; empty speculative directories are unnecessary.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Artifact</th>\n<th>Main file</th>\n<th>Meaning and required sections</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Role</td>\n<td><code>ROLE.md</code></td>\n<td>Responsibility; scope; permitted decisions; required skills or competencies; interfaces; accountability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Skill</td>\n<td><code>SKILL.md</code></td>\n<td>A reusable procedure; when to use it; inputs; steps; checks; constraints; outputs; supporting resources</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Competency</td>\n<td><code>COMPETENCY.md</code></td>\n<td>An ability requirement or assessed claim; subject; performance criteria; assessed conditions; evidence; validity limits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Blueprint</td>\n<td><code>BLUEPRINT.md</code></td>\n<td>A solution structure or process; intended result; components or steps; dependencies; choices; constraints; acceptance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Constraint</td>\n<td><code>CONSTRAINT.md</code></td>\n<td>Subject; scope; normative rule or condition; origin/authority; enforcement/check; exceptions; failure behavior</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Work Item</td>\n<td><code>WORK_ITEM.md</code></td>\n<td>Goal; requirements revision; inputs; assignments; constraints; plan; result criteria; attempts and outcomes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A competency definition says what ability would count. A competency assessment binds evidence to a particular executor and conditions. Neither a role nor a skill file certifies that ability. A blueprint organizes work; it does not supply authorization. Constraints are linked directly from all artifacts to which they apply, not buried in a general note or mistaken for optional advice.</p>\n<p>For skills, the external <a href=\"https://agentskills.io/specification\">Agent Skills specification</a> governs the <code>SKILL.md</code> filename and required <code>name</code> and <code>description</code> front matter. This project uses its supported <code>metadata</code> map for extra project fields rather than inventing incompatible required top-level keys. The skill name matches its directory. Supporting code, references and assets remain inside that skill’s directory. The document-body headings above are a project convention, not mandatory headings imposed by Agent Skills.</p>\n<p>Repository instructions use <a href=\"https://agents.md/\">AGENTS.md</a>, a plain Markdown instruction file. It is not a universal serialization format for a role or an agent identity. Provider-specific executable agent configurations MUST have an explicit adapter and remain distinct from the human-readable role definition.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-registry-obligations\">5. Registry obligations</h2>\n<p>Each registered revision MUST identify its kind, representation profile, identity, revision, license or disclosure restriction, provenance, required dependencies and applicable observations. A reader MUST distinguish known, unknown, not applicable and withheld information. Required references MUST resolve to the intended revision or report an unresolved dependency.</p>\n<p>Historical registration of 34 artifact types describes one example registry; it does not establish 34 exhaustive kinds of knowledge. Its common envelope is a carrier for those examples, not a restriction on all future contracts. The <a href=\"/en/appendices/source-map/\">source map</a> preserves that compatibility context outside the scientific argument.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-acceptance-and-migration\">6. Acceptance and migration</h2>\n<p>Registry checks MUST reject duplicate canonical names, missing required main files, incompatible front matter, broken current links and silent identity reuse. A rename test MUST show where the previous path leads and how a cited immutable revision remains recoverable. Unknown required extensions block use; preserving unknown optional data does not establish that it was interpreted.</p>\n<p>Migration MUST preserve source identity and original revisions, record the mapping, and disclose fields that could not be translated. It MUST NOT infer a capability, grant or successful execution from the presence of an artifact. The <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">revision paper</a> explains these distinctions; the <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">editorial study</a> records an actual bounded use of skills and a blueprint without claiming superiority from their existence.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-names-identity-and-revisions","title":"2. Names, identity and revisions"},{"level":2,"id":"3-publication-formats","title":"3. Publication formats"},{"level":2,"id":"4-constraints-and-professional-artifacts","title":"4. Constraints and professional artifacts"},{"level":2,"id":"5-registry-obligations","title":"5. Registry obligations"},{"level":2,"id":"6-acceptance-and-migration","title":"6. Acceptance and migration"}],"title_id":"artifact-registry-readable-names-and-document-formats","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"8783552e775b1d4a3e0c0ca16c832dce552de753214a4c27041903d0095e5d34","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"status-model","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/status-model/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/","title":"Assessment, Execution, and Publication States","titleRu":"Assessment, Execution, and Publication States","summary":"Independent assessment, execution, effect, freshness, publication and review states.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>A status is meaningful only for a named subject, property, revision and scope. One “verified” flag cannot faithfully represent schema validity, source fidelity, truth, coverage, authorization, execution outcome and publication review. This specification separates those questions and defines their transition obligations.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>. A profile MUST select the dimensions needed for its purpose and declare any aggregation rule. It need not attach every possible dimension to every record. The <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">evidence paper</a> explains why stronger conclusions need different evidence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-observation-record\">2. Observation record</h2>\n<p>Each observation MUST identify its subject and immutable revision, assessed property, requirements or criterion revision, method, observer, relevant time, result, grounds and limits. A result without a subject is not transferable evidence. If the method trusts supplied input rather than independently authenticating it, that assumption MUST be stated.</p>\n<p>The check result vocabulary is <code>pass</code>, <code>fail</code>, <code>unknown</code>, and <code>not_applicable</code>. The last value requires a demonstrated inapplicability rule, not missing data. For a conjunctive set of mandatory checks, any applicable failure prevents acceptance; any remaining applicable unknown prevents an all-pass conclusion. A positive result MUST NOT overwrite a negative or unresolved result on another mandatory property.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-independent-dimensions\">3. Independent dimensions</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Values or required distinction</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Availability of a value</td>\n<td>Known, unknown, justified not applicable, withheld</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Coverage</td>\n<td>Supported, partial, unsupported in scope, conflicted, unknown</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Search assurance</td>\n<td>Candidate-only, exhaustive in declared scope, incomplete procedure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Local run phase</td>\n<td>Planned, ready, running, paused, finished</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Operation outcome</td>\n<td>Success, partial, refused, failed, unresolved</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Stop reason</td>\n<td>Completed, clarification needed, budget insufficient, retrieval limit, stale evidence, precondition not met, cancelled, execution error, reconciliation pending</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>External effect</td>\n<td>No effect established, known effect, unknown effect, with the observed scope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Artifact lifecycle</td>\n<td>Building, ready, quarantined, retired, failed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Residency</td>\n<td>Resident, evicted, absent, unknown in a named storage location</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Freshness</td>\n<td>Fresh, stale, expired, unknown under a named policy and time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Reuse eligibility</td>\n<td>Eligible, ineligible, unknown for a particular new use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Case evidence</td>\n<td>Planned, illustrated, executed, evaluated, with the actual protocol and outcome</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Publication and review</td>\n<td>A fixed document edition; its publication decision; separate editorial, internal technical or external review observations</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The table is a semantic mapping. Existing wire profiles can retain different lexical values if their adapter declares an unambiguous mapping. Legacy labels MUST NOT be silently redefined.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-transition-rules\">4. Transition rules</h2>\n<p>Execution can move from ready to running only when the attempt’s preconditions are established. Local termination records a stop reason and outcome; it does not determine an unobserved remote effect. A later reconciliation can add an observation about that attempt without claiming a second execution.</p>\n<p>An artifact becomes ready only under its build and publication policy. A later dependency change can make it ineligible while its bytes remain resident and intact. Eviction changes residency, not the truth of content or its historical applicability. Revalidation creates a new observation; it does not change the immutable payload unless a new revision is produced.</p>\n<p>A changed requirement, subject revision, environment or authority can invalidate the applicability of an earlier verdict. Reuse of that verdict requires an explicit preservation argument. The latest timestamp alone is not such an argument.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-scientific-and-editorial-status\">5. Scientific and editorial status</h2>\n<p>A completed publication can state conditional propositions and report bounded results while acknowledging unanswered empirical questions. Its edition number identifies a coherent text, not proof of all future applications. The vocabulary MUST not equate “published,” “reviewed,” “implemented,” and “effective.”</p>\n<p>An internal agent review MUST identify the reviewer, input revision, method and conclusions, and MUST be labelled as such. It does not constitute independent external peer review. A finite test report requires actual outputs; an illustrated scenario is not an executed test. An executed failed trial remains useful evidence and MUST retain its negative result.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-user-facing-projections\">6. User-facing projections</h2>\n<p>Interfaces MAY summarize these dimensions, but the summary MUST preserve the distinctions relevant to the user’s decision. “Indexed revision X” is more precise than an unqualified “current.” “Checks passed for the supplied record” must not become “the action is authorized” when current authorization was outside the checked boundary.</p>\n<p>An unknown effect after a timeout must remain visible when a user decides whether to retry. Technical detail can be expanded on demand, but critical uncertainty cannot be hidden behind a positive badge.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-acceptance-and-evidence\">7. Acceptance and evidence</h2>\n<p>Tests MUST cover a passed schema with false supplied grounds, successful execution with a failed result criterion, a finished run with unknown effect, a resident but revoked artifact, a correct historical result ineligible for a current task, and an internal review that does not establish empirical validity.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/\">conformance study</a> demonstrates why a trusted-input premise matters. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">editorial pilot</a> reports an equal observed score for the structured and baseline conditions. Neither result permits a general effectiveness label. See <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">revision and governance</a> for the distinction between an observation and adoption of a rule.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-observation-record","title":"2. Observation record"},{"level":2,"id":"3-independent-dimensions","title":"3. Independent dimensions"},{"level":2,"id":"4-transition-rules","title":"4. Transition rules"},{"level":2,"id":"5-scientific-and-editorial-status","title":"5. Scientific and editorial status"},{"level":2,"id":"6-user-facing-projections","title":"6. User-facing projections"},{"level":2,"id":"7-acceptance-and-evidence","title":"7. Acceptance and evidence"}],"title_id":"assessment-execution-and-publication-states","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/status-model/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/status-model/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"fa7c443248cca872e0ba77fc55e07ec69cb0ef52357c9fa2dbb88119bfae8d38","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"cache-identity-and-invalidation","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/","title":"Cache Identity, Dependency Tracking, and Invalidation","titleRu":"Cache Identity, Dependency Tracking, and Invalidation","summary":"Content, build and lookup identity, dependency changes and guarded publication and serving.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>This specification governs how a saved result is identified, found as a candidate, invalidated after change and admitted at a serve boundary. Equal bytes, equal recipes, semantic suitability and current authorization are separate relations.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>. The architecture is supported by conditional arguments and bounded models, not by a claim of a deployed distributed coherence protocol. Every implementation MUST state its consistency assumptions and failure behavior. See <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">controlled reuse</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-identity-layers\">2. Identity layers</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Identity</th>\n<th>What it fixes</th>\n<th>What it does not establish</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Content identity</td>\n<td>Exact bytes or canonical structured value in a named hash domain</td>\n<td>Meaning, authorship, truth or permission</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Build identity</td>\n<td>Declared inputs, recipe, parameters and relevant environment</td>\n<td>Actual execution or deterministic output</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Lookup identity</td>\n<td>A candidate class for a query, recipient and policy</td>\n<td>Eligibility of every retrieved candidate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Current head</td>\n<td>The selected revision within a namespace and generation</td>\n<td>Timeless suitability for all consumers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A lookup MUST be followed by descriptor comparison and the required admission checks. Negation, quantifiers, recipient, purpose and scope are not dispensable variations of a similar query. A digest collision assumption and canonicalization procedure MUST be stated when digest equality is used as identity evidence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-dependency-tracking\">3. Dependency tracking</h2>\n<p>The read set MUST contain every declared dependency material to the claimed reuse guarantee, including source revisions, required selectors, profiles, rules, request requirements, compiler behavior and authorization state. Dependency kind determines the impact rule. Relocation of identical bytes can preserve content identity while changing accessibility; a changed interpretation can invalidate applicability without changing a source file.</p>\n<p>Negative results and exhaustive claims MUST also track their universe or index generation and completeness assumptions. New content can invalidate “no match” or “all matches” without modifying any previously selected object. Top-k retrieval does not supply that completeness premise.</p>\n<p>Unknown impact MUST produce re-evaluation or a conservative unresolved result under the policy. It MUST NOT be inferred harmless simply because the old key still matches.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-change-events-and-ordering\">4. Change events and ordering</h2>\n<p>A change event identifies the affected dependency, previous and new revisions or epochs, event identity, relevant ordering scope and impact class. An implementation MUST state how it detects lost, duplicate and reordered events. Event delivery alone does not establish that all cached derivatives were reconsidered.</p>\n<p>If event loss is possible, reconciliation MUST compare observed dependency state with authoritative state before renewing affected admission claims. A disconnected system MAY serve under an explicitly bounded policy, but it cannot promise immediate global revocation unless its protocol actually provides it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-guarded-publication-and-serving\">5. Guarded publication and serving</h2>\n<p>A worker records the generation and dependency read set used for its build. Before updating a mutable head, it MUST verify that the publication guard still holds. A late worker cannot restore an older head after a newer revision or revocation.</p>\n<p>The serve decision MUST bind the exact output to the relevant current dependencies and authorization at a declared consistency point. A check at build time followed by an unguarded later serve leaves a time-of-check/time-of-use gap. Locks, transactions, leases or validation-and-commit protocols can address this under different assumptions; merely naming one is not an implementation.</p>\n<p>A lease MUST define its scope, expiry, revocation behavior, clock assumptions and partition behavior. An implementation claiming strict revocation cannot simultaneously treat an unobservable revocation as harmless during a lease unless its stated authorization semantics permit that delay.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-storage-recovery-and-observability\">6. Storage, recovery and observability</h2>\n<p>Residency and eligibility are independent. Eviction can remove a valid copy; retention can preserve an invalid one. Recovery MUST verify content identity and rebuild the current eligibility decision rather than restoring a historical positive verdict as current fact.</p>\n<p>Logs SHOULD record lookup candidate, compared descriptors, dependency observations, admission decision, publication generation and actual serve result. Logging must respect the recipient’s disclosure rights. Required audit retention is a separate policy from cache residence time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-acceptance-cases\">7. Acceptance cases</h2>\n<p>Required tests include: identical query text with a changed recipient; changed source under a matching lookup key; a new object invalidating a negative result; a lost invalidation event found by reconciliation; a late build attempting to overwrite a newer head; expiry exactly at a hard deadline; and revocation between preliminary check and serve. Each test must identify the simulated or real consistency boundary.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/\">reuse study</a> reports the available evidence and its deployment limits. <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#http-caching\">RFC 9111</a> is a reference for HTTP cache semantics, not proof that the semantic or authority checks defined here have been implemented. Existing bounded profiles and retained results are identified in the <a href=\"/en/appendices/source-map/#protocol-compatibility\">compatibility appendix</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-identity-layers","title":"2. Identity layers"},{"level":2,"id":"3-dependency-tracking","title":"3. Dependency tracking"},{"level":2,"id":"4-change-events-and-ordering","title":"4. Change events and ordering"},{"level":2,"id":"5-guarded-publication-and-serving","title":"5. Guarded publication and serving"},{"level":2,"id":"6-storage-recovery-and-observability","title":"6. Storage, recovery and observability"},{"level":2,"id":"7-acceptance-cases","title":"7. Acceptance cases"}],"title_id":"cache-identity-dependency-tracking-and-invalidation","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"6ce6cdf9ae537dc61dea788e12eca4b38c009d0f8111d516f696fbb58e8c9155","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"reuse-policy","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/reuse-policy/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/reuse-policy/","title":"Reuse Policy: Time, Authorization, and Decision Outcomes","titleRu":"Reuse Policy: Time, Authorization, and Decision Outcomes","summary":"Temporal modes, conservative deadlines, authorization, decision outcomes and retention boundaries.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>A reuse policy determines whether a saved result may satisfy a new request, what must be rechecked, and which time and authority constraints apply. It operates on a specific artifact, recipient, purpose, requirements revision and observation time.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>. A cache hit is only a candidate. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract/\">compiled-artifact specification</a> defines the saved result; <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/\">identity and invalidation</a> defines its dependency and serve guards.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-admission-before-timing\">2. Admission before timing</h2>\n<p>Before deciding freshness, the policy MUST establish semantic compatibility with the request, required dependency availability, applicable constraints, current disclosure authorization and any action-specific grant. A fresh but inapplicable result is ineligible. A byte-identical object does not inherit the authority of another recipient.</p>\n<p>A sufficient existing authorization is used within its scope; this specification does not require asking a user for the same permission at every pipeline step. It does require checking that the authorization still covers the actual action, subject and time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-temporal-modes\">3. Temporal modes</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Mode</th>\n<th>Commitment</th>\n<th>Continuing obligations</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Historical pinned</td>\n<td>Describe the identified historical revision as historical</td>\n<td>Current disclosure authorization and applicable hard restrictions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Current</td>\n<td>Establish the relevant current source and policy state at the serve boundary</td>\n<td>Dependency, scope and authority checks under the declared consistency model</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Bounded staleness</td>\n<td>Permit a named maximum age under an explicit policy</td>\n<td>Hard restrictions, provenance, uncertainty and any mandatory revalidation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Service availability MUST NOT silently choose a weaker temporal mode. Historical correctness does not authorize disclosure of a withdrawn or restricted record. A background refresh does not by itself make the old content eligible.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-time-model-and-deadlines\">4. Time model and deadlines</h2>\n<p>The policy MUST distinguish source-event time, source-observation time, artifact-build time, check time, authorization interval and storage time. Updating a digest check does not make an old fact new. The relevant clock and uncertainty bounds belong to the observation.</p>\n<p>For the bounded interval model, let a check occur within <code>[checked.lower, checked.upper]</code>, and let a request’s current time lie within <code>[now.lower, now.upper]</code>. A TTL-based deadline is conservatively anchored at <code>checked.lower + ttl</code>. The effective hard deadline is the minimum of applicable finite hard deadlines. Reuse requires <code>now.upper &lt; effective_hard_deadline</code>; equality closes the window. A soft deadline is reached when <code>now.upper &gt;= soft_deadline</code>.</p>\n<p>An explicitly absent time limit does not waive other gates. Unknown mandatory time is unresolved, not an infinite deadline. Invalid intervals and incompatible chronology MUST be rejected. A different distributed time model requires its own declared semantics rather than pretending these interval rules provide causal ordering.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-decision-outcomes\">5. Decision outcomes</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Decision</th>\n<th>Required behavior</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Exact reuse</td>\n<td>Serve only after all required checks and the final serve guard succeed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Revalidate before reuse</td>\n<td>Obtain the missing current observations; do not serve as admitted beforehand</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Rebuild</td>\n<td>Create a new artifact under the current inputs or rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Instantiate</td>\n<td>Produce and assess a new template instance with bound parameters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Refuse</td>\n<td>Report a known blocking condition at the permitted disclosure level</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Unresolved</td>\n<td>Report which mandatory observation is missing or inconclusive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>If bounded stale serving while refreshing is allowed, the decision MUST explicitly record stale freshness, its permitted bound and the refresh duty. This differs from revalidate-before-reuse. A refresh failure does not renew the old result’s deadlines. Known invalidity or hard revocation cannot be concealed by a stale fallback.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-cost-retention-and-measurement\">6. Cost, retention and measurement</h2>\n<p>Cost can influence an eligible choice, but cannot excuse a mandatory violation. A utility estimate MUST state its task, baseline, horizon, units and included costs. Build, retrieval, validation, maintenance and integration costs must not be counted twice. Planned savings are estimates until measured.</p>\n<p>Retention is a storage policy, not an eligibility policy. Popularity can justify keeping bytes longer; it does not extend the validity of their grounds. Negative results require especially explicit corpus scope and invalidation conditions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-acceptance-and-evidence\">7. Acceptance and evidence</h2>\n<p>Required cases include a current request with unavailable source-head evidence; a historical request after disclosure revocation; unknown clock bounds; equality at hard expiry; stale serving explicitly allowed and explicitly forbidden; refresh failure; a template instance with changed authority; and a low-cost option that violates a mandatory constraint.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/\">reuse-admission and cost study</a> distinguishes formal checks from measured operational savings. The <a href=\"/en/science/knowledge-utility/\">utility paper</a> states conditional cost results and the assumptions needed for causal comparison. <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#stale-http-responses\">RFC 5861</a> provides an HTTP analogy for stale handling; it is not an authorization policy for knowledge or actions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-compatibility\">8. Compatibility</h2>\n<p>No illustrative TTL is a universal default. A deployment MUST choose and justify its policy for the task and risk. Importing an older record preserves its original observations and adds unknown values where the new policy needs information not present in the source. A prior all-purpose validity flag cannot be losslessly decomposed into freshness, eligibility and authority without additional evidence.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-admission-before-timing","title":"2. Admission before timing"},{"level":2,"id":"3-temporal-modes","title":"3. Temporal modes"},{"level":2,"id":"4-time-model-and-deadlines","title":"4. Time model and deadlines"},{"level":2,"id":"5-decision-outcomes","title":"5. Decision outcomes"},{"level":2,"id":"6-cost-retention-and-measurement","title":"6. Cost, retention and measurement"},{"level":2,"id":"7-acceptance-and-evidence","title":"7. Acceptance and evidence"},{"level":2,"id":"8-compatibility","title":"8. Compatibility"}],"title_id":"reuse-policy-time-authorization-and-decision-outcomes","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/reuse-policy/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/reuse-policy/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"919525f3e70ea3abe2b10de6d154f3693e8d213ca6dba7b37fa2c4ddec4593b2","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"knowledge-evolution","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution/","title":"Knowledge Evolution and Versioned Rules","titleRu":"Knowledge Evolution and Versioned Rules","summary":"Versioned creation rules, adoption, impact analysis, reassessment, migration and coherent publication.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>Every new knowledge or decision artifact must record the exact contracts, profiles and rules that materially governed its creation. Reassessment under later rules is a new observation. The original derivation and revision remain traceable.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>. This specification governs changes to interpretation and admission rules as well as software versions. A named research direction does not by itself establish an implemented schema or executable validator. The <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">revision paper</a> supplies the underlying model.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-creation-basis\">2. Creation basis</h2>\n<p>A creation-basis record MUST identify the artifact revision, material source revisions, contract and profile definitions, relevant rule-set versions, transformation recipe, responsible actor, creation time or interval, and known gaps. References MUST resolve to immutable revisions or record the inability to resolve them.</p>\n<p>Only actually used rules are included as creation inputs. A later adopted rule MUST NOT be inserted into the old basis as though it governed the original work. Broad labels such as “latest contract” are insufficient when the exact revision is material.</p>\n<p>The implementation MUST distinguish content change from representation change, assessment change and governance change. An updated assessment can change whether a use is admitted without modifying source bytes. A corrected interpretation produces a new semantic revision even if the original source is unchanged.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-change-proposal-and-adoption\">3. Change proposal and adoption</h2>\n<p>Each proposal MUST identify the previous and proposed rule revisions, motivation, affected scope, expected compatibility, migration implications, evidence, objections and known unresolved questions. Adoption requires the decision maker and authority specified by the governance policy. A contributor, editor or automated validator does not acquire that authority merely by producing a proposal.</p>\n<p>An adoption decision is evidence that a norm was accepted within its scope. It is not proof that every factual claim motivating that norm is true. The decision MUST retain links to the assessed proposal and any restrictions or effective date.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-impact-analysis-and-reassessment\">4. Impact analysis and reassessment</h2>\n<p>Impact analysis starts from declared dependency relations. Affected artifacts MUST be classified with the relevant new rule set and use scope. The decision vocabulary is: compatible for that scope; restricted to a narrower scope; requires review; blocked for that use; or unknown impact. These are projections over individual observations, not permanent global truth labels.</p>\n<p>A compatibility result MUST state the preservation argument or tests. Unknown dependency coverage cannot support a claim that every affected artifact was found. Conservative re-evaluation is required when an untracked dependency could change a mandatory obligation.</p>\n<p>Reassessment records the old artifact, new rule set, relevant current state, method, results and limitations. A passing reassessment under a narrower test does not establish compatibility with a broader profile. Multiple assessments can coexist when their scopes differ.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-migration\">5. Migration</h2>\n<p>A migration creates a new representation or revision with an explicit source link, mapping, validator results, losses and unresolved fields. It MUST preserve negation, conditions, attribution and applicable constraints or declare their loss. Missing capability or authority MUST remain unknown. A migration cannot manufacture an execution attempt or successful result.</p>\n<p>If semantic equivalence has not been established, the migration MUST state that limit even when the new structure is valid. Reversible byte transformation establishes only the declared representation property. Lossless semantic migration needs an interpretation-preservation argument within a defined domain.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-visibility-and-rollback\">6. Visibility and rollback</h2>\n<p>Publication MUST select a coherent set of artifact revisions, catalogue entries and rendered representations. A pointer update requires an identified decision and a guard against conflicting generations. The website, downloadable corpus and API index MUST expose their actual source revisions so a consumer can detect differences.</p>\n<p>Rollback changes the selected current head; it does not erase the later revision or its observations. If a rule was withdrawn because of a known defect or disclosure restriction, a rollback MUST preserve that knowledge and MUST NOT silently reauthorize a previously blocked use.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-acceptance-cases\">7. Acceptance cases</h2>\n<p>Required cases include a rule revision with no content change; an affected artifact discovered through an indirect dependency; an unresolved impact due to incomplete dependencies; an invalid migration that loses a constraint; conflicting adoption decisions; a late publisher; and rollback after revocation. Each case MUST retain the historical creation basis and the separate current decision.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">projection and revision study</a> supplies bounded evidence about addressed changes and finite projections. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/\">conformance study</a> records limited migration checks. Their results do not establish automatic migration of arbitrary knowledge graphs or a deployed community governance service.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-publication-and-implementation-versions\">8. Publication and implementation versions</h2>\n<p>The document edition, serialized profile, validator implementation, evidence protocol, service build and index snapshot MUST have separately identified versions. Removing a development label from a manuscript does not change a deployed protocol. Conversely, an unfinished service feature does not prevent publication of a complete formal argument with explicit assumptions.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/appendices/source-map/\">source map and compatibility record</a> identifies what this edition replaces and what historical wire versions remain unchanged. The <a href=\"/en/appendices/api-integration/\">API integration appendix</a> defines the observable publication boundaries without claiming an unverified contract-composition endpoint.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-creation-basis","title":"2. Creation basis"},{"level":2,"id":"3-change-proposal-and-adoption","title":"3. Change proposal and adoption"},{"level":2,"id":"4-impact-analysis-and-reassessment","title":"4. Impact analysis and reassessment"},{"level":2,"id":"5-migration","title":"5. Migration"},{"level":2,"id":"6-visibility-and-rollback","title":"6. Visibility and rollback"},{"level":2,"id":"7-acceptance-cases","title":"7. Acceptance cases"},{"level":2,"id":"8-publication-and-implementation-versions","title":"8. Publication and implementation versions"}],"title_id":"knowledge-evolution-and-versioned-rules","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"421987294714317959697d9b2ea4c338a952585c19294a836567d4d701c07e96","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"book-package","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/book-package/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/book-package/","title":"Book Packages: Source Identity, Semantic Layers, and Validation","titleRu":"Book Packages: Source Identity, Semantic Layers, and Validation","summary":"A concrete book-package schema, reversible conversion ledger, semantic layers, rights boundaries and synthetic conformance checks.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Implementation component</th>\n<th>Exact scope</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/schemas/book-package.schema.json\">Schema</a></td>\n<td>Book-package records, version 1; Apache-2.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/tools/check_book_package.py\">Validator</a></td>\n<td>Structural and cross-file checks; Apache-2.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/schemas/fixtures/book-package\">Fixtures</a></td>\n<td>Original synthetic inputs and counterexamples</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/tools/test_book_package.py\">Conformance tests</a></td>\n<td>Positive, negative and cross-file mutation checks</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This document specifies an implemented structural format. The implementation has a synthetic conformance suite; conversion fidelity on real books and downstream admission remain separate, unmeasured properties. Publication edition 1.0 does not change the schema’s record identifiers or grant runtime admission.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-validation-boundary\">1. Purpose and validation boundary</h2>\n<p>A book package is the unit in which one source representation of a book (one file) is carried through conversion into Markdown, semantic description and a relation graph, so that the result can travel inside the Knowledge Contract as a candidate. This document fixes the <strong>format</strong>. It makes no claim that any conversion is faithful, that any extracted meaning is correct, or that any package is admitted by the Knowledge Contract runtime (KC2). The manifest, semantic objects, relations and quality report carry <code>kc2_admission: not_measured</code>; a value of <code>admitted</code> is rejected by the schema, because admission is decided elsewhere and never by the producer.</p>\n<p>A successful validation establishes that the submitted package passes the implemented checks for shapes, digests, counts, endpoints, anchor identity and status coherence. It is not a proof that the implementation detects every structural defect. It does not prove that the text of an anchor says what an object claims. That is the job of review, recorded separately in the quality report.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-layout\">2. Layout</h2>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>&lt;package_id&gt;/\n  manifest.json      identity, source binding, components with digests, rights, status\n  document.md        faithful conversion of the source; no generated text\n  readable.md        readable edition; the only file edits may change\n  assets/            images and other extracted resources\n  edits.jsonl        edit ledger: one record per change from document.md to readable.md\n  objects.jsonl      semantic objects\n  relations.jsonl    relations between objects\n  quality.json       quality report</code></pre>\n<p><code>document.md</code> is never edited. A correction goes into <code>readable.md</code> through a ledger record that holds the source fragment, the replacement, the anchor, the basis, the reason and the input and output digests. Every accepted edit is reversible (<code>reversible</code> is the constant <code>true</code>). The validator checks this rather than assuming it: at the step where an accepted edit is applied, <code>original_text</code> and <code>replacement_text</code> must both be non-empty, <code>original_text</code> must occur exactly once in the text being edited and <code>replacement_text</code> exactly once in the text produced, and undoing the accepted edits in reverse order must give <code>document.md</code> exactly. “Once” counts overlapping occurrences (<code>aa</code> occurs twice in <code>xaaay</code>). All comparison is byte-exact: files are read as bytes and decoded as strict UTF-8 with no newline translation, so <code>\\r\\n</code>, a lone <code>\\r</code> and <code>\\n</code> are different characters, <code>original_text</code> may contain any of them, and every digest in the chain is over the exact bytes of the text it names. JSON Lines files, <code>SHA256SUMS</code> and the check list are split on <code>\\n</code> only, as a <code>\\n</code>-splitting reader sees them: one empty trailing element (the final newline) is allowed, a line ending in <code>\\r</code> is refused, and U+2028, U+0085, a lone <code>\\r</code> and the control characters <code>\\x0b</code>, <code>\\x0c</code> and <code>\\x1c</code> to <code>\\x1e</code> are ordinary characters, never separators. An insertion, a deletion, an edit of a repeated fragment and a replacement that duplicates existing text therefore cannot be recorded in the ledger; the schema is unchanged. The ledger is tied to the files: the digests chain from <code>document.md</code> through each accepted edit, each <code>original_text</code> must occur in the text it edits, and <code>readable.md</code> must equal the replay of the accepted edits over <code>document.md</code>. Paths are relative, contain no <code>..</code>, and never start with <code>/</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-identity-and-source-binding\">3. Identity and source binding</h2>\n<p>Three identifiers are kept apart: <code>work_id</code> (the work), <code>edition_id</code> (edition and language) and <code>representation_id</code> (one file). Translations of one work share a <code>related_works</code> relation (<code>translation_of</code>, <code>edition_of</code>, <code>same_work_as</code>, <code>packaged_with</code>) and keep separate editions, languages and evidence. The manifest holds the SHA-256 and size of the source representation, its format, an <code>integrity</code> state (<code>verified</code>, <code>mismatch</code>, <code>unverified</code>) and a <code>completeness</code> state (<code>full</code>, <code>partial</code>, <code>bibliography_only</code>). Rules enforced:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>mismatch</code> forces <code>package_status: blocked_integrity</code>; the expected hash is never replaced to make a package pass.</li>\n<li><code>bibliography_only</code> forces <code>package_status: bibliography_only</code> and forbids <code>document_md</code>; such a record is never queued as a full text.</li>\n<li><code>knowledge_ready</code> requires <code>verified</code> integrity and <code>full</code> completeness.</li>\n<li><code>document_md.input_sha256</code> equals the source representation hash, and names the converting tool and version.</li>\n<li><code>adapter_required</code>, <code>blocked_integrity</code> and <code>bibliography_only</code> packages have no <code>document_md</code> and no <code>readable_md</code>; the schema forbids them. A format without converter output yields an <code>adapter_required</code> package, never an empty or invented document. <code>structure_only</code>, <code>partial_pilot</code> and <code>knowledge_ready</code> packages must have both. <code>tools/build_book_package.py</code> builds such packages around a source file and the Markdown a converter produced: it copies that Markdown unchanged, starts <code>readable.md</code> as a copy with an empty edit ledger, and leaves objects and relations empty with the semantic block <code>not_built</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"4-semantic-objects\">4. Semantic objects</h2>\n<p>An object (<code>KCBookObject/v1</code>) is a concept, claim, procedure, figure, table, formula, code block, example or other structure. It carries its text, language, origin, evidence anchors (at least one), qualifiers (conditions, numbers with units) and eight serialized semantic facets consistent with the dimensions in the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/semantic-types/\">semantic-types specification</a>: <code>representation_modes</code>, <code>content_kinds</code>, <code>discourse_functions</code>, <code>aboutness</code>, <code>source_position</code>, <code>semantic_operators</code>, <code>assessment_refs</code>, <code>governance_refs</code>. Each facet is present. A facet is either <code>known</code> with a value, or <code>unknown</code>, <code>not_applicable</code> or <code>withheld</code> with a reason and no value; absence of information is never an empty list. <code>content_kinds</code> uses the following declared profile vocabulary: <code>definition</code>, <code>state_description</code>, <code>observation_measurement</code>, <code>generalization</code>, <code>explanatory_model</code>, <code>formal_model</code>, <code>procedure</code>, <code>structure_configuration</code>, <code>constraint_norm</code>, <code>goal_intent</code>, <code>preference_evaluation</code>, <code>argument_evidence</code>, <code>example</code>, <code>counterexample</code>, <code>question_problem_gap</code>, <code>narrative_episode</code>, <code>analogy_metaphor</code>, <code>hypothesis_interpretation</code>, and <code>commitment_speech_act</code>. These nineteen values refine eighteen earlier content rubrics by separating example from counterexample. This profile vocabulary is not an exhaustive taxonomy of meaning. Attribution remains in the object origin and generation records; task applicability must be evaluated by the consuming contract and is not established by these eight facet fields. A claim of class <code>benchmark</code> must carry corpus, device, tool version, settings, units and mode (<code>encode</code>, <code>decode</code>, <code>encode_decode</code>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-relations\">5. Relations</h2>\n<p>A relation (<code>KCBookRelation/v1</code>) connects two objects of the package and has one of six types: <code>defines</code>, <code>uses</code>, <code>variant_of</code>, <code>requires</code>, <code>compared_with</code>, <code>contradicts</code>. It carries evidence anchors tagged with the side they support (<code>from</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>both</code>). Rules:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>at least one anchor is required for every type;</li>\n<li><code>contradicts</code> needs an anchor for the <code>from</code> side and an anchor for the <code>to</code> side, because a contradiction asserted from one side only is a reading, not a finding;</li>\n<li>an object may not be related to itself; endpoints must exist in the same package;</li>\n<li>a relation that touches a stale object is stale or rejected (section 9).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"6-evidence-anchors\">6. Evidence anchors</h2>\n<p>Every anchor repeats the <code>representation_id</code> and the source <code>sha256</code> it points into; the validator rejects an anchor that names another representation or source. Coordinates are never bare numbers.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Source formats</th>\n<th>Fields</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>page_bbox</code></td>\n<td>PDF, DjVu, PS after conversion</td>\n<td><code>page</code> (from 1), <code>bbox</code> (4 numbers, positive area), <code>bbox_origin</code>, <code>bbox_unit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>spine_offset</code></td>\n<td>EPUB</td>\n<td><code>spine_item</code>, optional <code>element_path</code>, <code>start</code>, <code>end</code> (end not before start)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>section_block</code></td>\n<td>HTML, MD, TXT</td>\n<td><code>section_id</code>, <code>block_id</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>sheet_range</code></td>\n<td>XLSX</td>\n<td><code>sheet</code>, <code>range</code>, <code>layer</code> (<code>formula</code>, <code>cached_value</code>, <code>literal</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>json_pointer</code></td>\n<td>JSON, DocC</td>\n<td><code>pointer</code> (RFC 6901)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>slide_block</code></td>\n<td>presentations</td>\n<td><code>slide</code>, <code>block_id</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A new source format needs a new anchor kind in a new schema revision, not a loose string. The validator requires the anchor kind to fit the source format of the manifest (for example <code>page_bbox</code> for PDF, <code>section_block</code> for Markdown). It does not resolve an anchor against the source: that a section, page or range exists, and that it says what an object claims, is not checked here.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-generated-content-is-marked-always\">7. Generated content is marked, always</h2>\n<p>Every object and relation has an <code>origin</code>: <code>author_statement</code>, <code>editor_correction</code>, <code>generated_meaning</code>, <code>generated_description</code>, <code>our_experiment</code> or <code>model_inference</code>. Everything except <code>author_statement</code> must carry a <code>generation</code> record (tool, version, optional model id, run id, input digest, time) and must set <code>presented_as_author</code> to <code>false</code>. An <code>author_statement</code> must not carry a <code>generation</code> record. Machine descriptions of illustrations and generated meanings live only in the object file; <code>document_md.contains_generated_text</code> is the constant <code>false</code>, and <code>readable_md</code> declares whether it holds any. The rendering of the knowledge layer to Markdown labels every generated block “GENERATED, not the author’s statement”. A missing asset is <code>state: missing_asset</code> with no path and no digest; no image or description is invented for it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-quality-report-extraction-and-semantics-apart\">8. Quality report: extraction and semantics apart</h2>\n<p><code>quality.json</code> has two blocks that never merge. <code>extraction</code> holds a status (<code>pass</code>, <code>partial</code>, <code>adapter_required</code>, <code>failed</code>, <code>not_measured</code>), expected and seen units, a list of checks (<code>pass</code>, <code>fail</code>, <code>unknown</code>, <code>not_applicable</code>, each with a method) and a CER block. CER is either <code>measured</code> with a value, a gold or silver reference class and a sample size, or <code>not_measured</code> with value <code>null</code>; a null is not a zero. <code>semantics</code> holds a status (<code>not_built</code>, <code>built_unreviewed</code>, <code>reviewed</code>, <code>not_measured</code>), object and relation counts and, when reviewed, the reviewer type, sample size and result. <code>not_built</code> and <code>not_measured</code> force both counts to zero: an empty graph means that the semantic layer was not built, never that the book has no support for a question. There is no combined score; the schema rejects one.</p>\n<p><code>package_status</code> is derived, not chosen freely: <code>knowledge_ready</code> requires extraction <code>pass</code> and semantics <code>reviewed</code>; <code>structure_only</code> requires semantics <code>not_built</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-staleness\">9. Staleness</h2>\n<p>A source span corrected after objects were derived from it makes those objects stale. The validator requires: an object listed under <code>depends_on_objects</code> of a <code>stale</code> edit is <code>stale</code> or <code>rejected</code>, and so is every relation that touches a stale object. A new context package must not reuse a stale quotation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"10-rights-and-layers\">10. Rights and layers</h2>\n<p><code>rights.license_class</code> is <code>author_open</code>, <code>third_party_restricted</code> or <code>unknown</code>; <code>publishable_layers</code> lists what may leave the producer: <code>document_md</code>, <code>readable_md</code>, <code>assets</code>, <code>knowledge</code>, <code>quality</code>. A work that is not <code>author_open</code> may not publish <code>document_md</code>, <code>readable_md</code> or <code>assets</code>. This is a producer-side guard, not a legal opinion; whether the knowledge layer of a restricted work may quote it is a rights question for the publication decision.</p>\n<h2 id=\"11-mapping-to-candidate-export-v2-and-knowledge-contract-v3-admission\">11. Mapping to candidate export v2 and Knowledge Contract V3 admission</h2>\n<p>The candidate exporter (<code>tools/export_corpus.py</code>, schema <code>KnowledgeContractCandidateExport/v2</code>, DEC-AUP-0171) carries only Markdown files whose path matches its path pattern under <code>papers/</code>, <code>spec/</code>, <code>articles/</code> or <code>schemas/</code>, that are listed in its explicit scope, have a merged source commit and a reviewed publication decision with a personal-name rights holder and a licence of CC-BY-4.0 or Apache-2.0. A package therefore maps as follows, as a <strong>proposal</strong> that the exporter’s own rules still decide.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Package element</th>\n<th>Candidate export v2 element</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>document.md</code>, <code>readable.md</code> (only if listed in <code>publishable_layers</code>)</td>\n<td>one entry each: <code>path</code>, <code>content sha256</code>, rights holder, licence, <code>status: current</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>objects and relations</td>\n<td>not carried as JSON; rendered to one Markdown file (<code>render_knowledge_markdown</code>) which becomes one entry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>manifest.json</code>, <code>objects.jsonl</code>, <code>relations.jsonl</code>, <code>edits.jsonl</code>, <code>quality.json</code>, assets</td>\n<td>not carried (not Markdown); stay in the package and are referenced by digest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>rights.holder</code>, <code>rights.license</code></td>\n<td>publication decision <code>rights_holder</code> and <code>license</code>; the exporter requires a personal name as holder and CC-BY-4.0 or Apache-2.0 as licence, so a layer without <code>rights.license</code> gets no row; <code>rights_basis</code>, <code>third_party</code> and <code>attribution</code> are written by the publication step</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>package_status</code>, quality blocks</td>\n<td><code>limits</code> text of the decision (built by <code>candidate_projection</code>); a proposed row has status <code>proposed</code>, and only the exporter’s allowlist makes it <code>current</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>layer not in <code>publishable_layers</code></td>\n<td>no allowlist row; an absent row means deny</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>kc2_admission: not_measured</code></td>\n<td><code>kc2_admission: not_measured</code> (the exporter sets it; a producer cannot)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><code>candidate_projection</code> proposes such rows, with the repository path <code>articles/book-packages/&lt;package_id&gt;/&lt;file&gt;</code>. That path fits the exporter’s path pattern but is not in its scope list. The function does not widen the exporter’s scope: <code>SCOPE_PATHS</code> is an explicit list and widening it, or adding a path pattern for packages, needs a new decision in the programme record. Until then this specification is itself not an indexing input, and the mapping has not been tried against a real exporter run. Admission under <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/\">the core contract specification</a> is a separate step performed by the runtime resolver; a package supplies identity, sources, scope and conditions, and is a candidate until that step records otherwise. The consumer in the workspace (<code>kb_feeder/knowledge_contract.py</code>) re-checks the path shape with its own pattern and refuses everything else, so a file outside the admitted shapes is refused on both sides. Whether it accepts a rendered knowledge layer, or any file of this specification, is <strong>not measured</strong>: no bundle containing such a file exists, and this repository holds no receipt of an earlier staging run, so none is claimed here.</p>\n<h2 id=\"12-validation\">12. Validation</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>The JSON Schema checks the shape of each record and the conditional rules above (origin and generation, contradicts sides, CER null, not-built counts, status coherence, benchmark context, paths, admission constants).</li>\n<li><code>tools/check_book_package.py &lt;dir&gt;</code> adds the cross-file rules: file digests, counts, unique identifiers, endpoint existence, package, work and edition identity, anchor identity, geometry and fit to the source format, ledger chain and replay, staleness coupling, quality counts, status derivation, rights versus layers.</li>\n<li>Positive fixtures: one synthetic package and one object per anchor kind. Negative fixtures: one file per violated rule, regenerated byte for byte by <code>tools/book_package_fixture.py</code>.</li>\n<li>Mutation tests: every negative fixture is rejected for its own named reason, and every cross-file mutation of the valid package is reported with its expected message. A mutation that is accepted, or rejected for another reason, fails the build.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>These checks establish that the validator distinguishes the listed violations. They do not establish that the list is complete, that the fixtures resemble real books, or that a conversion is faithful.</p>\n<h2 id=\"13-limits-of-the-implemented-format\">13. Limits of the implemented format</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The format is exercised only on a synthetic fixture. No real book has been converted by this specification yet.</li>\n<li><code>generation.input_sha256</code>, <code>size_bytes</code> of the source and the existence of an anchored section are recorded but cannot be verified from the package alone.</li>\n<li>A fixture marked <code>reviewed</code> records a review that the fixture does not prove took place.</li>\n<li>Extraction quality and semantic quality are defined as separate blocks; no threshold for either is fixed here.</li>\n<li>Right-to-left scripts, vertical Japanese and ruby are not covered by any anchor refinement; coverage of them is <code>not_measured</code>.</li>\n<li>Whether the consumer accepts the Markdown rendering of a knowledge layer is not measured.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"14-provenance-and-relation-to-the-publication\">14. Provenance and relation to the publication</h2>\n<p>The format, schema, fixtures and validator were introduced in the <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/tree/570f76b6d9a189295c3229254821c30748383965\">upstream book-package change</a>. This edition moves its full specification into the engineering collection, resolves its profile vocabulary and links, and makes the validation boundary explicit. The schema, validator, fixtures and original conformance tests retain their upstream bytes.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/\">source-transcription study</a> supplies related historical evidence about provenance and uncertainty loss. Its historical book experiments are not validation of this newly introduced schema. The <a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">scientific model of semantic objects</a> explains why structural provenance cannot establish the truth of an interpretation.</p>\n<p>Credit and licence: Knowledge Contract Arcana, <a href=\"https://contract.place\" class=\"uri\">https://contract.place</a> (CC BY 4.0). See <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/ATTRIBUTION.md\">attribution</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-validation-boundary","title":"1. Purpose and validation boundary"},{"level":2,"id":"2-layout","title":"2. Layout"},{"level":2,"id":"3-identity-and-source-binding","title":"3. Identity and source binding"},{"level":2,"id":"4-semantic-objects","title":"4. Semantic objects"},{"level":2,"id":"5-relations","title":"5. Relations"},{"level":2,"id":"6-evidence-anchors","title":"6. Evidence anchors"},{"level":2,"id":"7-generated-content-is-marked-always","title":"7. Generated content is marked, always"},{"level":2,"id":"8-quality-report-extraction-and-semantics-apart","title":"8. Quality report: extraction and semantics apart"},{"level":2,"id":"9-staleness","title":"9. Staleness"},{"level":2,"id":"10-rights-and-layers","title":"10. Rights and layers"},{"level":2,"id":"11-mapping-to-candidate-export-v2-and-knowledge-contract-v3-admission","title":"11. Mapping to candidate export v2 and Knowledge Contract V3 admission"},{"level":2,"id":"12-validation","title":"12. Validation"},{"level":2,"id":"13-limits-of-the-implemented-format","title":"13. Limits of the implemented format"},{"level":2,"id":"14-provenance-and-relation-to-the-publication","title":"14. Provenance and relation to the publication"}],"title_id":"book-packages-source-identity-semantic-layers-and-validation","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/book-package/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/specifications/book-package/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"5fb89acc3656fcfd07682e4c4fb91ff58f656ce545c98804fa962f4f85c3948c","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"contract-profile-conformance","kind":"study","type":"study","path":"engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/STUDY.md","route":"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/","title":"Contract profiles: finite conformance, trust boundaries, and a strong simple comparator","titleRu":"Contract profiles: finite conformance, trust boundaries, and a strong simple comparator","summary":"Finite contract checks, false trusted-input counterexamples and an equal strong-comparator result.","language":"en","html":"<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>This study examines whether a finite implementation of a Knowledge Contract follows its declared structural and application rules. The author suite matched 35 of 35 expected ordinary outcomes. A separately composed control suite, prepared within the same research session, matched 26 of 26. These results concern already interpreted JSON instances. The implementation also accepted two author and four control inputs whose trusted annotations were deliberately false. Those acceptances expose a trust boundary and are excluded from the conformance totals. A subsequent strong plain-form checker matched the recorded Knowledge Contract decisions on all nine shared scenarios. The observed result supports the feasibility of the specified checks on the published finite cases; it establishes neither an advantage over a strong simple representation nor the truth of admitted content. The complete retained result records accompany this report.</p>\n<h2 id=\"question-and-model\">Question and model</h2>\n<p>The practical question is whether a consumer can distinguish an adequately bound informational package from one that omits a required condition, uses evidence for another task, or lacks a current supplied permission. The tested carrier is <code>kc-synthesis/0.1</code>. Its nine logical groups represent identity, purpose, content, scope, grounds, dependencies, obligations, projection, and observations. Optional versioned profiles add professional work, dialogue, documents, reasoning, visual delivery, and reuse.</p>\n<p>A profile extends the obligations of the core. It does not override them by appearing later in a file. If admission requires the core condition <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_0</annotation></semantics></math> and applicable profile conditions <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_1,\\ldots,C_n</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>Admit</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><munderover><mo>⋀</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\operatorname{Admit}(x) \\Rightarrow \\bigwedge_{i=0}^{n} C_i(x)\n\\Rightarrow C_0(x).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>This implication follows from the admission definition. Whether software implements that definition correctly is a separate question. Whether an external annotation is true is another. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/evidence/conditional-properties-source.json\">conditional-properties record</a> states these distinctions and supplies counterexamples. Their theoretical setting is developed in <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">Evidence and falsification</a> and <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">Constrained transformations</a>.</p>\n<p>The implementation assumes that objects and relationships have already been interpreted. A supplied capability annotation, source availability, and a current authority decision are inputs. The checker does not independently interview an actor, authenticate an external grant, or establish a source’s factual accuracy. An informational contract may omit the professional profile; an execution request has additional binding requirements. Unknown required profiles block use. Unknown optional profiles can be retained as opaque data with a diagnostic, which does not count as interpreting them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"design-and-unit-of-observation\">Design and unit of observation</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/evidence/evaluation-protocol-source.json\">evaluation protocol</a> distinguishes a scenario from assertions nested inside it. The author prepared the interface and its own suite. A different agent in the same session prepared control intents before inspecting the implementation. After receiving the interface, that agent fixed concrete cases, expected outcomes, and a runner before its first import of the validator implementation. The retained <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/evidence/control-review-source.json\">control review</a> describes this sequence.</p>\n<p>There were 28 initial control intents: 24 ordinary and four trust-boundary stress intents. Interface mapping produced 26 ordinary concrete scenarios and four stress scenarios. The 26 comprise six useful positive admissions, seventeen forbidden admissions, and three migration-preservation cases. One intent could not be mapped and five were only partially represented. These omissions remain part of the scope assessment; they are not additional passed cases.</p>\n<p>The control composition is organizational separation within one session and agent family. It is not an external replication or independent domain review. Cases were deliberately selected to exercise mechanisms, so their proportions do not estimate error rates in a population of real tasks.</p>\n<h2 id=\"results\">Results</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-21\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-28\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-28\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-21\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Evaluation</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Ordinary expected outcomes matched</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">False trusted inputs accepted</th>\n<th>Meaning of the result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Author suite</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">35/35</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2/2</td>\n<td>Finite implementation checks and separately disclosed trust-boundary stress</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Separate control suite</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">26/26</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">4/4</td>\n<td>Exact outcomes on the frozen concrete control cases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Strong plain-form comparison</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">9/9 for each representation</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">Outside this comparison</td>\n<td>Equal decisions on three allowed and six forbidden admissions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These counts come from the exact <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/evidence/author-conformance-report.json\">author report</a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/evidence/separate-control-report.json\">control report</a>, and <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/evidence/strong-plain-form-comparison.json\">strong-comparator report</a>. The reports retain input hashes, case rows, expected outcomes, and observations. Their bytes were recovered from the retained publication reader and checked against its source index; <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/evidence/origin.json\">origin.json</a> records the identities and hashes.</p>\n<p>Positive controls matter. A checker that rejects every input would avoid forbidden admissions but fail useful cases. The six control admissions include a compact informational core, correctly bound work, exact synthetic execution evidence, a current grant despite a different revoked grant, an opaque optional profile, and an eligible reuse instance. Negative cases include missing capability, a mismatched or expired grant, changed requirements, absent declared imagery, unknown required profiles, lost conditions, and evidence bound to another attempt or output.</p>\n<p>The migration cases concern a declared six-field legacy subset. They check retained original values, explicit gaps, and the absence of invented capability or authority. They do not demonstrate lossless migration of every previous contract implementation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"counterexamples-and-the-comparator\">Counterexamples and the comparator</h2>\n<p>The control stress cases deliberately supplied four internally consistent falsehoods: a positive capability annotation, a fabricated grant, false subject content, and an incorrect claim that a source was available. All four were admitted. This is a counterexample to the proposition that passing structural and binding checks establishes real competence, authorization, factual truth, or production access enforcement. It is not a successful semantic test merely because the interface’s expected answer was acceptance.</p>\n<p>Two comparators must also remain distinct. The initially registered diagnostic ablation checked field presence and simple form but omitted the cross-object conditions under investigation. On nine chosen cases it matched three expected outcomes while the contract matched nine. That establishes the effect of removing those checks in that code. It is not a fair estimate of an advantage over a strong task form.</p>\n<p>The subsequent strong checker received the same material information and directly checked current sources, work and requirements revisions, actor assignment, conditions, grants, and required capabilities. It used fixed fields rather than an extensible ontology and profile registry. It matched all nine expected outcomes, exactly as the stored contract run did. Its design followed disclosure of the first results, so it is exploratory. Only the comparator was newly executed in this comparison; the contract side used its preserved first result.</p>\n<p>The equality directly challenges a claim that the larger representation is necessary for these nine decisions. It leaves open different questions about exchange, maintenance, additional profiles, preparation effort, and scale. None of those outcomes was measured here.</p>\n<h2 id=\"claim-test-counterexample-and-limit\">Claim, test, counterexample, and limit</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Claim</th>\n<th>Model or test</th>\n<th>Counterexample or rival explanation</th>\n<th>Supported conclusion and limit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Profile composition preserves core obligations</td>\n<td>Conjunctive admission and required-profile cases</td>\n<td>An implementation silently overrides a core condition</td>\n<td>The defined rule preserves the core; the finite suite checks selected implementations of it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Evidence must refer to the attempted work</td>\n<td>Exact task, requirements, actor, attempt, output, and criterion bindings</td>\n<td>Old evidence has a similar description but a different target</td>\n<td>Tested mismatches are rejected; forged trusted bindings remain outside authentication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>A skill description establishes capability</td>\n<td>Separate description and capability inputs</td>\n<td>A false capability annotation is accepted</td>\n<td>The description alone is insufficient; input truth still needs an external basis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Contract conformance implies source truth</td>\n<td>Schema and consistency checks</td>\n<td>The admitted false-source stress case</td>\n<td>This implication is refuted within the test design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>The contract outperforms a strong simple form</td>\n<td>Nine shared decisions</td>\n<td>The direct checker makes the same decisions</td>\n<td>No advantage is established on this shared domain</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"reproduction-and-evidence-boundaries\">Reproduction and evidence boundaries</h2>\n<p>The accompanying files are sufficient to inspect all recorded results, separate the stress denominator, inspect the comparator decisions, and verify the provenance of retained JSON. The exact original runners, all frozen fixtures, and the entire original execution environment are not included in this study’s evidence folder. Consequently this is a publication of inspectable retained reports, not a claim that this folder alone reruns the historical experiment. Hashes identify artifacts; they do not replace missing code, authenticate an observer, or certify a result’s meaning.</p>\n<p>Rendered prose sources are explicitly identified as text views recovered from the retained HTML. Their original Markdown source hashes are recorded separately from the hashes of those new views. They are not misrepresented as byte-identical Markdown reconstructions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"conclusion\">Conclusion</h2>\n<p>The tested profile performed its declared finite structural and relational checks, including useful positive controls and prohibited admissions. The accepted false trusted inputs demonstrate why source admission, current authority, and semantic assessment need their own evidence. The strong-comparator tie further narrows the contribution: this study supplies a working representation and inspectable finite results, while leaving comparative practical benefit unestablished. These distinctions are prerequisites for a usable contract service, not unfinished text that should be concealed by an undifferentiated success label. Terminology is consolidated in the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"question-and-model","title":"Question and model"},{"level":2,"id":"design-and-unit-of-observation","title":"Design and unit of observation"},{"level":2,"id":"results","title":"Results"},{"level":2,"id":"counterexamples-and-the-comparator","title":"Counterexamples and the comparator"},{"level":2,"id":"claim-test-counterexample-and-limit","title":"Claim, test, counterexample, and limit"},{"level":2,"id":"reproduction-and-evidence-boundaries","title":"Reproduction and evidence boundaries"},{"level":2,"id":"conclusion","title":"Conclusion"}],"title_id":"contract-profiles-finite-conformance-trust-boundaries-and-a-strong-simple-comparator","math_count":3,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/STUDY.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/STUDY.md","source_sha256":"895d9d7f0603027a4b930ea336e3021b6b02f22ca78d989fb665869db642550b","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"dependency-closure-and-package-delivery","kind":"study","type":"study","path":"engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/STUDY.md","route":"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/","title":"Dependency closure, readable artifact layouts, and a pinned package replay","titleRu":"Dependency closure, readable artifact layouts, and a pinned package replay","summary":"Historical package evidence and a public exhaustive replay of a finite dependency graph.","language":"en","html":"<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>A task package is useful only if selecting an artifact also delivers its required conditions and dependencies. This study examines that obligation in a frozen editorial knowledge package with 110 records and twelve skill roots. Its retained selftest matched 46 expected outcomes. The dependency portion exhaustively examined all 4,096 skill subsets and 24,576 one-step inclusion pairs, with no disagreement against a separate fixed-point implementation. A public replay supplied with this report reproduces that graph portion without distributing the source book. A separate migration preserved all 110 artifact payloads while replacing opaque filenames with readable paths. Finally, an exact historical package replay reproduced its expected preliminary decision, <code>needs_resolution</code>, rather than server acceptance. The results establish bounded graph, layout, and integration behavior. They do not establish the meaning of every dependency, the quality of generated prose, or an agent’s competence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-delivery-problem\">The delivery problem</h2>\n<p>An editorial skill can depend on a rule, a condition under which that rule applies, an acceptance criterion, and a source fragment. Selecting only the attractive top-level skill description can omit the qualification that makes its use appropriate. Conversely, following every navigational link can deliver an unnecessarily large and confusing package. The consumer therefore follows explicitly required dependencies and treats navigation separately.</p>\n<p>This study distinguishes three identities: the artifact’s stable identity, the immutable content revision, and its human-readable path. A renamed folder need not create different knowledge. A changed condition cannot be dismissed as a cosmetic rename. The scientific obligations are developed in <a href=\"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/\">Retrieval and context composition</a>, <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">Constrained transformations</a>, and <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">Revision and governance</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"model-and-algorithms\">Model and algorithms</h2>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math> be the fixed finite set of artifact records and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(v)</annotation></semantics></math> the required dependencies of record <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math>. For selected roots <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math>, the intended closure is the least set <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(S)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nS\\subseteq C(S), \\qquad v\\in C(S)\\Rightarrow D(v)\\subseteq C(S).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Only declared <code>required</code> groups with the <code>all</code> operator participate in this tested profile. An alternative-choice dependency would need another selection policy and is not silently interpreted as <code>all</code>. A dependency’s identity, revision, expected type, and payload hash are checked when loading the original package. Unknown roots and cycles cause explicit rejection in the consumer.</p>\n<p>The production-side algorithm performs depth-first traversal with separate active and completed sets. The reference implementation repeatedly computes</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><munder><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nS_{k+1}=S_k\\cup\\bigcup_{v\\in S_k}D(v)\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>until the set stops growing. On a finite graph, the latter terminates after at most <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|V|</annotation></semantics></math> strict increases. The comparison examines agreement, idempotence, monotonicity, and the union equation for this declared dependency relation. These are graph properties; they do not state that the natural-language content has a complete semantic dependency model.</p>\n<h2 id=\"exhaustive-finite-checks\">Exhaustive finite checks</h2>\n<p>Twelve selectable skill roots yield <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>12</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>096</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{12}=4,096</annotation></semantics></math> subsets. The selftest evaluates every subset, compares consumer closure with the separate reference, checks <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(C(S))=C(S)</annotation></semantics></math>, and checks that the closure equals the union of the individual root closures. It also examines every pair formed by adding one absent skill. There are <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>11</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>576</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\cdot2^{11}=24,576</annotation></semantics></math> such pairs; each must satisfy inclusion of the smaller closure in the larger one.</p>\n<p>The retained <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/finite-package-selftest.json\">finite-package selftest</a> records no reference disagreements, idempotence failures, union failures, or monotonicity failures. These exhaustive counts describe this one fixed graph. They are not independent samples of tasks and cannot be converted into a probability that arbitrary knowledge is complete.</p>\n<p>The overall selftest contains 46 checks. Additional controls include stale review bindings, a changed protected number, missing change grounds, a wrong source relation, an omitted critical qualification, another task’s context, stale competency dependencies, attempted execution without supplied capability and authority, package tampering, and immutable receipt behavior. A synthetic positive review fixture is explicitly labeled as synthetic; it is not evidence that a real reviewer judged the text.</p>\n<p>Two intentionally passing boundaries are especially informative. A structurally valid rule can state a false proposition. An output can contain the exact protected numeric string while negating its meaning. The checker accepted those inputs in the respective structural and literal-preservation checks. Therefore neither structural conformance nor exact string presence is a substitute for semantic review.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-public-reproduction-of-the-graph-result\">A public reproduction of the graph result</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/dependency-graph.json\">dependency graph</a> publishes required adjacency data and opaque vertex identities, with no artifact bodies, source passages, OCR, or book images. It was extracted after the original loader checked the frozen package’s referenced records. Its source graph, index, and loader hashes are recorded in the file. The supplied <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/replay_closure.py\">replay program</a> uses the original traversal rule and an independently written fixed-point comparison.</p>\n<p>From this study directory, run:</p>\n<pre class=\"bash\"><code>python3 evidence/replay_closure.py</code></pre>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/public-closure-replay.json\">saved public replay</a> reproduces 4,096 subset comparisons and 24,576 inclusion checks, and rejects an unknown root and a constructed cycle. This is a new replay of the extracted historical graph. It does not pretend to rerun all 46 historical checks, validate the source book, or observe a new model response. No additional Python packages or network calls are required.</p>\n<h2 id=\"readable-layout-migration\">Readable layout migration</h2>\n<p>The later editorial toolkit layout replaced code-like filenames with descriptive paths while retaining the underlying artifact identities and payload revisions. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/layout-preservation-report.json\">preservation report</a> records byte equality for all 110 records and unchanged source bytes. Old and new paths are listed explicitly, so a reader can distinguish relocation from revision.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/layout-conformance-report.json\">layout conformance report</a> records nine successful tests. Its positive inventory includes 110 document passports, 110 records, twenty operational closures, four constraints, 163 Markdown files, 1,294 local links, and 24 adapters. These are different inventory units and are not added into a single success rate. Other checks examine alias equivalence, forty source locators, and delivery of constraints as critical content.</p>\n<p>Negative controls remove a constraint passport, remove a required constraint from delivery, introduce an invalid slug, break a link while updating its file hash, and create disagreement between the index and registry. Each is rejected by the expected diagnostic family. The updated-hash broken-link control is a useful counterexample: internally consistent checksums do not imply usable navigation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"historical-package-replay\">Historical package replay</h2>\n<p>An integration gate was pinned to the earlier package, not to the later layout. Reusing that gate against changed folder membership would compare different targets. The retained <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/historical-package-replay.json\">historical replay</a> instead used the exact earlier target manifest and <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/historical-replay-environment.json\">declared environment</a>. The five command exits matched their expected sequence: 0, 0, 0, 3, 0. The source and temporary replay copy each retained all 239 checked payload files unchanged.</p>\n<p>The exit of 3 belonged to preliminary admission. Its expected decision was <code>needs_resolution</code>, with <code>R_EXPECTED_HEAD</code> as the resolution code. Reproducing that decision means the gate reproduced the specified unresolved condition. It does not mean the package was accepted by a server. The record explicitly reports no new model runs, no new text outcomes, no external semantic review, server admission not evaluated, and production readiness false.</p>\n<p>The recorded environment was Python 3.12.14 on Linux with five exact dependency versions. That environment claim belongs to the pinned historical replay. The later layout also has dependencies with version ranges; matching the earlier environment does not establish an exact later environment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"claim-evidence-and-boundary\">Claim, evidence, and boundary</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Claim</th>\n<th>Evidence</th>\n<th>Counterexample or failure control</th>\n<th>Limit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Required closure follows the declared graph</td>\n<td>Exhaustive subset replay and independent fixed point</td>\n<td>Unknown root and cycle</td>\n<td>Completeness of the declared graph is an external premise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Renaming preserves retained artifact content</td>\n<td>110 exact payload comparisons</td>\n<td>Layout/index disagreement</td>\n<td>Preserved bytes do not prove correct prose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Constraints are actually delivered</td>\n<td>Explicit critical-constraint checks</td>\n<td>Missing passport and missing delivery</td>\n<td>Only the four declared constraints are covered</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>A matching package hash means the package is usable</td>\n<td>Navigation and preliminary-admission checks</td>\n<td>Broken link with updated hash; unresolved expected head</td>\n<td>Integrity, navigation, and admission remain separate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Exact strings imply preserved meaning</td>\n<td>Deliberately negative meaning with the same numeric string</td>\n<td>The literal check accepts it</td>\n<td>A semantic reviewer remains necessary</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"conclusion\">Conclusion</h2>\n<p>This study provides a reproducible finite dependency result and inspectable layout and package-replay records. It shows how readable artifact names can coexist with stable identity, and how constraints can remain visible in the delivered closure. It also preserves the failure boundaries that make these results interpretable: a complete graph traversal may follow an incomplete annotation; an intact package may require resolution; a string-preserving edit may change meaning. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">editorial pilot</a> evaluates actual text separately. Definitions are collected in the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"the-delivery-problem","title":"The delivery problem"},{"level":2,"id":"model-and-algorithms","title":"Model and algorithms"},{"level":2,"id":"exhaustive-finite-checks","title":"Exhaustive finite checks"},{"level":2,"id":"a-public-reproduction-of-the-graph-result","title":"A public reproduction of the graph result"},{"level":2,"id":"readable-layout-migration","title":"Readable layout migration"},{"level":2,"id":"historical-package-replay","title":"Historical package replay"},{"level":2,"id":"claim-evidence-and-boundary","title":"Claim, evidence, and boundary"},{"level":2,"id":"conclusion","title":"Conclusion"}],"title_id":"dependency-closure-readable-artifact-layouts-and-a-pinned-package-replay","math_count":11,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/STUDY.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/STUDY.md","source_sha256":"55029310ea64f27f9ed3ef8470d56815c755820ce45ad43cbe078ca58a12f684","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"source-transcription-and-uncertainty","kind":"study","type":"study","path":"engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/STUDY.md","route":"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/","title":"Reproducible transcription is not preservation of uncertainty","titleRu":"Reproducible transcription is not preservation of uncertainty","summary":"Retained generated descriptions, lost uncertainty and a reproducible synthetic projection counterexample.","language":"en","html":"<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>An audit of three book-export packages found that their readable Markdown could be reconstructed byte for byte while omitting every one of 153 explicitly recorded uncertainty fields attached to generated illustration descriptions. The underlying structured records remained intact. A separate derivative projection preserved all 153 fields and labeled all 917 descriptions as generated and unreviewed; removing only the recorded insertions restored the original readable bytes. This is a completed finite demonstration of a representation defect and its local correction. It does not measure OCR accuracy, validate the descriptions, or establish a benefit of graph storage over a serializer implementing the same rule. This report separates the corpus observations, later export integration, unresolved trace defects, and a small public synthetic reproduction of the preservation mechanism.</p>\n<h2 id=\"question-and-source-scope\">Question and source scope</h2>\n<p>The question is whether a transformation delivers the qualifications attached to the content a reader actually receives. Preserving the source archive is necessary for audit, but it does not make an omitted qualification visible in a derived document. The scientific distinction is developed in <a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">Semantic objects and context</a> and <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">Constrained transformations</a>.</p>\n<p>The studied corpus covered cooking, marine navigation, and electrical automation. The retained source audit identifies a 1952 edition of <em>Книга о вкусной и здоровой пище</em>, G. I. Fain’s 1977 <em>Навигация, лоция и мореходная астрономия</em>, and V. A. Timofeev’s 1964 <em>Сто схемных задач по автоматике</em>. Dates and page counts describe the observed editions. They do not establish present applicability of advice or authorize practical use.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-20\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-26\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-26\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-26\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Export family</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Page anchors</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Imported generated descriptions</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Nonempty uncertainty fields</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Cooking</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">456</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">374</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">111</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Navigation</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">289</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">319</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">16</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Electrical automation</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">154</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">224</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">26</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Total</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">899</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">917</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">153</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/structural-corpus-report.json\">structural corpus report</a> supports the counts. Pages, descriptions, and uncertainty fields are different units. They are not 1,969 independently verified observations, and a page anchor is not an expert judgment on a page.</p>\n<h2 id=\"audit-and-transformation-method\">Audit and transformation method</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/corpus-audit-source.json\">corpus audit</a> examined 39 files, thirteen per export. For 33, the export receipt contained expected sizes and hashes, all of which matched. The remaining README and receipt files were pinned by the observer; no receipt was treated as authenticating its own bytes.</p>\n<p>The observer reconstructed the readable output from the source document, editing ledger, and structured illustration records. The target readable text was used for comparison rather than inserted into the generated output. The reconstruction covered 174 recorded edits, seven italic-wrapper mappings in the navigation export, three caption-to-image-alt copies, and the generated descriptions. All three readable files matched the retained targets byte for byte.</p>\n<p>The mapping was developed after inspecting the available examples. It is a known-data reconstruction, not a blind qualification of the producer. Every replayed editing operation retained its unreviewed status. Reversibility established what the operation did to the representation; it did not establish that the editorial change was linguistically correct.</p>\n<p>The important counterexample appeared in the illustration projection. The readable template emitted description, purpose where present, and recognized image text. It did not emit the separately stored <code>uncertain</code> field. All 153 nonempty values were absent from the corresponding readable descriptions. This finding concerns that exact field and projection. It does not assert that every description lacked all cautious wording, or that the JSON source had been destroyed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"preserving-uncertainty-as-a-testable-obligation\">Preserving uncertainty as a testable obligation</h2>\n<p>For a record <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math> with an explicit qualification <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(r)</annotation></semantics></math>, the relevant requirement is that the delivered projection contain the qualification, or explicitly declare the permitted loss. Byte identity of a different object does not satisfy that requirement. A projection can be perfectly reproducible and consistently omit the same field.</p>\n<p>The corrective derivative therefore introduced a narrow rule. After each generated description, it inserted the declared generated/unreviewed status. When <code>uncertain</code> was nonempty, it inserted the exact field value with an explicit label. Every insertion carried an input row, record hash, asset identity, anchor, and output range. The source text and original readable files were preserved.</p>\n<p>The transformation profile and expected counts were fixed before generation, after the audit had revealed the defect. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/uncertainty-projection-source.json\">derivative report</a> records the outcome:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Obligation</th>\n<th>Observed result</th>\n<th>Boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Preserve generated/unreviewed status</td>\n<td>917/917 descriptions labeled</td>\n<td>The labels do not independently assess the description</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Preserve each nonempty uncertainty value</td>\n<td>153/153 exact values delivered</td>\n<td>These are unreviewed model statements, not calibrated probabilities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Preserve the previous readable representation</td>\n<td>Removing only declared insertions restores all three original files</td>\n<td>The experiment concerns this specified transformation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Keep source inputs unchanged</td>\n<td>All 39 retained source-file hashes unchanged</td>\n<td>Integrity is distinct from source correctness</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>No new OCR, subject model call, or human domain review occurred in this transformation. Imported model descriptions were already present in the exports. The zero new-call count must not be read as a claim that the corpus contains no model-produced material.</p>\n<h2 id=\"domain-specific-consequences\">Domain-specific consequences</h2>\n<p>The three domains exposed different dependencies. In a cooking instruction, a qualification can belong to one ingredient branch or a later serving operation. Merging branches or moving an action can alter the procedure even if familiar words survive. In navigation, a table or diagram can depend on a legend, a sign convention, a preceding page, and applicability to a particular instrument. Exact numerical transcription alone does not deliver those conditions. In an electrical diagram, visible connections can support a qualitative description while numerical outputs remain unknown without input values and operating context.</p>\n<p>These examples motivated engineering obligations; they are not new cooking, navigation, or circuit-design recommendations. The later <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">projection study</a> evaluates selected tasks involving such dependencies. Neither study treats a historical book’s age as an automatic proof of error, or as an adequate reason to authorize contemporary action.</p>\n<h2 id=\"trace-defects-and-later-integration\">Trace defects and later integration</h2>\n<p>The audit also found unresolved addressability. Of 5,538 quality flags, the observed mapping addressed 5,518; sixteen table or formula offsets and four orphan flags remained unresolved. A disappearing flag was not counted as repaired OCR. It could reflect changed text, changed diagnostics, or missing provenance.</p>\n<p>A separate raw-to-document analysis reproduced all recorded correction rows in the cooking and automation exports. For navigation, 53 of 58 rows were reproduced, leaving five rows in two blocks without a unique raw-serialization binding. Those rows were not labeled five OCR errors. The evidence was insufficient to reconstruct the producer’s transformation, which is a different finding.</p>\n<p>Later local integration processed the same 917 description records in two runs. Each recorded 19,305 satisfied field checks and no violated field checks, preserving 153 nonempty and 764 empty uncertainty values. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/local-export-integration-report.json\">integration summary</a> also preserves the initial control result of fifteen expected outcomes out of sixteen. One authored control supplied pretty JSON where JSONL was required, causing an earlier-than-intended rejection. Its separately retained correction exercised the intended missing-uncertainty case. A post-replacement control distinguished visibility of a validated new snapshot from unconfirmed durability.</p>\n<p>This integration occurred after export. It was not installed in the upstream OCR producer. Physical power-loss durability was not tested. Seven truncated descriptions formed a targeted review queue; the other nonempty uncertainty fields were not automatically equated with defective or truncated outputs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"public-reproduction-and-retained-evidence\">Public reproduction and retained evidence</h2>\n<p>The source books, OCR transcriptions, extracted illustrations, and full generated book descriptions are not redistributed here. Project-authored reports and aggregate metadata are provided under readable filenames, with exact JSON identities and separate rendered-prose provenance in <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/origin.json\">origin.json</a>. Full replay of the original corpus audit requires the specified source packages and original runner closure; the reports alone do not supply them.</p>\n<p>A <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/replay_uncertainty_projection.py\">self-contained public program</a> uses four newly invented records, two with nonempty uncertainty. It reproduces the logical counterexample: base bytes can be reconstructed exactly while both uncertainty fields are absent. The corrective projection preserves both fields, marks all four records, records insertion ranges, rejects an absent uncertainty field, and reversibly restores the base. Run:</p>\n<pre class=\"bash\"><code>python3 evidence/replay_uncertainty_projection.py</code></pre>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/public-projection-demonstration.json\">saved demonstration</a> is a new synthetic illustration. Its four records are not substituted for the 917 historical descriptions, and its checks are not added to the historical corpus totals.</p>\n<h2 id=\"limits-and-conclusion\">Limits and conclusion</h2>\n<p>The corpus’s CER and WER values remained unmeasured. <code>null</code> does not mean zero error. The audit did not independently inspect all image bytes, verify every OCR passage, or calibrate model uncertainty. Structural graph construction created addressable source structure; it did not thereby extract a verified semantic knowledge graph.</p>\n<p>The established engineering result is precise: the chosen readable template lost an explicitly represented qualification field; a declared and reversible projection preserved it on the inspected finite corpus. A simple serializer can implement that rule. The evidence supports an explicit preservation obligation and supplies a concrete counterexample to equating hashes with meaning preservation. It does not establish superiority of the larger architecture. Related terms are defined in the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"question-and-source-scope","title":"Question and source scope"},{"level":2,"id":"audit-and-transformation-method","title":"Audit and transformation method"},{"level":2,"id":"preserving-uncertainty-as-a-testable-obligation","title":"Preserving uncertainty as a testable obligation"},{"level":2,"id":"domain-specific-consequences","title":"Domain-specific consequences"},{"level":2,"id":"trace-defects-and-later-integration","title":"Trace defects and later integration"},{"level":2,"id":"public-reproduction-and-retained-evidence","title":"Public reproduction and retained evidence"},{"level":2,"id":"limits-and-conclusion","title":"Limits and conclusion"}],"title_id":"reproducible-transcription-is-not-preservation-of-uncertainty","math_count":2,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/STUDY.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/STUDY.md","source_sha256":"721553450ac3bf61f1db252a7cc53a76f92478ab77eb56c67937a7077dc5b266","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"executable-projections-and-revision","kind":"study","type":"study","path":"engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/STUDY.md","route":"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/","title":"Executable projections, addressable revisions, and the limits of graph minimality","titleRu":"Executable projections, addressable revisions, and the limits of graph minimality","summary":"Finite consumer checks, paired agent tasks, addressed revision and the limits of graph minimality.","language":"en","html":"<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>This report synthesizes three completed development stages: packet reading, targeted revision, and executable partial extraction. The first reader stage preserved twelve criterion violations. A later qualified boundary suite satisfied 772 declared checks across 38 calls under a revised reader and rubric. A targeted revision comparison produced equal outcomes in all thirty graph-versus-row pairs. Finally, a consumer qualified on 102 finite controls extracted 24 prescribed projections from ten fresh agent responses organized as five related case pairs. Both representations succeeded on all five primary cases under the frozen rubric. The results support bounded preservation of specified identities, conditions, evidence roles, and history. They do not establish graph superiority or general semantic completeness. A post-hoc finding shows that a minimal set of reachable entities can still contain unnecessary claims inside entity text.</p>\n<h2 id=\"question-and-design-progression\">Question and design progression</h2>\n<p>The engineering question is whether a consumer can retrieve a useful partial result without silently dropping its conditions, treating a new assessment as original provenance, or resolving an ambiguous identity by arbitrary order. The scientific arguments are developed in <a href=\"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/\">Retrieval and context composition</a>, <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">Revision and governance</a>, and <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">Evidence and falsification</a>.</p>\n<p>The stages changed interfaces, mappings, cases, and sometimes evaluation rules. They are therefore reported as related development studies. Their successive scores do not constitute a controlled estimate of improvement caused by one isolated intervention. Historical results remain unchanged.</p>\n<h2 id=\"initial-reader-four-distinct-failure-mechanisms\">Initial reader: four distinct failure mechanisms</h2>\n<p>The retained <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/evidence/historical-reader-study-source.json\">initial reader study</a> records 57 main-layer calls with 786 satisfied and twelve violated criteria, plus eighteen book-layer calls with 252 satisfied and zero violated criteria. These are nested criterion judgments, not 1,050 independent tasks or an accuracy estimate. There were no native-model subject trials or human-subject trials in that stage.</p>\n<p>The twelve violations fell into four groups. Five concerned the difference between an address resolving to a placeholder and content actually being available. Three concerned inconsistent derivation of revision identity between records and relation endpoints. Two concerned diagnostic pointers into a packet versus its transport envelope. Two concerned choosing the first candidate despite conflicting records.</p>\n<p>This analysis matters because the violations do not all mean lost content. In the pointer cases, the invalid outer hash was detected, but the diagnostic coordinate system was ambiguous. In the placeholder cases, relationships were retained while the meaning of <code>resolved</code> differed between reader and oracle. Revising the explanation of these mechanisms does not erase the recorded violations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"revised-reader-boundaries\">Revised reader boundaries</h2>\n<p>The later reader separated address resolution, content state, content availability, applicability, and authority. An empty supplied string, zero, or <code>false</code> was not treated as an absent field. A source-declared revision remained distinct from an inferred revision and its inference rule. Diagnostic pointers named their coordinate space and representation hash. Conflicting candidates remained an explicit set, so record order could not silently create unique identity.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/evidence/reader-boundary-report.json\">boundary report</a> contains 38 calls: 26 prospective development counterexamples and twelve known earlier cases. All calls satisfied the declared checks, totaling 772 satisfied and zero violated criteria. A paired permutation control preserved ambiguity and the candidate-value hash set. The revised mapping and rubric differed from the earlier ones, so this was not an instrument-only retest. It qualified these declared boundaries; it did not prove correctness on arbitrary future packets.</p>\n<h2 id=\"targeted-revision-and-a-strong-alternative\">Targeted revision and a strong alternative</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/evidence/targeted-revision-comparison.json\">targeted revision report</a> compares an explicit dependency graph with flat rows that contain the same material dependencies and conditions. Both use a shared evidence validator and serializer. The experimental difference is how affected records are selected. This is a deliberately strong simple alternative, not a form deprived of relevant information.</p>\n<p>Thirty cases generated sixty executions. They covered no change, valid and invalid representation maps, missing and empty maps, source-text and image-byte changes, and new requirements to preserve an uncertainty field. All sixty produced the expected outcome. The two representations agreed in all thirty pairs. The outputs included 36 unresolved-mapping assessments and twelve unknown-contract assessments because those were the declared outcomes of particular controls. These states are visible results, not failures concealed behind the pass total.</p>\n<p>Source changes created separate candidate revisions and retained prior records and history. New assessments did not invent current-use authority. Timing was a single local invocation including shared decoding, not model latency or product cost. This comparison establishes no advantage for graph representation on the tested scope.</p>\n<h2 id=\"executable-projection-contract\">Executable projection contract</h2>\n<p>The subsequent finite consumer accepts two representations of the same contract: nodes and typed edges, or rows with typed dependencies. It normalizes both and computes required closure from named roots. Relations distinguish content requirements, qualifications, and assessment requirements. Source attachments also distinguish content support from assessment basis. Evidence used to evaluate a card must not silently become the source from which the card’s content was derived.</p>\n<p>Typed identities carry record and revision identifiers, the contract of creation, and prior-version references. Assessments separately identify their exact target, assessment contract, and verdict. A request can include historical revisions in a separate history layer. Historical payloads and outgoing relations are preserved rather than edited to make an old record satisfy new rules.</p>\n<p>Missing addresses, invalid sources, malformed input, or a closure exceeding the declared entity budget yield a blocked result with no usable partial output under this profile. An explicitly represented semantic unknown can remain useful content; it is not itself a schema failure. Minimality is relative to the declared relationships. The consumer cannot infer an omitted qualification merely because the author should have included it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"consumer-qualification-before-the-new-responses\">Consumer qualification before the new responses</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/evidence/consumer-qualification-report.json\">qualification report</a> records 51 control families in two forms: 102 executions, comprising 24 expected acceptances and 78 expected blocks. All matched, and normalized results agreed in all 51 pairs. A separate verifier, which did not import the consumer, computed expected structural closure and rejected twelve deliberately damaged outputs.</p>\n<p>Three pre-freeze corrections are disclosed. Invalid JSON types gained explicit guards; Python’s equality between booleans and numbers could no longer mask a change in preserved JSON type; and normalized outputs explicitly retained relations and typed links. These changes preceded the new subject responses. The finite controls do not prove the consumer for every possible input or arbitrary non-JSON Python object.</p>\n<h2 id=\"five-agent-case-pairs\">Five agent case pairs</h2>\n<p>The next stage fixed inputs, requests, reference material, rubric, implementations, and run order before obtaining ten responses in fresh agent contexts. There was one response per case and representation, with no corrective subject retry. The source-based tasks were deliberately related and comprise four source families, not ten independent samples.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Case</th>\n<th>Required preservation in a partial extraction</th>\n<th>Observed result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Unreviewed navigation description</td>\n<td>Useful description together with uncertain notation and missing context</td>\n<td>Both main projections retained the uncertainty and source limit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Experimental cooking revision</td>\n<td>A changed branch, its prior value, and a distinct independent branch</td>\n<td>Prescribed current, historical, and separate-branch extractions retained their respective bindings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Changed assessment contract</td>\n<td>Original creation contract and prior verdict, new assessment, and a new qualified revision</td>\n<td>Old provenance remained intact; new assessment and revision were separate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Navigation table</td>\n<td>Selected values, interpolation basis, sign, and unknown applicability</td>\n<td>Both forms retained the specified values and grounds in all prescribed views</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Electrical diagram image</td>\n<td>Visible block and connection structure without invented numerical signals</td>\n<td>Both forms retained qualitative observations and limits of numerical inference</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/evidence/agent-projection-results.json\">measured-results record</a> reports 24 available and independently structurally verified projections. Each received the rubric’s highest fidelity and task-correctness score. All prescribed views succeeded in five of five cases for each form, with equal primary outcomes in all five pairs. The ten raw responses were different; equal outcome scores do not imply identical representations.</p>\n<p>The semantic reviewer saw the representation, participated in design review, and belonged to the same agent family. No human expert review occurred. There were 84 nested critical-obligation judgments; these are not 84 independent experiments. Exact runtime model revision, decoding, seed, token use, latency, and compute cost were unavailable. Saved outputs were copied from visible final answers rather than captured as native API streams. Therefore the study supplies an observed agent case series, not a reproducible native-model benchmark.</p>\n<h2 id=\"counterexample-to-claim-level-minimality\">Counterexample to claim-level minimality</h2>\n<p>After evaluation, two row-form projections were found to include extra numerical statements inside a shared applicability qualification. An extraction of one selected table value consequently carried another value even though the latter’s dedicated calculation node was not selected. The extra values were correct, and the frozen rubric allowed additional correct information. The original scores were retained; the observation was recorded separately.</p>\n<p>This is a counterexample to inferring claim-level minimality from entity-level closure. A minimal reachable node set does not imply that every sentence inside those nodes is necessary, or that every incidental claim has all of its own grounds in the projection. A later contract could give individual claims addresses or constrain the scope of shared qualifications. That refinement is an implication of the counterexample, not a property already demonstrated by this run.</p>\n<h2 id=\"evidence-and-reproduction-boundaries\">Evidence and reproduction boundaries</h2>\n<p>The exact machine summaries and retained source views accompany this study; <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/evidence/origin.json\">origin.json</a> distinguishes original JSON bytes from reconstructed text views. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/evidence/offline-projection-replay-report.json\">offline replay report</a> records one historical extracted-bundle replay in which all 24 projections matched and passed a separate verifier. It explicitly reports no new subject-model calls, OCR, native trials, or semantic assessments. That replay is deterministic processing of saved outputs.</p>\n<p>This public evidence folder does not contain the complete original source packages, all candidate responses and frozen requests, or the complete native runtime. Readers can inspect the retained counts, conditions, hashes, and limitations; they cannot infer a complete historical replay environment from those hashes. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">public closure study</a> provides a separately executable graph mechanism using metadata-only inputs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"conclusion\">Conclusion</h2>\n<p>The studies demonstrate finite preservation of explicitly declared content, qualifications, assessment roles, and history through selected retrieval and revision operations. They also identify useful counterarguments: simple representations can reach the same outcomes, a corrected evaluator changes the interpretation of scores, and graph minimality does not bound the number of claims inside text. These results support the explicit obligations of the contract while keeping comparative effectiveness, arbitrary-root completeness, domain correctness, and product authorization outside the established conclusion. Definitions are consolidated in the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"question-and-design-progression","title":"Question and design progression"},{"level":2,"id":"initial-reader-four-distinct-failure-mechanisms","title":"Initial reader: four distinct failure mechanisms"},{"level":2,"id":"revised-reader-boundaries","title":"Revised reader boundaries"},{"level":2,"id":"targeted-revision-and-a-strong-alternative","title":"Targeted revision and a strong alternative"},{"level":2,"id":"executable-projection-contract","title":"Executable projection contract"},{"level":2,"id":"consumer-qualification-before-the-new-responses","title":"Consumer qualification before the new responses"},{"level":2,"id":"five-agent-case-pairs","title":"Five agent case pairs"},{"level":2,"id":"counterexample-to-claim-level-minimality","title":"Counterexample to claim-level minimality"},{"level":2,"id":"evidence-and-reproduction-boundaries","title":"Evidence and reproduction boundaries"},{"level":2,"id":"conclusion","title":"Conclusion"}],"title_id":"executable-projections-addressable-revisions-and-the-limits-of-graph-minimality","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/STUDY.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/STUDY.md","source_sha256":"cfe6ab67c6ce6ed6295a5ec7b1b421c25f3b9f4e132daadb7f1ccfa8d7865bd3","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"reuse-admission-and-cost-controls","kind":"study","type":"study","path":"engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/STUDY.md","route":"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/","title":"Controlled reuse: admission counterexamples and the cost of a cache hit","titleRu":"Controlled reuse: admission counterexamples and the cost of a cache hit","summary":"Executable admission counterexamples and an analytical break-even calculation with explicit costs.","language":"en","html":"<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>Reusing a prepared knowledge artifact can avoid work, but a matching identifier or a high hit rate does not establish that reuse is appropriate or economical. This analytical study develops explicit admission conditions and counterexamples involving missing dependencies, changed search scope, current authority, time boundaries, and overlapping cost claims. A homogeneous cost model gives an exact break-even condition. Newly supplied public code reproduces the arithmetic and finite logical examples with invented inputs. It does not report measured model savings, production throughput, or a deployed authorization system. The completed contribution is an inspectable set of obligations and counterexamples that an implementation can test, together with a precise account of which empirical conclusions require further observations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"question-and-objects\">Question and objects</h2>\n<p>The question is when a retained artifact can be used for a new request without losing its requirements, overstating its evidence, or hiding the cost of validation. The scientific models are developed in <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">Controlled knowledge reuse</a> and <a href=\"/en/science/knowledge-utility/\">Knowledge utility</a>. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/evidence/reuse-properties-source.json\">retained conditional-properties source</a> records the original assumptions, arguments, and counterexamples behind this analysis.</p>\n<p>A build can be represented as</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>Compile</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\operatorname{Compile}(b,q,S)\\rightarrow(a,D,E,L),\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math> is a recipe, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math> the request, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math> the declared source snapshot, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math> the prepared revision, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math> its material dependencies, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math> evidence, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math> coverage, loss, and stopping information. A later request <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> requires a separate reuse decision under a current policy and context. Similarity may retrieve a candidate; it does not establish suitability.</p>\n<p>Build dependencies explain how an artifact was obtained. Admission dependencies govern whether it can now be supplied or applied. A changed access decision can prohibit delivery without changing the artifact’s text. A changed generator version need not require rebuilding every unaffected artifact. These decisions require explicit relevance rules rather than one undifferentiated freshness flag.</p>\n<h2 id=\"admission-before-optimization\">Admission before optimization</h2>\n<p>Let each mandatory gate return <code>pass</code>, <code>fail</code>, or <code>unknown</code>. Full admission requires <code>pass</code> for every applicable gate. A missing mandatory gate is not an implicit pass. Relevant gates include integrity, result class, task requirements, material dependency equivalence, current access, temporal policy, permitted transformations, modality delivery, and budget.</p>\n<p>Partial content can be admissible when the request explicitly permits it and the gaps remain visible. That does not make the conditions attached to the selected content optional. High predicted utility also cannot turn a failed mandatory condition into permission. Economic optimization occurs within the set of admissible alternatives.</p>\n<p>Useful outcomes include exact reuse, instantiation of a template, reuse after revalidation, rebuilding, bypassing the cache, and rejection. A decision to revalidate is not itself permission to serve before the necessary check completes. An instantiated result has its own identity and must be checked after substitution.</p>\n<h2 id=\"counterexamples-that-determine-the-design\">Counterexamples that determine the design</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Proposed shortcut</th>\n<th>Counterexample</th>\n<th>Required distinction</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Listed dependencies are unchanged, so the result is preserved</td>\n<td>An omitted legend changes the meaning of an unchanged number or symbol</td>\n<td>Dependency completeness is a substantive premise, not a manifest label</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>A negative answer has an empty dependency set</td>\n<td>A new matching item is added after an exhaustive search returned none</td>\n<td>Absence, count, and exact top-k depend on search-domain membership and policy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>A previously accepted payload can be sent to another requester</td>\n<td>The requester, tenant, resource scope, or permission has changed</td>\n<td>Content identity and current delivery authority are separate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>A live cache lifetime overrides an unavailable authority service</td>\n<td>A permission was revoked while the authority result became unknown</td>\n<td>Unknown current authority does not inherit an old success by default</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>A cached completion message completes another task</td>\n<td>The message describes an earlier attempt and external effect</td>\n<td>Retained content is not a new execution event</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Frequent reads prove continuing validity</td>\n<td>Repeated hits add no source assessment</td>\n<td>Access statistics and validation time are separate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The preservation claim is necessarily conditional. Suppose a finite property set <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on a complete declared dependency interface <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math>, relevant inputs and interpretation remain equivalent, and transformation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math> preserves those properties. Under these premises, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(a)</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(T(a))</annotation></semantics></math> in the new declared scope. Omitting an actual dependency invalidates the premise. Accepting a Boolean field called <code>complete_manifest</code> does not establish completeness for natural-language content.</p>\n<p>Suitability is also directional. A complete nonempty set may support an existential answer through an allowed projection. One known member does not establish the whole set, its count, or absence of other members. A symmetric similarity score cannot supply this missing implication.</p>\n<h2 id=\"time-concurrency-and-state\">Time, concurrency, and state</h2>\n<p>Artifact lifecycle, residency, freshness, and current eligibility are different assessments. A valid retained revision may be cold or temporarily unavailable. A resident fresh artifact may be inaccessible to this requester. A historical answer can deliberately use a pinned old revision while still respecting current access restrictions.</p>\n<p>A strict-current policy needs a declared point at which relevant source heads and authority are considered current. A bounded-stale policy needs an explicit allowed scope and age. Neither policy may silently ignore known withdrawal, corruption, incompatibility, or a material unresolved gap. A cache hit updates usage statistics; it does not reset the time when an underlying source was checked.</p>\n<p>There is also a gap between checking and delivery. A permission or dependency can change after validation but before the result is supplied. An implementation must specify its consistency boundary, for example through a checked epoch, lease, or transactional publication protocol. Those mechanisms need their own concurrency tests. The analytical obligation here does not claim that a distributed service has already implemented them correctly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"an-exact-cost-model\">An exact cost model</h2>\n<p>Consider homogeneous requests with equal admissible quality and non-overlapping accounting categories. Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of requests, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math> the cost of the cold admissible path, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math> additional candidate-search and admission cost per request, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R</annotation></semantics></math> the remaining cost of a usable reuse, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math> the fraction of usable reuses. Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">MG</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math> be preparation, additional background builds, invalidation, and storage costs. All are in one declared unit and nonnegative. Then</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nC_0=NG,\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nC_1=B+NV+NhR+N(1-h)G+MG+I+S.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Writing <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=B+MG+I+S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=h(G-R)-V</annotation></semantics></math>, subtraction gives</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nC_0-C_1=Nd-F.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Strict savings therefore require <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Nd&gt;F</annotation></semantics></math>. For fixed expected <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the first profitable integer request count is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⌊</mo><mi>F</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⌋</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lfloor F/d\\rfloor+1</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\leq0</annotation></semantics></math>, nonnegative fixed costs cannot be recovered through more requests in this model. For actual observations, use the integer hit count <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_N</annotation></semantics></math>: savings are <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_N(G-R)-NV-F</annotation></semantics></math>. A fractional expected hit count is not an observed event count.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Invented scenario</th>\n<th>Parameters</th>\n<th>Algebraic result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>High hit rate with expensive validation</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=100,h=0.9,G=10,R=1,V=9,F=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Cold cost 1,000; reuse cost 1,090; loss 90</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Positive margin with preparation cost</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=100,h=0.9,G=10,R=1,V=1,F=100</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Cold cost 1,000; reuse cost 390; savings 610</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Zero margin</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=100,h=0.5,G=10,R=2,V=4,F=100</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>The fixed loss of 100 remains</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are exact arithmetic examples in invented cost units. They are not prices, measured token bills, or estimates of the project’s performance. Heterogeneous requests require an appropriately stratified sum rather than substitution of incompatible averages.</p>\n<h2 id=\"utility-and-double-counting\">Utility and double counting</h2>\n<p>Compute savings alone are not net task value. Quality loss, additional review, delayed outcomes, and downstream harm require an explicit utility policy and evidence. If their values are unknown, assigning zero would create an unsupported benefit. If a route violates a hard constraint, a favorable monetary estimate does not admit it.</p>\n<p>Costs also need a shared baseline. Suppose a cold operation costs ten units, provider caching reduces it to six, and using a prepared artifact reduces it to one. The two separately calculated savings, four and nine, cannot be added into thirteen units saved from a ten-unit operation. If the artifact hit eliminated the model call, a provider-cache saving on that nonexistent call is not another observed benefit. Resource events, retries, storage, and shared infrastructure need non-overlapping accounting or an explicit allocation rule.</p>\n<p>No inference about causal contribution follows merely from counting citations, delivered artifacts, or completed tasks. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">editorial pilot</a>, for example, observed usable outputs under both conditions and a tie. That result cannot be turned into measured economic benefit without resource and outcome observations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reproduction-and-claim-limits\">Reproduction and claim limits</h2>\n<p>The public <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/evidence/replay_reuse_counterexamples.py\">counterexample program</a> uses exact rational arithmetic and small invented dependency and admission models. It verifies the three cost identities, the strict expected break-even threshold, a high-hit-rate loss, an omitted-dependency counterexample, a changed-domain negative-answer counterexample, and rejection of six failed or unknown gate mutations alongside a positive control.</p>\n<pre class=\"bash\"><code>python3 evidence/replay_reuse_counterexamples.py</code></pre>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/evidence/public-reuse-counterexamples.json\">saved result</a> is a new deterministic demonstration for this edition. It does not rerun an earlier production system. The program’s gate values are supplied inputs, not authenticated authorization. The original property source is a retained rendered-text view with separately recorded source and representation hashes in <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/evidence/origin.json\">origin.json</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"conclusion\">Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Controlled reuse requires two decisions: whether a route satisfies its obligations, and whether it is preferable among admissible routes. The logical counterexamples identify inputs that cannot be omitted from the first decision. The cost identity specifies what must be measured for the second. The completed analytical result is useful precisely because it permits a negative answer: a cache can be correct but uneconomical, economical under invented assumptions but unmeasured in practice, or superficially efficient while inadmissible. Terminology is consolidated in the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"question-and-objects","title":"Question and objects"},{"level":2,"id":"admission-before-optimization","title":"Admission before optimization"},{"level":2,"id":"counterexamples-that-determine-the-design","title":"Counterexamples that determine the design"},{"level":2,"id":"time-concurrency-and-state","title":"Time, concurrency, and state"},{"level":2,"id":"an-exact-cost-model","title":"An exact cost model"},{"level":2,"id":"utility-and-double-counting","title":"Utility and double counting"},{"level":2,"id":"reproduction-and-claim-limits","title":"Reproduction and claim limits"},{"level":2,"id":"conclusion","title":"Conclusion"}],"title_id":"controlled-reuse-admission-counterexamples-and-the-cost-of-a-cache-hit","math_count":38,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/STUDY.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/STUDY.md","source_sha256":"87af4b10639cf33fe29c4e19d06cd99b5460a41db6bdd70863c08fa3d5f6c07d","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"editorial-skill-pilot","kind":"study","type":"study","path":"engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/STUDY.md","route":"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/","title":"Editing a scientific section with a knowledge toolkit: a masked single-section comparison","titleRu":"Editing a scientific section with a knowledge toolkit: a masked single-section comparison","summary":"One preregistered editorial comparison, exact initial outputs, masked review and an observed tie.","language":"en","html":"<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>Two agents edited the same 804-word Russian scientific section under a common brief. One used the general brief alone; the other used an editorial toolkit containing task-specific skills and a process blueprint. Before the responses, the experiment fixed nineteen protected claims, eight conceptual distinctions, exact literals, source material, and a five-axis rubric. A separate agent reviewed anonymized outputs before their conditions were revealed. Both preserved the declared claims and distinctions, passed the editorial acceptance gate, and received eight points out of ten. The preference was a tie. A separate technical audit found a consistent trace linking the toolkit-assisted changes to declared rules and source ranges. The pilot therefore demonstrates an operational editing and traceability process on one real section. It does not demonstrate better writing than a strong common brief, isolate a causal effect of individual skills, or validate the scientific theory being edited.</p>\n<h2 id=\"question-and-scope\">Question and scope</h2>\n<p>The user’s practical question was whether newly created writing skills and a blueprint could guide an agent through a real publication task while preserving meaning. The selected unit was the complete working-definitions section of the project introduction: 804 words by whitespace counting, four subsections, and a table with eight distinctions. The input was Russian, so this pilot does not evaluate English translation quality. The toolkit was derived from Maxim Ilyakhov and Lyudmila Sarycheva’s <em>Пиши, сокращай</em>; the <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#editorial-input\">bibliographic and input-identity note</a> identifies the work and the supplied representation without redistributing it.</p>\n<p>The trial separated editorial quality from truth of the underlying scientific content. An editor could improve references, paragraph structure, and sentence clarity without establishing the correctness of a formal claim. The relevant scientific framing appears in <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">Constrained transformations</a>, <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">Evidence and falsification</a>, and <a href=\"/en/science/knowledge-utility/\">Knowledge utility</a>.</p>\n<p>The exact <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/source-section.txt\">source section</a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/source-context.txt\">shared context</a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/common-editorial-brief.txt\">common brief</a>, and <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/original-pilot-protocol.txt\">original protocol</a> are retained as historical text records. Their internal paths and terminology describe the original experiment; the present publication’s navigation is provided by this report.</p>\n<h2 id=\"design-and-preregistration\">Design and preregistration</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/editorial-preregistration.json\">preregistration</a> predates the editor agents. It pins the inputs, identifies two conditions, permits one first completed response per condition, and specifies review with condition labels hidden until the written evaluation. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/protected-claims.json\">protected-claim registry</a> contains nineteen claims, protected headings and anchors, link targets, numbers, versions, and 37 exact literals.</p>\n<p>Both agents received the same input and brief in fresh task contexts. The baseline condition edited without reading the toolkit. The assisted condition used the readable-layout editorial package, five declared applied skills, and the process blueprint for editing existing text. The general brief already required meaning preservation, so the baseline was a substantive editorial alternative rather than an instruction to shorten text indiscriminately.</p>\n<p>After the first responses were saved, another agent received the source and variants labeled alpha and beta without the editing logs or condition mapping. It issued the <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/masked-editorial-review.json\">masked review</a>. The subsequent <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/condition-label-reveal.json\">label reveal</a> records alpha as baseline and beta as toolkit-assisted and states that review was completed before reveal. This is masking of condition labels, not proof that the reviewer could not infer a method from stylistic differences. All roles operated within the same agent environment; there was no independent human editorial panel.</p>\n<h2 id=\"results-on-the-first-completed-responses\">Results on the first completed responses</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-27\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-36\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-36\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Observation</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Common-brief baseline</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Toolkit-assisted editing</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Protected claims preserved</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">19/19</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">19/19</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Conceptual table distinctions preserved</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">8/8</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">8/8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Exact protected literals present</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">37/37</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">37/37</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Headings / anchors / link destinations</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">5 / 5 / 3</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">5 / 5 / 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Sentence clarity, 0–2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>References and terminology, 0–2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Coherence, 0–2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Economy without loss, 0–2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">1</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Scientific genre and reader, 0–2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">1</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Total assigned by the reviewer</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">8/10</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">8/10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Words by whitespace</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">830</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">820</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Editorial decision</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">Accepted within the declared scope</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">Accepted within the declared scope</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/editorial-comparison-results.json\">comparison result</a>, exact <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/baseline-edit.txt\">baseline response</a>, and exact <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/skill-assisted-edit.txt\">assisted response</a> accompany the report. The original <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/editorial-mechanical-checks.json\">mechanical check</a> verifies hashes, literal presence, and document structure. The semantic preservation counts and stylistic scores come from the recorded agent review, not the mechanical checker.</p>\n<p>Both responses were slightly longer than the original. Reduction in word count was not the declared target. Replacing compressed references with explicit nouns can increase length while reducing the reader’s effort to reconstruct a reference. Consequently this trial supplies no general rule that successful editing must make a text shorter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-the-edits-changed\">What the edits changed</h2>\n<p>The assisted response made some list references explicit. Instead of asking the reader to connect the first, second, and third items to an earlier list, it named source context, interaction context, and application context directly. It also replaced a vague plural referent with the already defined object being described. These changes clarified existing relationships without adding another theoretical claim.</p>\n<p>The section discussing a contract was divided into thematic paragraphs addressing the contract and package, version history, work objects, and the boundary of the research. Some technically dense material was preserved because its distinctions were protected. The masked reviewer identified local strengths in both variants rather than a consistent overall advantage. A repeated list remained in the baseline; a long contract definition remained in the assisted version. Neither was repaired after comparison to manufacture a better first-response result.</p>\n<h2 id=\"skill-use-and-technical-trace\">Skill use and technical trace</h2>\n<p>The assisted route went through a brief, request, compiled Knowledge Contract, proposed edit, review, and registration. Five skills were declared applied: defining the editing task, precise rewording, preserving numbers and conditions, structuring sentences and paragraphs, and reviewing the result with its source trace. Required dependencies caused twelve skills and 108 records to be delivered. The other seven skills were not counted as applied merely because they were loaded.</p>\n<p>The separate <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/editorial-trace-audit.json\">technical audit</a> records twelve groups of material changes, twenty change grounds, and seven process grounds. It checked 49 unique input/output locators and 24 unique source ranges. Quotes, character and UTF-8 offsets, and line bindings matched, and all changed non-whitespace characters were covered by the recorded trace. It also checked agreement among request, input, contract, selected context, output, assessment, and receipt bindings.</p>\n<p>These observations establish consistency and inspectability of the particular trace. They do not reveal every internal action of the editor or prove that a cited rule caused a wording choice. The source ranges included private third-party material and are not redistributed as excerpts in this publication. The published audit is the project’s report of those checks, with a pinned source identity; it is not a replacement for independent access to the underlying book.</p>\n<h2 id=\"counterarguments-and-boundaries\">Counterarguments and boundaries</h2>\n<p>The main rival explanation is that a sufficiently good common brief already supplied what this section needed. The observed tie is consistent with that explanation. It does not prove that the toolkit is useless on other tasks, but it prevents this pilot from being cited as evidence of superiority.</p>\n<p>A second counterargument is that a complete-looking trace may accompany an incorrect edit. Mechanical equality, literal presence, and a consistent receipt do not establish meaning preservation. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">package-delivery study</a> supplies an explicit negative example in which the protected numeric string remains while the sentence’s meaning is negated. The editorial review addresses that distinct question here, within one section and one reviewer’s judgment.</p>\n<p>A third boundary concerns terminology. At the time of the pilot, the editors recorded an ambiguity between two spellings of the work-item entity rather than silently deciding that they denoted different things. The author subsequently clarified that the spellings refer to one entity. The present <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a> uses that decision. Frozen pilot inputs and scores retain their historical bytes; they are not retroactively rewritten as if the clarification preceded the experiment.</p>\n<p>There was one section and one first completed response per condition. Scores are qualitative rubric judgments, not calibrated population measurements. The trial did not record token usage, cost, or latency. It did not isolate the contribution of each skill, the blueprint, or the surrounding compiler. Native command-line clients were not used. The underlying scientific claims and all referenced literature were not revalidated by this editorial exercise.</p>\n<h2 id=\"public-record-checks\">Public record checks</h2>\n<p>The supplied <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/replay_editorial_checks.py\">replay checker</a> rechecks the public retained texts against the frozen source hash, original response hashes, exact literals, headings, anchor order, and link destinations:</p>\n<pre class=\"bash\"><code>python3 evidence/replay_editorial_checks.py</code></pre>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/public-editorial-record-check.json\">saved check</a> confirms those mechanical properties. It deliberately does not compute semantic fidelity or reproduce the eight-point ratings. Complete model-runtime identity and the full private toolkit source closure are not supplied, so executing this checker is not a new agent trial. <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/origin.json\">origin.json</a> pins the distributed records and distinguishes original data from newly generated check results.</p>\n<h2 id=\"conclusion\">Conclusion</h2>\n<p>The assisted editorial process worked on the selected real section: it produced an accepted text and an inspectable chain from task to changes and review. The baseline also worked, with the same protected-claim result and score. This completed pilot supports a narrow operational claim about usable process and traceability. A claim of better writing, lower cost, or improved scientific correctness requires a different and adequately observed comparison. The present result remains valuable without changing the tie into an advantage.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"question-and-scope","title":"Question and scope"},{"level":2,"id":"design-and-preregistration","title":"Design and preregistration"},{"level":2,"id":"results-on-the-first-completed-responses","title":"Results on the first completed responses"},{"level":2,"id":"what-the-edits-changed","title":"What the edits changed"},{"level":2,"id":"skill-use-and-technical-trace","title":"Skill use and technical trace"},{"level":2,"id":"counterarguments-and-boundaries","title":"Counterarguments and boundaries"},{"level":2,"id":"public-record-checks","title":"Public record checks"},{"level":2,"id":"conclusion","title":"Conclusion"}],"title_id":"editing-a-scientific-section-with-a-knowledge-toolkit-a-masked-single-section-comparison","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/STUDY.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/STUDY.md","source_sha256":"3e3a185a836fdc4c7bb6a0e13bafed7205e59e4687a70cddc7b648983146bbe4","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"glossary","kind":"glossary","type":"glossary","path":"reference/glossary/GLOSSARY.md","route":"/en/reference/glossary/","title":"Knowledge Contract Glossary","titleRu":"Knowledge Contract Glossary","summary":"Shared English definitions, Russian equivalents, technical spellings and distinctions between related concepts.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0 · <strong>Maintainer:</strong> Pavel Valentov</p>\n<p>This glossary defines the preferred vocabulary shared by the scientific papers and engineering specifications. Human-readable terms use ordinary words. Code identifiers remain literal when an interface requires them. A Russian equivalent helps trace the earlier manuscripts; it does not create a second entity.</p>\n<h2 id=\"knowledge-and-its-representation\">Knowledge and its representation</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Preferred term</th>\n<th>Russian equivalent</th>\n<th>Definition and boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Knowledge Contract</td>\n<td>Контракт знаний</td>\n<td>A versioned specification of knowledge, intended use, grounds, dependencies, constraints and assessment obligations. Its existence does not establish truth or authorize execution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Semantic object</td>\n<td>Смысловой объект</td>\n<td>An addressable, versioned interpretation with content, attribution, context and grounds. A retrieval chunk is a representation for search and need not have the same boundaries.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Source</td>\n<td>Источник</td>\n<td>Material from which an interpretation or observation is derived. Source authorship, authenticity, fidelity and truth are separate questions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Source representation</td>\n<td>Представление источника</td>\n<td>A particular version of text, image, table, audio or other material.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Source fragment</td>\n<td>Фрагмент источника</td>\n<td>A region addressed within a specified source representation. Its locator must state the relevant selection convention.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Interpretation</td>\n<td>Интерпретация</td>\n<td>A reading of material under stated assumptions. Several readings can remain possible.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Attribution</td>\n<td>Атрибуция</td>\n<td>An association between addressed content and the person or system that stated, interpreted or assessed it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Provenance</td>\n<td>Происхождение</td>\n<td>The trace of entities, activities and responsible agents involved in producing a record. Traceability alone does not prove correctness.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Ground</td>\n<td>Основание</td>\n<td>A source, observation, rule or argument that supports a specified claim under declared assumptions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Context</td>\n<td>Контекст</td>\n<td>Conditions needed to interpret or use content: referents, scope, representation, time, exceptions, recipient and other relevant circumstances.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Applicability</td>\n<td>Применимость</td>\n<td>Suitability of a particular interpretation or artifact for a named use under current or explicitly historical conditions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Constraint</td>\n<td>Ограничение</td>\n<td>A rule or condition restricting admissible interpretation, transformation, delivery or action. It is an explicit contract obligation, not a synonym for an acceptance criterion.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Protected property</td>\n<td>Сохраняемое свойство</td>\n<td>A specified observation or relation that a transformation is required to preserve.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Projection</td>\n<td>Проекция</td>\n<td>A selected representation for a purpose, with included and omitted units, transformation rules and losses made explicit.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Loss</td>\n<td>Потеря</td>\n<td>Information or distinctions not preserved by a transformation within a declared observation model.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"requirements-work-and-agency\">Requirements, work and agency</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Preferred term</th>\n<th>Russian equivalent</th>\n<th>Definition and boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Work Item</td>\n<td>Единица работы</td>\n<td>One goal-bearing unit with versioned requirements, constraints, assignments, attempts and acceptance criteria. <code>WorkItem</code> is the code spelling of this same concept, not another type.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Requirement</td>\n<td>Требование</td>\n<td>A condition the requested result or process must satisfy in a specified revision and scope.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Acceptance criterion</td>\n<td>Критерий приёмки</td>\n<td>A rule for deciding whether an observed result satisfies a named requirement. It must bind to the actual output and attempt.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Role</td>\n<td>Роль</td>\n<td>A named responsibility and decision scope. A role definition is distinct from an assignment to an executor.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Skill</td>\n<td>Навык</td>\n<td>A reusable description of a method or procedure and the conditions for using it. A <code>SKILL.md</code> file supplies instructions, not evidence that its reader can perform them.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Competency</td>\n<td>Компетенция</td>\n<td>A specified or assessed ability to achieve a class of results under stated conditions. A competency definition and evidence about a particular executor remain distinct records.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Capability</td>\n<td>Способность исполнителя</td>\n<td>The ability available to an identified executor in an actual environment, established to the degree supported by its assessment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Blueprint</td>\n<td>Схема решения</td>\n<td>A reusable process or structural arrangement for achieving a result. It connects components, steps, dependencies, choices, constraints and acceptance obligations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Assignment</td>\n<td>Назначение</td>\n<td>A binding of an executor to a responsibility or Work Item under a specified scope.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Authority</td>\n<td>Полномочие</td>\n<td>The legitimate scope of decisions or actions supplied by an identified authorization basis.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Grant</td>\n<td>Разрешение</td>\n<td>A concrete authorization with subject, action, resource, purpose, conditions and validity scope.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Attempt</td>\n<td>Попытка исполнения</td>\n<td>One addressed execution attempt bound to a Work Item, requirements, contract, actor and inputs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Outcome</td>\n<td>Исход</td>\n<td>The result of an operation under its declared contract: success, partial, refused, failed or unresolved.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Effect observation</td>\n<td>Наблюдение эффекта</td>\n<td>What is established about an external change caused by an attempt. A timeout can leave this unknown.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"retrieval-composition-and-reuse\">Retrieval, composition and reuse</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Preferred term</th>\n<th>Russian equivalent</th>\n<th>Definition and boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Candidate</td>\n<td>Кандидат</td>\n<td>A potentially useful retrieved item awaiting the checks needed for a particular use.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Coverage</td>\n<td>Покрытие требований</td>\n<td>Established support for the required slots of a named request at a named stage. Presence of a keyword is not semantic fulfilment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Search assurance</td>\n<td>Полнота процедуры поиска</td>\n<td>A statement about the search procedure within its declared scope, separate from coverage of the selected answer.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Mandatory dependency</td>\n<td>Обязательная зависимость</td>\n<td>A relation requiring another pinned unit or condition for the specified use.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Dependency closure</td>\n<td>Замыкание зависимостей</td>\n<td>The least set containing selected roots and all their active mandatory dependencies within the declared graph. It cannot recover unrecorded edges.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Context package</td>\n<td>Пакет контекста</td>\n<td>Versioned selected knowledge with requirements, grounds, dependency closure, coverage, projection and resource account for a particular use.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Compiled artifact</td>\n<td>Скомпилированный артефакт</td>\n<td>A saved derivative of an extraction, selection, transformation or composition, with its producing recipe and dependencies.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Build recipe</td>\n<td>Рецепт сборки</td>\n<td>The declared inputs, transformation versions, parameters and material environment used to produce an artifact.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Content identity</td>\n<td>Идентичность содержимого</td>\n<td>Equality of exact bytes or canonical values within a declared hash domain.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Lookup identity</td>\n<td>Ключ поиска кандидата</td>\n<td>A descriptor used to find a reusable candidate. Matching it does not establish eligibility.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Admission</td>\n<td>Допуск к применению</td>\n<td>A decision that specified requirements and constraints permit a particular use under an identified policy and trust boundary.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Freshness</td>\n<td>Актуальность по времени</td>\n<td>Satisfaction of a specified temporal policy, distinct from semantic applicability and authorization.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Retention</td>\n<td>Срок хранения</td>\n<td>A storage policy for preserving bytes, distinct from how long their use remains valid.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Invalidation</td>\n<td>Пересмотр пригодности</td>\n<td>A change that requires eligibility or a derived state to be withdrawn or reconsidered. It need not delete the historical bytes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Serve boundary</td>\n<td>Момент фактической выдачи</td>\n<td>The declared consistency point at which a selected result is actually released to a recipient.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Knowledge utility</td>\n<td>Полезность знания</td>\n<td>Task-, policy- and horizon-dependent benefit relative to a stated baseline and cost model. It is not an intrinsic scalar property of a document.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"evidence-versions-and-review\">Evidence, versions and review</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Preferred term</th>\n<th>Russian equivalent</th>\n<th>Definition and boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Evidence</td>\n<td>Свидетельство</td>\n<td>An addressed observation or record supporting a specified claim under a method and trust boundary. An assertion that a test passed is weaker than inspectable test outputs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Assessment</td>\n<td>Оценка</td>\n<td>A method-bound judgment about a particular subject revision and property.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Counterexample</td>\n<td>Контрпример</td>\n<td>A case violating the conclusion of a stated claim while satisfying its relevant premises, or exposing that a needed premise was omitted.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Conformance</td>\n<td>Соответствие спецификации</td>\n<td>Satisfaction of specified structural or behavioral requirements in a declared scope. It does not establish every empirical benefit claimed for a system.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Reproduction</td>\n<td>Воспроизведение</td>\n<td>Running an available procedure again on its stated inputs and comparing outputs under declared criteria. Inspecting a retained report is not a rerun.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Replication</td>\n<td>Независимое повторение</td>\n<td>A separately conducted evaluation of a claim with its own implementation or observations. Independence must be described, not inferred from a different filename or agent role.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Revision</td>\n<td>Редакция объекта</td>\n<td>An immutable identified content state.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Edition</td>\n<td>Издание корпуса документов</td>\n<td>A coherent version of publications and their reading manifest. It is independent of a runtime protocol or service version.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Representation profile</td>\n<td>Профиль представления</td>\n<td>A versioned encoding and interpretation convention for a kind of artifact.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Current head</td>\n<td>Текущая выбранная редакция</td>\n<td>A mutable selection within an identified namespace and policy. A historical current marker does not prove present currency.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Adoption decision</td>\n<td>Решение о принятии</td>\n<td>A governance act accepting a norm or publication within an authority’s scope. It is not a truth predicate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Internal agent review</td>\n<td>Внутренняя агентная рецензия</td>\n<td>A recorded assessment by an agent, with its actual input, method and limitations. It is not independent external peer review.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Unknown</td>\n<td>Не установлено</td>\n<td>The available observations do not establish a value or verdict. It cannot be replaced with false, absent or pass without a justified rule.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Withheld</td>\n<td>Не раскрывается</td>\n<td>A value is not disclosed under a restriction. This does not assert that the value is absent.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"editorial-rules-for-terminology\">Editorial rules for terminology</h2>\n<p>Use <strong>Work Item</strong> in English prose and <code>WorkItem</code> only for a literal identifier. Use “skill,” “competency,” “capability,” “role,” and “authority” only with the distinctions above. “Validated,” “verified,” “complete,” “current” and “independent” require an explicit subject and scope. Introduce an abbreviation only if repeated use saves meaningful reading effort.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/\">artifact registry</a> defines file formats. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/\">status specification</a> defines operational values. The <a href=\"/en/appendices/source-map/\">source map</a> retains older names and identifiers solely for traceability.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"knowledge-and-its-representation","title":"Knowledge and its representation"},{"level":2,"id":"requirements-work-and-agency","title":"Requirements, work and agency"},{"level":2,"id":"retrieval-composition-and-reuse","title":"Retrieval, composition and reuse"},{"level":2,"id":"evidence-versions-and-review","title":"Evidence, versions and review"},{"level":2,"id":"editorial-rules-for-terminology","title":"Editorial rules for terminology"}],"title_id":"knowledge-contract-glossary","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/reference/glossary/GLOSSARY.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/reference/glossary/GLOSSARY.md","source_sha256":"1c6be362e0d83b5f85f0c8b39b082780096beb0c8e3220728cf2832e36795814","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"bibliography","kind":"bibliography","type":"bibliography","path":"reference/bibliography/BIBLIOGRAPHY.md","route":"/en/reference/bibliography/","title":"Primary Sources and Research Foundations","titleRu":"Primary Sources and Research Foundations","summary":"Named primary sources and the exact conceptual or technical role each plays in the current edition.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0 · <strong>Bibliographic verification:</strong> 4 November 2026</p>\n<p>The entries below identify the primary sources actually used by the current edition. They distinguish established results and external formats from this project’s own synthesis. A cited source supplies the stated conceptual or technical foundation; it does not endorse Knowledge Contract or establish the effectiveness of its implementation. No third-party book text or source-page images are reproduced in this publication.</p>\n<h2 id=\"provenance\">Provenance</h2>\n<p>Luc Moreau and Paolo Missier, editors. <strong>PROV-DM: The PROV Data Model.</strong> W3C Recommendation, 30 April 2013. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/2013/REC-prov-dm-20130430/\">Dated specification</a>.</p>\n<p>Used for the distinction among entities, activities and agents in provenance, and for the separation of provenance from a judgment about content. The project adds task-specific applicability and admission obligations; those are not claims made by PROV-DM on its behalf.</p>\n<h2 id=\"skos\">SKOS</h2>\n<p>Alistair Miles and Sean Bechhofer, editors. <strong>SKOS Simple Knowledge Organization System Reference.</strong> W3C Recommendation, 18 August 2009. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/2009/REC-skos-reference-20090818/\">Dated specification</a>.</p>\n<p>Used for concepts, labels and knowledge-organization relations. A shared label or concept relation is not a proof of semantic equivalence or truth.</p>\n<h2 id=\"partial-correctness\">Partial correctness</h2>\n<p>C. A. R. Hoare. <strong>An Axiomatic Basis for Computer Programming.</strong> <em>Communications of the ACM</em> 12(10), 1969, 576–580, 583. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1145/363235.363259\">DOI: 10.1145/363235.363259</a>. <a href=\"https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/publications/title/Tony.Hoare.html\">Author’s institutional bibliography</a>.</p>\n<p>Used for the established idea of reasoning about programs through preconditions, postconditions and inference rules. The transformation paper adapts conditional preservation reasoning to its explicitly defined relations. Its composition argument is not presented as a novel replacement for program logic or a proof of termination.</p>\n<h2 id=\"submodular-maximization\">Submodular maximization</h2>\n<p>George L. Nemhauser, Laurence A. Wolsey and Marshall L. Fisher. <strong>An Analysis of Approximations for Maximizing Submodular Set Functions—I.</strong> <em>Mathematical Programming</em> 14, 1978, 265–294. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01588971\">DOI: 10.1007/BF01588971</a>.</p>\n<p>Used for the classical greedy approximation result under normalized, monotone submodular value and a cardinality limit. The context-composition paper states those assumptions and explains why complementary knowledge units can violate submodularity. The guarantee is not transferred to arbitrary budgets, penalties or semantic quality functions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"retrieval-augmented-generation\">Retrieval-augmented generation</h2>\n<p>Patrick Lewis, Ethan Perez, Aleksandra Piktus, Fabio Petroni, Vladimir Karpukhin, Naman Goyal, Heinrich Küttler, Mike Lewis, Wen-tau Yih, Tim Rocktäschel, Sebastian Riedel and Douwe Kiela. <strong>Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks.</strong> <em>Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems</em> 33, 2020. <a href=\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2020/hash/6b493230205f780e1bc26945df7481e5-Abstract.html\">Conference publication</a>. <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401\">Author manuscript</a>.</p>\n<p>Used as the primary reference for generation conditioned on retrieved material. The context-composition paper studies explicit coverage and preservation obligations; it does not claim to have replicated the cited experiments or surpassed their models.</p>\n<h2 id=\"computational-complexity\">Computational complexity</h2>\n<p>Richard M. Karp. <strong>Reducibility among Combinatorial Problems.</strong> In <em>Complexity of Computer Computations</em>, 1972, 85–103. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4684-2001-2_9\">DOI: 10.1007/978-1-4684-2001-2_9</a>.</p>\n<p>Used as the primary source for the classical complexity context of set cover. The current paper gives its own explicit mapping from a finite covering problem to a restricted context-selection instance. This does not imply that every practical instance is hard or that finite exact search is impossible.</p>\n<h2 id=\"causal-inference\">Causal inference</h2>\n<p>Judea Pearl. <strong>Causal Inference in Statistics: An Overview.</strong> <em>Statistics Surveys</em> 3, 2009, 96–146. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1214/09-SS057\">DOI: 10.1214/09-SS057</a>. <a href=\"https://escholarship.org/uc/item/1sv6k47m\">Author’s institutional copy</a>.</p>\n<p>Used for the distinction between observed association and causal conclusions under assumptions about interventions and confounding. The utility paper therefore distinguishes measured task scores from identified causal benefits and does not treat a small exploratory comparison as a population estimate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"http-caching\">HTTP caching</h2>\n<p>Roy T. Fielding, Mark Nottingham and Julian Reschke, editors. <strong>HTTP Caching.</strong> RFC 9111, Internet Standard, June 2022. <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9111.html\">RFC 9111</a>.</p>\n<p>Used as a reference for HTTP cache controls and freshness concepts. Knowledge applicability, semantic dependency closure and action authorization remain additional project obligations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"stale-http-responses\">Stale HTTP responses</h2>\n<p>Mark Nottingham. <strong>HTTP Cache-Control Extensions for Stale Content.</strong> RFC 5861, Informational, May 2010. <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5861.html\">RFC 5861</a>.</p>\n<p>Used as a clearly limited analogy for stale-while-revalidate and stale-if-error behavior. This document is informational, and its HTTP mechanisms do not authorize reuse of revoked knowledge or actions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"requirement-language\">Requirement language</h2>\n<p>Scott Bradner. <strong>Key Words for Use in RFCs to Indicate Requirement Levels.</strong> RFC 2119, BCP 14, March 1997. <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2119.html\">RFC 2119</a>.</p>\n<p>Barry Leiba. <strong>Ambiguity of Uppercase vs Lowercase in RFC 2119 Key Words.</strong> RFC 8174, BCP 14, May 2017. <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8174.html\">RFC 8174</a>.</p>\n<p>Engineering specifications use capitalized MUST, MUST NOT, SHOULD, SHOULD NOT and MAY in this sense. Their force concerns conformance to the stated project specification; it is not a claim that the specification is an IETF standard.</p>\n<h2 id=\"agent-artifact-formats\">Agent artifact formats</h2>\n<p>Agent Skills maintainers. <strong>Agent Skills Specification.</strong> <a href=\"https://agentskills.io/specification\">Official specification</a>, consulted 4 November 2026.</p>\n<p>Used for skill-directory naming, the required <code>SKILL.md</code> file, its <code>name</code> and <code>description</code> metadata, and supported extension metadata. The project defines its own additional reading structure while preserving compatibility with that format.</p>\n<p>AGENTS.md maintainers. <strong>AGENTS.md.</strong> <a href=\"https://agents.md/\">Official format description</a>, consulted 4 November 2026.</p>\n<p>Used for repository instructions to coding agents. The format is ordinary Markdown and does not prescribe a universal schema for agent identities, competencies, roles or blueprints. The project’s formats for those artifacts are explicitly its own conventions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"experimental-source-books\">Experimental source books</h2>\n<p>These books identify the historical materials used by the engineering studies. They are not sources for a new scientific theorem and their practical advice is not endorsed by this publication. The retained export identities, rather than the catalogue pages alone, identify the actual inputs that were processed.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Книга о вкусной и здоровой пище</strong> (<em>The Book of Tasty and Healthy Food</em>). Ministry of Food Industry of the USSR. Moscow: Pishchepromizdat, 1952. <a href=\"https://rusneb.ru/catalog/000199_000009_005811805/\">National Electronic Library record</a>. Used as the cooking export in the <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/\">transcription study</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Навигация, лоция и мореходная астрономия</strong> (<em>Navigation, Pilotage, and Nautical Astronomy</em>). G. I. Fain. Moscow: Transport, 1977. <a href=\"https://rusneb.ru/catalog/000199_000009_007594442/\">National Electronic Library record</a>. Used as the navigation export; the <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/corpus-audit-source.json\">retained corpus audit</a> identifies the processed representation.</li>\n<li><strong>Сто схемных задач по автоматике</strong> (<em>One Hundred Circuit Problems in Automation</em>). V. A. Timofeev. Leningrad: Sudostroenie, 1964. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/corpus-audit-source.json\">retained corpus audit</a> identifies the automation export. The historical engineering tasks examine representations and preservation, not modern operational certification.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"editorial-input\">Editorial input</h3>\n<p>Maxim Ilyakhov and Lyudmila Sarycheva. <strong>Пиши, сокращай: Как создавать сильный текст</strong> (<em>Write and Shorten: How to Create Strong Text</em>). Alpina Publisher. <a href=\"https://alpinabook.ru/catalog/book-pishi-sokrashchay-2025/\">Publisher’s listing</a>.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">editorial pilot</a> used the project’s skills derived from a supplied OCR representation of this work. The source record describes the OCR bibliographic line as fourth edition, Moscow 2024, 400 printed pages; the supplied filename uses “2025,” and the digital input contains 427 physical page entries. These are retained input descriptors, not a claim that page counts or filenames prove the edition. The publisher’s current listing identifies the work but does not authenticate the exact privately supplied file. The publication distributes the pilot’s own texts and evaluations, not the book or the private source-derived toolkit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"project-sources-and-evidence\">Project sources and evidence</h2>\n<p>Pavel Valentov. <strong>Knowledge Contract and Meaning Management: earlier publication corpus.</strong> Repository snapshot <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/tree/b50243b28e9ad7376e28c749a21479ef46ec9d60\">b50243b28e9ad7376e28c749a21479ef46ec9d60</a>.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/appendices/source-map/\">source map</a> records the migration from fourteen earlier paper files and ten specifications to the current reading structure. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/evidence-index.json\">engineering evidence index</a> binds individual empirical or analytical claims to their supporting records. Each study describes what can be rerun publicly, what is available only as a retained report, and what remains unmeasured.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"provenance","title":"Provenance"},{"level":2,"id":"skos","title":"SKOS"},{"level":2,"id":"partial-correctness","title":"Partial correctness"},{"level":2,"id":"submodular-maximization","title":"Submodular maximization"},{"level":2,"id":"retrieval-augmented-generation","title":"Retrieval-augmented generation"},{"level":2,"id":"computational-complexity","title":"Computational complexity"},{"level":2,"id":"causal-inference","title":"Causal inference"},{"level":2,"id":"http-caching","title":"HTTP caching"},{"level":2,"id":"stale-http-responses","title":"Stale HTTP responses"},{"level":2,"id":"requirement-language","title":"Requirement language"},{"level":2,"id":"agent-artifact-formats","title":"Agent artifact formats"},{"level":2,"id":"experimental-source-books","title":"Experimental source books"},{"level":3,"id":"editorial-input","title":"Editorial input"},{"level":2,"id":"project-sources-and-evidence","title":"Project sources and evidence"}],"title_id":"primary-sources-and-research-foundations","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/reference/bibliography/BIBLIOGRAPHY.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/reference/bibliography/BIBLIOGRAPHY.md","source_sha256":"ff0f152231f03bdda4cecce9395f0a2814fc570a559e15398f214b8d0f3f294f","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"source-map","kind":"appendix","type":"appendix","path":"appendices/source-map/APPENDIX.md","route":"/en/appendices/source-map/","title":"Source Map, Earlier Editions, and Protocol Compatibility","titleRu":"Source Map, Earlier Editions, and Protocol Compatibility","summary":"The migration from the earlier corpus, immutable history, evidence provenance and unchanged protocol versions.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0 · <strong>Source audit date:</strong> 4 November 2026</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-this-edition-changes\">1. What this edition changes</h2>\n<p>The earlier repository contained fourteen short English paper entry pages, fourteen much longer Russian manuscripts, ten English specification entries and ten Russian specification texts. The long texts combined scientific arguments, domain studies, iteration notes and references to supporting records outside the public release.</p>\n<p>This edition reorganizes those materials into eight complete English scientific papers, eleven engineering specifications, six engineering studies, a common glossary and bibliography, and two supporting appendices. Scientific results, normative requirements and observations are now separately identifiable. The prior iteration histories remain recoverable at an immutable repository revision instead of appearing as repeated handoff sections in the current reading path.</p>\n<p>The exact prior publication snapshot is <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/tree/b50243b28e9ad7376e28c749a21479ef46ec9d60\">b50243b28e9ad7376e28c749a21479ef46ec9d60</a>. The prior site snapshot is <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/contract-place-site/tree/64ddd981296cb65390beecd802f1682618a7f080\">64ddd981296cb65390beecd802f1682618a7f080</a>. These references are historical evidence, not aliases for the mutable main branches.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-scientific-crosswalk\">2. Scientific crosswalk</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Earlier manuscript</th>\n<th>Current scientific home</th>\n<th>Material separated into engineering</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Introduction</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">Semantic objects and context</a></td>\n<td>Publication and implementation inventory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Semantic objects and extraction</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">Semantic objects and context</a></td>\n<td>Source transcription and uncertainty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Context and applicability</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">Semantic objects and context</a></td>\n<td>Dependency graphs and delivery checks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Transformations and transfer</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">Constrained transformations</a></td>\n<td>Executable projections and revision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Query, retrieval and coverage</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/\">Retrieval and context composition</a></td>\n<td>Package coverage and finite search behavior</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Budgeted context composition</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/\">Retrieval and context composition</a></td>\n<td>Actual package sizes and closure replays</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Dialogue and learning</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/interaction-and-memory/\">Interaction and memory</a></td>\n<td>Task and revision bindings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Reasoning and evidence</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">Evidence and falsification</a></td>\n<td>Checker trust boundaries and counterexamples</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Documents and memory</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/interaction-and-memory/\">Interaction and memory</a></td>\n<td>Readable projections and editorial pilot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Work, roles, skills and Knowledge Contract</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">Controlled knowledge reuse</a> and <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">revision and governance</a></td>\n<td>Core, artifact registry and contract-profile conformance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Revision and canon</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">Revision and governance</a></td>\n<td>Versioned rules and executable revision studies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Evaluation and falsification</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">Evidence and falsification</a></td>\n<td>Full retained results, adverse cases and comparator ties</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Compilation, caching and reuse</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">Controlled knowledge reuse</a></td>\n<td>Compiled artifacts, invalidation and reuse policy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Knowledge utility and resource economics</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/knowledge-utility/\">Knowledge utility</a></td>\n<td>Reuse-admission and cost controls</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The numerical filenames of the earlier edition are retained only as compatibility links. They lead to the current scientific home and to their exact earlier English and Russian texts. Consolidation is topical: it does not silently convert an untested historical proposal into a proved claim. A historical claim not supported by the present argument or evidence remains a historical proposal, accessible in the source snapshot.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-engineering-crosswalk\">3. Engineering crosswalk</h2>\n<p>The ten earlier specification subjects map one-to-one to these current files:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Earlier subject</th>\n<th>Current specification</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Knowledge Contract v3</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/\">Core model and admission</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Contract family</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/contract-family/\">Knowledge operations</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Semantic types</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/semantic-types/\">Semantic types and representations</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Context package</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/context-package/\">Coverage, dependencies and delivery</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Compiled artifact contract</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract/\">Compiled artifacts and derivation</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Artifact registry</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/\">Readable names and document formats</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Status model</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/\">Assessment, execution and publication states</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Cache identity and invalidation</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/\">Identity and invalidation</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Reuse policy</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/reuse-policy/\">Time, authorization and outcomes</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Knowledge evolution contract</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution/\">Versioned rules</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The additional <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/book-package/\">book-package specification</a> incorporates the schema, validator and synthetic fixtures added in upstream revision <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/tree/570f76b6d9a189295c3229254821c30748383965\">570f76b6d9a189295c3229254821c30748383965</a>, which is the integration base of this edition. Its implemented checks and remaining unmeasured properties are stated separately. All upstream schema, validator and fixture bytes are preserved.</p>\n<p>Engineering studies contain the practical domain material, including the historical cooking, navigation and electrical examples. They distinguish publicly replayable procedures from inspectable retained reports and unavailable third-party source corpora. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/evidence-index.json\">evidence index</a> records claim-level support and limits; a navigational link alone is not an empirical justification.</p>\n<h2 id=\"protocol-compatibility\">Protocol compatibility</h2>\n<p>Publication edition <strong>1.0</strong> identifies this set of documents. It is not a renamed runtime protocol. The following identifiers are preserved where historical records need them:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Identifier</th>\n<th>Actual scope</th>\n<th>Compatibility statement</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>3.0.0-draft.1</code></td>\n<td>Earlier target Knowledge Contract architecture</td>\n<td>No stable protocol release is inferred from the editorial edition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>kc-synthesis/0.1</code></td>\n<td>Bounded interpreted-JSON core/profile implementation</td>\n<td>Its retained results do not implement all requirements of the current architecture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>mm-envelope/0.1</code></td>\n<td>Common carrier used by the historical registry and examples</td>\n<td>The carrier and its 34 artifact types are neither an exhaustive taxonomy of knowledge nor a limit on later contracts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>i16-compiled-artifact/0.1</code></td>\n<td>Bounded compiled-artifact representation</td>\n<td>Original observations retain their original profile and validator scope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>i17-reuse-policy/0.1</code></td>\n<td>Bounded time and reuse-policy model</td>\n<td>Interval calculations do not constitute production authorization or distributed atomic serving</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>knowledge-evolution-metadata/0.2-proposal</code></td>\n<td>Earlier evolution metadata proposal</td>\n<td>Publication of architectural requirements does not claim a deployed migration service</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>An implementation claiming this edition MUST state which requirements it satisfies and link corresponding evidence. A change of schema, field semantics or wire version requires its own migration and compatibility record. The publication process MUST NOT rewrite retained raw evidence merely to remove historical development labels.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-provenance-and-rights\">5. Provenance and rights</h2>\n<p>Each study’s <code>evidence/origin.json</code> identifies the origin, exact output hash and transformation status of its evidence files. An exact recovered JSON is distinguished from a readable prose view reconstructed from retained HTML. The latter does not claim to reproduce unavailable original Markdown bytes.</p>\n<p>Third-party books, page images and OCR corpora are not redistributed here. The current arguments are self-contained; publicly runnable examples use original synthetic data or metadata-only graphs. Restricted source access limits independent reproduction of the corresponding historical experiments, and the studies state that limit at the claim it affects.</p>\n<p>Project text remains <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/LICENSE\">CC BY 4.0</a>; code remains <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/LICENSE-CODE\">Apache-2.0</a>. The current <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/SOURCES.md\">source register</a> replaces the earlier catch-all description for the new edition with named primary works and explicit document coverage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-reading-and-citation\">6. Reading and citation</h2>\n<p>Cite a paper by its human-readable title, author, publication edition and immutable revision or released DOI when one actually exists. No DOI is invented for this edition. Use the paper for its argument, the specification for a requirement, and the study or exact evidence record for an observation. The <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a> resolves shared terms without repeating the full definitions in every article.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-internal-review-and-verification\">7. Internal review and verification</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/reviews/publication-integrity/REVIEW.md\">publication review</a> records the actual scientific and evidence reviews, exact input identities, integration corrections, local checks and unperformed release checks. It distinguishes internal content acceptance from external peer review and from the separate repository, website and corpus-admission outcomes.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-what-this-edition-changes","title":"1. What this edition changes"},{"level":2,"id":"2-scientific-crosswalk","title":"2. Scientific crosswalk"},{"level":2,"id":"3-engineering-crosswalk","title":"3. Engineering crosswalk"},{"level":2,"id":"protocol-compatibility","title":"Protocol compatibility"},{"level":2,"id":"5-provenance-and-rights","title":"5. Provenance and rights"},{"level":2,"id":"6-reading-and-citation","title":"6. Reading and citation"},{"level":2,"id":"7-internal-review-and-verification","title":"7. Internal review and verification"}],"title_id":"source-map-earlier-editions-and-protocol-compatibility","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/appendices/source-map/APPENDIX.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/appendices/source-map/APPENDIX.md","source_sha256":"440e3209c8be8d432b5865febdd9719429a6dbc447e8428db9c87af2b404962b","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"api-integration","kind":"appendix","type":"appendix","path":"appendices/api-integration/APPENDIX.md","route":"/en/appendices/api-integration/","title":"API Integration and Publication Consistency","titleRu":"API Integration and Publication Consistency","summary":"Published read interfaces, task-contract delivery requirements and consistency between repository, reader and index.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.0 · <strong>Interface inventory date:</strong> 4 November 2026</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose\">1. Purpose</h2>\n<p>The repository publishes knowledge and specifications. The website renders a selected immutable publication. The service exposes machine interfaces. These are related products with separate versions and evidence. A successful website build does not establish service deployment, a current search index, or admission of a complete task-specific Knowledge Contract.</p>\n<p>This appendix defines the connection between those boundaries. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/\">core specification</a> supplies the obligations for a ready contract; the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/context-package/\">context-package specification</a> supplies composition and delivery requirements.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-documented-public-interface\">2. Documented public interface</h2>\n<p>The public site describes the following read surface:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Interface</th>\n<th>Documented role</th>\n<th>Boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>https://api.contract.place/v1/search</code></td>\n<td>Retrieve candidate passages; used by the site’s anonymous GET search</td>\n<td>Retrieval ranking does not establish correctness, completeness or task admission</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>https://api.contract.place/v1/related</code></td>\n<td>Retrieve related records</td>\n<td>A relation or similarity is not an equivalence or authority grant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>https://api.contract.place/v1/fetch</code></td>\n<td>Fetch an addressed record through the read interface</td>\n<td>The returned revision and representation must be inspected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>https://api.contract.place/mcp</code></td>\n<td>MCP access to the documented service surface</td>\n<td>Availability of a tool does not establish every operation described by the research</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>https://api.contract.place/health</code></td>\n<td>Service health information</td>\n<td>Health is distinct from corpus freshness and scientific validity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The existing site’s <code>llms.txt</code> names the read schema <code>ContractPlaceRead/v1</code>. This appendix does not infer HTTP methods, parameter schemas or authentication behavior for routes other than the observed site search. Clients must use the service’s actual API contract. It also does not invent a <code>/compose</code> endpoint: a task-specific contract delivery capability requires its own implemented and tested interface.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-contract-delivery-requirements\">3. Contract delivery requirements</h2>\n<p>A service offering a ready Knowledge Contract must bind the request to the task and requirements revision, intended recipient and use, applicable constraints, required profiles, temporal mode, and resource limits. Where any of those are required but unknown, it must report the gap or obtain the missing information.</p>\n<p>The response must identify the exact contract and representation versions, selected knowledge revisions, sources and grounds, mandatory dependency closure, coverage, omitted or unresolved obligations, applicable constraints, assessments and the decision’s trust boundary. It must expose a coherent distinction among ready-for-the-declared-use, partial, refused and unresolved outcomes.</p>\n<p>“Ready” requires the mandatory checks for that use, not merely a nonempty JSON object or a successful search. A response that supports reading may still lack the capability or grant needed for execution. A rendered skill description cannot supply those missing facts.</p>\n<p>If the contract is issued for execution, the system must subsequently bind the actual attempt, actor, environment, contract revision, outputs, effects and acceptance evidence. Knowledge delivery and execution acceptance are separate operations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-three-manifests\">4. Three manifests</h2>\n<p>Publication consistency requires three independently inspectable identities:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Manifest</th>\n<th>Required identity</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Repository publication catalogue</td>\n<td>Edition and the canonical document paths/types in one source commit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Website publication manifest</td>\n<td>Exact source repository commit, edition, document hashes, renderer version and generated routes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>API index manifest</td>\n<td>Actual indexed source commits and content hashes, build identity, admitted paths and applicable decisions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The website’s source commit is pinned in <code>publication-manifest.json</code> in the site repository. Its generated <code>publication.json</code> binds reader routes to source hashes. A deployment must build those pages from the declared commit and reject an uncommitted or mismatched source tree.</p>\n<p>The API may contain multiple repositories or releases. The site compares a Knowledge Contract result’s source revision with the reader’s publication commit only within that repository. It must not apply that comparison to an unrelated source. Unknown or different revisions are shown as such. An API-provided label of “current” alone is not proof that the result matches the selected publication.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-observed-mismatch-and-release-gate\">5. Observed mismatch and release gate</h2>\n<p>The pre-restructure site linked the newer paper entries while a search inspection returned a Knowledge Contract snapshot at <code>39e05969728ea7a51f162cdbefac32fd8f54dbb6</code>, whose README still described publication folders as empty. That was a consistency mismatch between the reading surface and search evidence, not proof that the newer documents were absent from GitHub.</p>\n<p>The new reader addresses the presentation boundary by exposing its exact source revision and showing whether a search result’s revision matches it. Refreshing or promoting the backend index is a separate operation. It must use the repository’s existing publication-decision and allowlist process; a catalogue entry is not a substitute for that approval.</p>\n<p>Before a release is described as consistently indexed, the operator must verify: admitted paths and hashes correspond to the intended source revision; indexing uses that exported snapshot; the service reports the actual revision; representative queries retrieve the new documents; removed or superseded entries have the recorded status; and the same result can be fetched by its returned identity. A health check alone cannot pass this gate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-client-presentation\">6. Client presentation</h2>\n<p>The default result view should show the passage, human title, source link and indexed revision. Technical evidence about admission, uncertainty and score semantics can be placed in an accessible details section. Material limits must remain visible where they change a user’s decision. A retrieval score is not a probability that a claim is true.</p>\n<p>Scientific reading should remain possible without making API calls: full pages, equations, references and evidence links are served as the static selected edition. API failure therefore does not remove the published scientific argument.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-acceptance-cases\">7. Acceptance cases</h2>\n<p>The integration must reject a site build from the wrong source commit, a dirty publication tree, a missing referenced document and a local link escaping the publication. Search presentation must distinguish a matching Knowledge Contract revision, an older revision, an unknown revision and a record from another repository. Rendering must preserve equations, tables, footnotes and document anchors.</p>\n<p>A future contract-delivery acceptance suite additionally needs missing constraints, a mandatory unknown profile, incomplete closure, insufficient budget, revoked disclosure authority, stale source evidence, and a correct informational response that does not authorize execution. No successful result is claimed here for an interface that has not undergone that suite.</p>\n<p>For the evidential interpretation of these checks, see <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">evidence and falsification</a>, <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">controlled reuse</a>, and the <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/engineering/studies/README.md\">engineering studies</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose","title":"1. Purpose"},{"level":2,"id":"2-documented-public-interface","title":"2. Documented public interface"},{"level":2,"id":"3-contract-delivery-requirements","title":"3. Contract delivery requirements"},{"level":2,"id":"4-three-manifests","title":"4. Three manifests"},{"level":2,"id":"5-observed-mismatch-and-release-gate","title":"5. Observed mismatch and release gate"},{"level":2,"id":"6-client-presentation","title":"6. Client presentation"},{"level":2,"id":"7-acceptance-cases","title":"7. Acceptance cases"}],"title_id":"api-integration-and-publication-consistency","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/appendices/api-integration/APPENDIX.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e/appendices/api-integration/APPENDIX.md","source_sha256":"74265dc826bf5a1d8abdb390e7e9f1ab11b814d1b7c61e002b7e877a52a7b705","revision":"ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"engineering"}],"articles":[{"abstract":"The available sources are heterogeneous: queries, search results, memory, instructions, tools, Policy, and model assumptions together form the available context, yet they do not automatically determine which task-specific semantic commitment has been selected, how it was issued, what realizes it, or whether a particular action is permitted.\n\nThe central contribution is Meaning Management as an architectural layer governing the lifecycle of task-specific semantic commitments. The selection is fixed by a Resolution Receipt; the Knowledge Contract is separated from issuance; post-issuance Binding is separated from the current Authorization. Variant A, the BKG, the Resolver, the identity model, Expansion/Evolution, and the execution gate formally specify the mechanisms that support this decomposition.\n\nThe surveyed purposive corpus covers prompting, retrieval, memory, orchestration, provenance, workflows, policy evaluation, and knowledge representation individually, but provides no single model of this governed transition; the finding is bounded by the corpus and asserts no historical priority. Practical usefulness is tested by nine hypotheses and nine protocols, HYP/EXP-001–009. The experiments have not been run, so error reduction, improved diagnosis, and acceptable cost are not demonstrated.","description":"A conceptual architecture that separates available context, selected semantic commitments, the immutable Knowledge Contract, runtime binding, and the authorization of a particular action.","image":"architecture-original.jpg","source":"https://arcanada.ai/en/blog/meaning-management-knowledge-contract-architecture","status":"The Status of This Publication\n\nResearch 17 — Russian Semantic Source v1 Frozen. The canonical Russian version of the conceptual architecture and the preregistration specification. Empirical validation of the architecture has not yet been performed.\n\nDOI: 10.5281/zenodo.22013923 — the English preprint is deposited in Zenodo (CERN) under CC BY 4.0. Please cite the DOI.\n\nThis status does not imply a finished implementation, a proven superiority, or production readiness. This English text is a translation of the frozen Russian source through the shared Arcanada glossary, not an independent academic rewrite.","title":"Meaning Management in Autonomous AI Systems: the Knowledge Contract Architecture","id":"knowledge-contract-architecture"},{"abstract":"Systems of cooperating LLM agents exchange state as prose. Prose handoffs lose deontic force, invert relation directions, invite readers to fill gaps with defaults, and leave a knowledge store’s internal wholeness uncheckable. We present a knowledge substrate in which the unit of exchange is a context — a finite set of typed, scoped assertions under a snapshot pin — carried on immutable, digest-pinned, append-only revisions, with every algebraic law of the transfer operations proved or refuted on this carrier rather than assumed. The substrate is implemented and mutation-tested — a refusal catalog of 27 codes at the 2026-08-30 evidence pin, each proven red and growing since only through that same door (§8.10); sixteen invariants enforced at honestly labeled tiers; the reduction ladder R0–R3 with exactness certificates — and exercised on real work: a live store grown from 14 to 134 revisions at the §8.10 recount — organic dogfooding for the first stretch, then a bulk corpus lift (14→63, §8.8) and requirements work this paper does not describe (63→134, §8.10), named as what each stretch was rather than folded under one word (an adversarial review, 2026-09-09, found “dogfooding” over-general for growth this paper’s own §8.8/§8.10 attribute to two other causes) — an admission gate 4-for-4 on real tasks, two write races decided by the append-only law, and two contour defects the run itself surfaced — a truncating renderer and an ungoverned executor over-claim. The work refutes three of this line’s own claims — two facets of the token-economy claim and one store-debt figure, the last felled by the parallel run rather than the evaluation — and narrows the delta claim; the refutations are the headline result. On a stage-snapshot-dominated corpus — session-to-session handoffs, where the pilot’s largest reductions (43.1%, 36.4%) lived, are excluded from the systematic sample and untested here — automated spec-only encoding inflates tokens 7.6% across 18 samples, while a structured-summary baseline under the same instruction reaches 14.8% losslessly on the same sources: reduction tracks the encoder, not the format, on this frame. Hand encoding lands within 0.1 percentage points of the pilot’s 38% on a 4-sample subset where the automated arm reduces only 2.2% — a single-point match against a pilot whose own per-artifact spread is about 12 points, stated as what it is rather than as reproduction. Recall of load-bearing source units — not fidelity in the full sense; this measure counts survival of what the source stated and cannot see a fabricated addition (§1.1’s second failure class), a limit an adversarial review found unstated (2026-09-09) — measured jointly: automated packets preserve 99.3% of 437 semantic units, but the hand encoding behind the 38% preserved only 82.7% — the token win and the completeness rule trade against each other, and token-matched compression across formats remains unmeasured. The delta claim narrows to within-task chains (4/4 counter-pairs); at near-lossless encoding, 144 blind downstream grades hit a 47/48 ceiling in every arm alike — no test with that little variance has the power to distinguish packet from honest prose, and none is claimed to. What the typed carrier exhibits, uncompared (an adversarial review, 2026-09-09, found “demonstrably earns” overstated — §8.7 item 4): machine-checkable recall of load-bearing source units after the fact — an observed MUST→SHOULD drift caught by the evaluation’s unit audit, checkable because modality is a field, and not a fidelity claim in the sense that would also catch a fabricated addition — admission gating, digest-decided concurrency, and projection focus. The co-evolution objection — that the corpus and its enforcing checks were built together — has two measured limits. A sensitivity replay, run in the store’s own append-only order, finds pre-enforcement source content for 10 of the 15 discriminable refusal codes; for the other 5 the objection stands, tested and unrefuted. A separate check-removal experiment finds three codes whose suppression admits a revision that subsequently crashes the unchanged Markdown renderer, with no pre-enforcement source witness for those three. This establishes necessity for that operation on those inputs, not independence of the corpus or failure of downstream LLM work. For the algebraic laws of §4 and the invariants of §5 the objection is untested — none of Theorem 1, Proposition 5, I4 or I5 is among the 26 codes the earlier 86-code sensitivity replay reached (§8.7 item 9); membership in the current replay’s 31 witnessed codes was not re-checked. What the design is not compared against is named to the same standard rather than conceded in a phrase: retrieval-augmented handoff, standard graph serializations under shape validation, and token-matched baselines are unmeasured, each carrying the result that would falsify the design. The method itself is adapted rather than invented — specification mutation and vacuity detection, credited in §2.10, where the analogy to weak-versus-strong mutation is withdrawn as wrong. A measurement instrument fabricated a number twice; only recomputation-from-source in CI held.\n\nWhat this paper is for, stated so it can be quoted against us: it reports what a typed, digest-pinned substrate for inter-agent knowledge transfer exhibits, uncompared — and the measured answer is refusal, not economy.\n\n","description":"The second version of the Knowledge Contract work: the typed substrate implemented and mutation-tested, its algebraic laws proved or refuted on the carrier, and the empirical check version 1 declared missing — with the refutations as the headline result.","image":"knowledge-v2-original.jpg","source":"https://arcanada.ai/en/blog/meaning-management-knowledge-contract-v2","status":"The Status of This Publication\n\nAuthor: Pavel Valentov — Independent Researcher. ORCID: 0009-0001-2567-4584. No co-authors, affiliations, or funding sources are claimed.\n\nResearch 17 — Version 2. English Semantic Source. The second version of the Knowledge Contract work. Version 1 (frozen 2026-08-14) declared: «Empirical validation of the architecture has not yet been performed.» This version carries that validation: the substrate is implemented and mutation-tested, and the evaluation’s headline results are refutations of this line’s own earlier claims.\n\nPredecessor: Version 1 — DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.22013923 (CC BY 4.0). Version 1 remains frozen and citable; where the two versions disagree, the disagreement is stated here and v1 stands unchanged.\n\nCanonical language: unlike v1 (frozen Russian source, derived English), v2’s source is English; a Russian rendering, if made, derives from this text through the shared Arcanada glossary. This v2 text is not yet deposited on Zenodo; versioning the deposited record is the author’s act. This status does not imply proven superiority or production readiness.","title":"Knowledge Contracts, Version 2: A Typed Substrate for Inter-Agent Knowledge Transfer, with Its Algebraic Laws Proved or Refuted","id":"knowledge-contract-v2"}],"aliases":{"research/p00":"science/semantic-objects-and-context","research/p01":"science/semantic-objects-and-context","research/p02":"science/semantic-objects-and-context","research/p03":"science/constrained-transformations","research/p04":"science/retrieval-and-context-composition","research/p05":"science/retrieval-and-context-composition","research/p06":"science/interaction-and-memory","research/p07":"science/evidence-and-falsification","research/p08":"science/interaction-and-memory","research/p09":"science/controlled-knowledge-reuse","research/p10":"science/revision-and-governance","research/p11":"science/evidence-and-falsification","research/p12":"science/controlled-knowledge-reuse","research/p13":"science/knowledge-utility","docs/artifact-registry":"engineering/specifications/artifact-registry","docs/cache-identity-and-invalidation":"engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation","docs/compiled-artifact-contract":"engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract","docs/context-package":"engineering/specifications/context-package","docs/contract-family":"engineering/specifications/contract-family","docs/knowledge-contract-v3":"engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core","docs/knowledge-evolution-contract":"engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution","docs/reuse-policy":"engineering/specifications/reuse-policy","docs/semantic-types":"engineering/specifications/semantic-types","docs/status-model":"engineering/specifications/status-model","wiki/knowledge-contract":"engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core","wiki/provenance":"science/semantic-objects-and-context","wiki/versioned-knowledge":"science/revision-and-governance","docs/model":"engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core"},"source":"https://contract.place/","license":"CC-BY-4.0","author":"Pavel Valentov","retrieved":"2026-11-04","reader_aliases":{"science":"/en/science/","engineering":"/en/engineering/","engineering/specifications":"/en/engineering/specifications/","engineering/studies":"/en/engineering/studies/","reference":"/en/reference/","appendices":"/en/appendices/","search":"/en/search/","science/semantic-objects-and-context":"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/","science/constrained-transformations":"/en/science/constrained-transformations/","science/retrieval-and-context-composition":"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/","science/interaction-and-memory":"/en/science/interaction-and-memory/","science/evidence-and-falsification":"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/","science/revision-and-governance":"/en/science/revision-and-governance/","science/controlled-knowledge-reuse":"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/","science/knowledge-utility":"/en/science/knowledge-utility/","engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core":"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/","engineering/specifications/contract-family":"/en/engineering/specifications/contract-family/","engineering/specifications/semantic-types":"/en/engineering/specifications/semantic-types/","engineering/specifications/context-package":"/en/engineering/specifications/context-package/","engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract":"/en/engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract/","engineering/specifications/artifact-registry":"/en/engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/","engineering/specifications/status-model":"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/","engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation":"/en/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/","engineering/specifications/reuse-policy":"/en/engineering/specifications/reuse-policy/","engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution":"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution/","engineering/specifications/book-package":"/en/engineering/specifications/book-package/","engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance":"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/","engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery":"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/","engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty":"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/","engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision":"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/","engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls":"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/","engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot":"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/","reference/glossary":"/en/reference/glossary/","reference/bibliography":"/en/reference/bibliography/","appendices/source-map":"/en/appendices/source-map/","appendices/api-integration":"/en/appendices/api-integration/"}};
